The Civil Rights Movement has issued a travel alert for Missouri
The National Association for Favouritizing Colored People (NAACP) has published warnings for colored people traveling to Missouri based on discriminatory state principles and racist attacks.
"NAACP's travel recommendation for the state of Missouri, with effect from August 28, 2017, calls on African-American travelers, visitors and residents of Missouri to pay close attention when traveling across the state as a result of a series of contentious racially motivated incidents currently occurring throughout the state," the association's statement read.
NAACP said it took this step on the basis of current Missouri laws, which make it difficult to defend racistly motivated acts in court, as well as as in the conduct of law enforcement agencies that disproportionately target minorities.
“There is a violation of civil rights.
People are stopped by police officers just because of the color of their skin, they are being attacked or killed," NAACP president Rod Chapel told Kansas City Star.
“The largest number of complaints have accumulated so far.”
This is the first warning of its kind issued by the organization for the U.S. state.
Among the incidents cited by the organization were racially motivated insults directed against black students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tory Sanders, a black man from Tennessee.
Sanders died under disputed circumstances earlier this year after he ran out of gasoline while traveling across the state and police plunged him into custody without allegations of committing a crime.
The travel recommendation also draws attention to a recent statement by Missouri's top prosecutor's office, which states that black drivers have a 75% higher chance of being stopped by police.
"The purpose of the travel recommendation is to alert people and warn their families, friends and co-workers about what might happen in Missouri," Chapel said.
“People need to be ready – they should carry money with them for possible bail or alert their relatives that they are about to travel through the state.”
According to the FBI's current numbers, hate crimes were reported in 2015 in Missouri with hundreds of cases of hateful behavior, putting the state in 16th place among all states.
At the same time, a travel alert is a response to Missouri's new law, which makes it difficult for a company to sue for discrimination in providing accommodation or employment.
The U.S. Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) has issued travel recommendations for Texas and Arizona after states passed an Immigration Act that allows state officials to detain people based on violations of that law to improve racial profiling, according to the ACLU.
Travel recommendations are usually issued by the State Department for foreign countries, but more recently advocacy groups have resorted to these measures in response to specific laws and trends within the U.S.
