Chilian parliament lifts ban on abortion, law will assess ÚS
There is still an absolute ban on abortion in Chile, so it must not be carried out for medical reasons.
"I'm sorry that the legislators filed a complaint with the Constitutional Court and that now ten people will decide what we've been arguing about in parliament for more than two years," Socialist Senator Isabel Allende complained.
The Constitutional Court in Chile has ten members and is expected to receive a new president who is close to the opposition, according to AFP, at the end of August.
A bill abolishing an absolute ban on abortion, initiated by current President Michelle Bachelet, was passed by the Senate on July 19.
The right-wing opposition then announced that it would challenge the law in court because, according to it, it violates the right of the unborn child to life.
MPs postponed the vote on the bill last month until it was analyzed by a mixed parliamentary commission.
On Wednesday, MPs approved the bill, after which the Senate did so again.
Under the new legislation, there should be an artificial interruption of pregnancy in three cases: if the life of a woman is at risk, if the fetus has no hope of survival, or if the pregnancy arises from rape.
Similar exceptions have existed since 1931, according to the Spanish newspaper El País in Chile, even before women were given the right to vote there in 1949.
An absolute ban on abortion in 1989 was imposed by right-wing dictator Augusto Pinochet, just months before he left the presidency.
Chile is one of the few countries in the world where there is an absolute ban on abortion.
Nicaragua, the Dominican Republic, El Salvador, Haiti, Malta and Honduras.
In El Salvador, for example, about 20 women are now behind bars, serving up to thirty years in prison for interrupting their pregnancies.
In many cases, according to human rights defenders, it was a spontaneous abortion, which the court described as homicide.
Is there anything else sacred?
The target of Trump's White House now is the Statue of Liberty
An unmistakable sign of regime change is criticism of statues.
The Americans could talk about it as well as everyone else - see Saddam Hussein's carefully staged overthrow of the statue in Baghdad's Firdos Square in 2003.
Stephen Miller, one of the Trump regime's key proponents, certainly knew what was going on when, in a passionate debate with CNN reporter Jim Acosta on the subject of the president's proposals to drastically curtail legal immigrants, he set off with a symbolic ax on the Statue of Liberty.
It's fitting to add that Miller didn't attack the statue itself.
For this he would be lynched by a crowd of owners of cruise ships and imitators of the Statue of Liberty.
However, he attacked its importance, specifically the fact that in 1903 a plaque was placed on the pedestal with the famous poem Emmy Lazarus, up to 17 years after the completion of the monument.
Miller rebuked Acosta, “I don’t want to go down in history here, but the Statue of Liberty is... a symbol of American freedom that sheds light on the world.
A poem that has been placed on the pedestal additionally and to which you refer is not part of it.”
Miller's actually right, but as he's said, this isn't about history.
These are, above all, the startling words of Emmy Lazarus, which resonate from today's time - he invites not only immigrants, but the poorest of the poorest: "May he come to me, whoever decreases, the poor, who is barely able to breathe freely, who with a wretched worm in the midst of full cities."
And it is in modern times that we can watch these “wretched worms” on our screens better than ever.
Miller didn't try to criticize poetry - he wanted to clarify that these people were not welcome in the U.S.
This conflict about the significance of the statue is part of a comprehensive political and cultural war: it's essentially a conflict about the importance of America.
Emma Lazarus, as well as her friends, who after her death tried to plant a plaque with a poem on the statue's pedestal, knew quite well that she was embarking on a high political game.
The statue was supposed to express the union of the French and U.S. republican government by representing a classical theme, that is, the female embodiment of freedom.
Emma Lazarus has altered this meaning: in her poem, the female figure is not abstract, but has his own voice.
It also has a very different name: the mother of exiles.
Merging a poem with a woman's notion is a brilliant feminist move and a devastating attack on American nativism.
And in the same way Emma Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, the followers of the right have long sought to reverse this meaning.
Well-known commentator Rush Limbaugh - Jana the Baptist on Trump's rise to power - argued in 2010 that the Statue of Liberty doesn't actually invite anyone into the country, but represents early neo-constructivism, bringing an American flame to a backward world: "The Statue of Liberty goes forward.
It raises the torch of freedom from the United States and offers it to the rest of the world.
The torch isn’t supposed to illuminate the road to the United States.”
Limbaugh surprisingly encounters an important thing.
The importance of the statue depends on the point of view.
Emma Lazarus describes the statue from the point of view of an exhausted and damned passenger, who often arrives in a new land after a long and scary voyage across the ocean with hope in his heart.
It is literally a view of the world through the eyes of people seeking refuge and a chance for a better life.
From this point of view, the statue expresses hope and listens to their needs.
And that, of course, is a perspective that neither Miller nor Trump will recognize.
Their torch is not to illuminate the path, but to inflame hatred, which is their only source of power.
Athletics World Championships 2017: Neil Black sings praise for Scottish members of the British team
Eilidh Doyle recalls a time when the British team had only two Scottish members
UK athletics Federation director Neil Black said a strong squad of 16 Scots in the World Cup team would be a "huge contribution to the team."
A record number of Scottish athletes have been selected for the 2017 World Championships in London, which begins on Friday.
Black believes that “something is happening in Scotland” and British athletics could easily learn from Scots.
“We’re trying to understand and adapt – we’re going to work on it,” Black said.
“I’ve spoken to people from the Scottish Sports Institute and they think it’s something to do with what they’ve done.
Scotch athletic officials think it has something to do with what they've done.
Athletic: it has something to do with what they've done.
That's what people on the streets think.
Whatever it is, it’s absolutely amazing.”
Several Scottish athletes will seek medals, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart - who will compete with Sir Mo Farah in the 5000-meter race, while Eilidh Doyle has been voted to be the team captain.
“Eilidh is modest.
Her opening speech was amazing," Black said.
“Several athletes, whether Scots or athletes of other nationalities, came out of the room with a sense of immense pride and motivation.
The passion and undisguised feelings that Eilidh spoke to were unrepeatable."
The Commonwealth silver medalist, who will compete in the 400-metre hurdles at her fifth world championships, said it was "unbelievable" that there are 16 Scottish athletes in the British team.
The previous record from 1983 and 2015 is seven.
In the last year alone Muir has set five British and two European records.
He will take part in 1500 m and 5000 m runs at the World Championships.
Another great Scot is Chris O'Hare, who will run the track at 1500 m. This season he has won the British team races and the Announcing Games.
Doyle remembers her first championship, at which Lee McConnell was the only other Scottish team member.
The team captain led that she is “very proud” of the fact that not only a large number of her colleagues got into the team, but in many cases they will also attack participation in the finals and medals.
“Unusual in the position of team captain is that I was elected by the other members of the team. It was a great honour for me, but the knowledge that I was elected by the team members is all the more pleasing,” she added.
"I'm obviously very proud of all the Scots who've made it to the team.
It proves how far I've moved.
They're athletes who will be thinking at the top spots on the podium.
And my introductory speech?
Yes, I think it went well.
I just pointed out the fact that we can be proud at this year's championship, because it takes place on home soil.
It's important to take advantage of the support of home fans and enjoy the fact that we're back to the Olympic stadium.
“I’ve been given a second chance for the race and I’m going to have an incredible atmosphere.”
Blocking critics on Facebook: don't apply for public office if you can't stand criticism from voters
The father of our country knew about his criticism.
Americans loved George Washington, but it didn't take long for newspapers to start criticizing him for anything, starting with domestic politics and ending with his political views.
Yes, this criticism didn't leave him cold.
But he didn't try to silence the critics.
Because in 1783 Washington said: "Freedom of speech can be taken away from us - and stupid and mute will be guided like sheep for slaughter."
That brings me to Maryland is Governor Larry Hogan, who needs to work on behaving like Washington.
Hogan's employees blocked and deleted the posts of at least 450 people, who freely expressed their views on his official Facebook page.
He was sued this week by the American Union for Civil Liberties.
The governor's staff dismissed the lawsuit as unreasonable, and there have been many "who is interested" in comments on the Internet.
"It's just Facebook," many users said.
Still, it matters.
Especially when it comes to people like Hogan.
In an absolutely democratic state, this is a hugely popular Republican governor.
It boasts the second-highest popularity of all 50 governors.
Hogan's not irritating.
As you can see, he governs his state with the certainty of himself and strictly adheres to the principles.
It turned out to be skillful in using Facebook as the main means of connecting with its constituents - the exception is not even the publication of the first photo with a bare's head after undergoing chemotherapy.
Blocking people who come to the governor's site - which is a public forum labeled official and managed by its employees paid with taxpayers' money - is unnecessary and, ultimately, dangerous.
In an interview with the Washington Post, Hogan's spokeswoman, Amelia Chasse, defended the governor, saying the blocking of the comments took place from the position of moderating contributions.
However, using an image of internet trolls, spammers or annoyed people is too easy.
The Washington Post interviewed several real people whose posts have been deleted.
And that's exactly what they are - real people who speak to their chosen leaders; a teacher, an entrepreneur and a pastor, no trolls.
As everyone put it, their comments were polite and thoughtful, not blasphematic.
Pastor in his commentary quoted the Bible, appealing to Hogan's Catholic faith.
Bowie Lakshmī lawyer Sarma Ramani, Md., asked about hate crimes, but her comment was not hateful.
"I politely noted that I am disappointed by his inadequate response to hate crimes and other current news," she wrote in the Washington Post comments section.
“I don’t like the idea that once a certain number of people comment on the same topic, they are identified by some people as promoting so-called collective efforts, instead of being recognized as a larger group of stakeholders.”
What the governor's employees called "focused spam interest," others would probably call "advocacy."
The modern Facebook era allows us to easily manage posts by blocking critics or deleting negative comments.
It is much more convenient than in the past, when the same procedure would require sending employees to the streets, where they would collect and burn all copies with criticism or arrest or silence Protestants.
And that's exactly what's going on, only in digital form.
Hogan is not the last public official to be criticized for defaming Facebook and other social media.
President Trump is facing a lawsuit by Twitter users who have been denied access to his account.
One of the first important regulations was born last week in Virginia.
The Loudoun County Board of Supervisors has violated the Alexandria First Amendment, according to District Judge James C. Cacheris, where it has prevented one of the voters from accessing its Facebook pages.
Governor Matt Bevin of Kentucky has also made a visit from ACLU because of his use of Facebook and Twitter.
That shouldn't be that difficult.
In Washington's days, the low cost of postage had virtually the same impact as the Internet.
Between 1776 and 1800, the number of daily newspapers quadrupled, and many anonymous writers questioned his position as leader.
And even in those days, Washington had to deal with anonymous trolls.
People writing under pseudonyms such as “Juricola,” “Valerius,” “Belisarius,” and “Portius,” wrote letters to newspapers, questioning Washington’s decision.
According to historical documents collected by people as part of an online Papers of George Washington Project, petitions criticizing his position against the Treaty of Amity, Commerce, and Vitaging with Britain were piling up in his office.
However, he did not silence them.
Freedom of expression, disapproval and intimidation lies in the very foundations of our nation.
And the real leader has to accept it.
Czechs quickly used free roaming, they consume more data abroad than at home.
Abroad, Czechs consume more of their tariff than at home.
Most often they move in Austria, but the consumption of data in the summer season is dominated by Croatia.
