Poverty and death in the Indonesian land of gold
When Bardina Degei cooks dinner, she doesn't use the stove.
In fact, he rarely uses a pelvis.
In its wooden hut, located in the capital of Paniai Enarotali in the Indonesian province of Papua, a woman usually simply throws a sweet potato - in the speech of the local "nota" - right into the fire.
After half an hour, he removes the potato and enjoys it with unwashed hands.
Bardina sits on a muddy land - the house is not equipped with furniture - where she performs her daily work at the same time, such as washing clothes in cloudy water from a nearby swamp.
A bucket in a room without a roof replaces the toilet.
As the youngest of her husband's four wives, she was not assigned any field to cultivate.
Polygamy is common here.
Working until late at night represents a danger: most of the men from the village are unemployed and often drink alcohol. In addition, soldiers reside in the village.
"In the afternoon after five o'clock, no one dares wander around the village," Bardina says.
It's a rare look into everyday life in the highlands of Papua, a former Dutch colony that was proclaimed part of Indonesia in 1969 after a controversial referendum, when just 1026 elders were forced to vote in the form of public upheaval of hands before the occupying army.
The current movement protesting against Dutch rules quickly turned its anger against jakarta's government, which controlled the entire region, with the exception of foreign journalists or civilian rights supervisors.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua - independent Papua New Guinea occupied the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is such a distant and ravaged city, where you can reach after a ninety-minute flight from Jayapura to Nabire, and a five-hour stomach clutching the ride.
Public transport in this place does not work.
The city with 19 000 inhabitants consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences, roofs made of wavy sheets in shades of great and rusty color.
Very few Indonesians, let alone journalists, and virtually no foreigners looked at this place.
Before the arrival of the Christians, the locals worshiped a god named Uga Tamee.
And that isn't all.
"We weren't used to wearing this dress," Bardina says, pointing to her colorful, hand-knitted turban, dark T-shirt and bright skirt.
“Previously, we wore only leaves.”
Papua is Indonesia's poorest province - 28% of the population lives below the poverty line, and child mortality and literacy is one of the worst in Asia.
At the same time, however, it is an Indonesian land of gold.
The world's largest and highest-grossing Grasberg gold mine, owned by Freeport McMoran of Phoeenix, is located only 60 miles from Paniai - a rocky province about the size of New Jersey, which is home to about 153,000 inhabitants.
In 2015 alone, Freeport extracted $3.1 billion in gold and copper.
In addition, Papua supports logging at an estimated $78bn.
However, these wealthies are a source of suffering and a guarantee of oppression on the part of the powerful Indonesian army.
An investigation carried out by the New York Times in 2005 shows that Freeport paid about US$20 million to local military officials and troops between 1998 and 2004, including an amount of US$150,000 for one officer.
Papuans calling for self-government threaten this golden goose and are therefore harshly suppressed.
According to human rights activists, more than 500 000 Papuans have been killed since 1969 and thousands more have been raped, tormented and imprisoned by the Indonesian military.
Mass murders in the Papua tribal region during the 70's of the last century amounted to genocide, according to the Asian Human Rights Commission.
Last year alone, Indonesian police arrested more than 3900 peaceful Protestants in the region.
We Will Lose Everything, a 2016 report by the Brisbanese archdiocese, describes the atrocities that occurred last year, as extrajudicial executions, torture - according to another report, particularly popular rape and execution by electric current - and the brutal suppression of peaceful protesters.
"It's difficult to count the death toll because incidents occur every week," said Andreas Harsono, an Indonese researcher at Human Rights Watch.
The situation was aggravated by the fact that Papuan sources attracted colonists to Indonesia.
Of the province's 3.5 million inhabitants, 83% claim Christianity, but demographics change with the influx of Muslim immigrants from the Indonese islands - Java, Borneo, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
Javanese canteens sell fried chicken and gado-gado, or a mixture of vegetables with peanut sauce.
Local people are struggling to survive.
"Immigrants have started selling chickens and vegetables in traditional markets at lower prices than local Papuans," explains twenty-four-year-old Abeth You, who was born in Paniai and moved to work in Jayapura province's capital.
“Original inhabitants of Papua – mama-mama (women) – lost their jobs.”
Indonesian President Joko Widodo, nicknamed Jokowi, as part of his 2014 presidential campaign vowed to address inequalities and rights violations.
The former carpenter has secured and his trip to the presidential palace in jakarta 27 of Papua's 29 districts, including Paniaa.
But very little has changed in Papua, and local people feel betrayed.
"Our hearts have been broken because in 2014 we voted for Jokpwi with the hope of hearing our call for justice," You said.
The reality is that just two months follow in the inauguration of Jakowi, Paniai experienced a major shock.
On December 7, 2014, a group of 11 children sang Christmas songs at a campfire in the town of Enarotali, when two Indonesian soldiers on motorbikes emerged from the gloom of night.
Two of the surprised children told them they should turn on their lights.
One of the soldiers was touched by this rebuke and tone, and later returned with four other soldiers, local parish priest Yavedt Tebai said.
Two of the soldiers in a drunken state pursued a group of children and beat them with the butts of their weapons, the victims and witnesses to the event said.
One of the soldiers then fired at a group of children.
One of the children, 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimo, was beaten so heavily that he fell into a coma.
A few hours later, someone set fire to a nearby electoral commission building, and the following day, things escalated.
About 1,000 young Papuan men and women gathered on a football pitch outside a local police station and army headquarters, demanding justice.
They came up with ceremonial hunting bows and performed a wait's dance - in which they run in a circle and imitate the singing of birds - the Paphuan tribe of Mee Pago.
Several Protestants began throwing stones at buildings.
With escalating tensions, the soldiers were ordered over the radio: "If the crowd tries at least three times for resistance, you have permission to shoot," according to the weekly TIME in an official document, the contents of which have never been published in the local media.
Yeremias Kayame (56), Enarotali's head of Kego Koto, recognized the impending danger and urged residents to diverge into their homes.
However, no one ignored his words.
“When I turned around, someone shot me in the left wrist,” he told TIME on the porch of his colourfully painted wooden house.
Kayame doesn't yet know who the shooter was, but, according to him, the bullet belonged to one of the soldiers present.
“There were a lot of people all around and there was a lot of shooting,” he added.
One of the locals, Alfius Youw, has been hit three times, according to his cousin who witnessed the shooting.
"I ran to my body to make sure it's really him," Yohanes, who, like many Indonesians, uses only one name, told TIME.
“I saw that he was dead...I kissed him.”
Papuan police inspector General Yotje Mende told reporters that his officers were only "providing" the station, which was under attack.
"We have to defend ourselves when people threaten us with deaths," Papuan Police spokesperson, Commissioner Pudjo Sulistiyo, said in 2015.
“It’s a matter of life and death.”
According to Human Rights Watch, five protesters were killed and many got away with injuries.
The news of the killings passed through the yakartic media the following day.
Three weeks later, Jokowi gave a passionate speech in Jayapura and expressed his sympathy for the families of the victims and vowed to deal with violence in Papua.
"I demand an immediate solution to this problem so that it can never happen again," he said.
National Security Minister Wiranto announced in October 2016 that it would introduce an out-of-court mechanism to resolve acts of violence that occurred in the past.
Almost immediately, however, excuses began to be heard.
“Most acts of violence happened a long time ago.
Some of them happened in the 90's of the last century and some at the beginning of the 21st century.
What I'm saying is that we're determined to punish these acts of violence, but it's not going to be an easy task," he said.
In a June 5 interview for the TIME weekly held in Jakarta, however, Wiranto claimed he did not plan to implement any measures in Papua.
Instead, he said that "all cases will be presented to the court."
Wiranto, whom the U.N
Families of shooting victims in Paniai welcomed his claims with disbelief.
"I've been interviewed four times in the last three years, but I haven't seen anything yet," Yohanes said.
"I'm tired."
As he says, several years later he still lives in fear.
“I’m afraid,” he says.
"I'm afraid I'll be arrested by an army or I'll be shot."
His brother Yacobus has confirmed the view of many Paniai residents who are afraid to talk about the incident.
According to him, members of the army beat him up after he helped bury four of the victims.
"After burying my bodies, the army started looking for me," he said.
And the shooting doesn't stop yet.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police shot at villagers in neighboring Deiyai.
A skirmish in which one person died and 17 others were injured took place between the villagers and the construction company's manager, who declined to provide assistance while transporting the man unconscious to the hospital.
Ravianus Douw (24), who drowned while fishing in a nearby river, died on his way to hospital.
Angry villagers protested in front of the company's building.
According to the statement, the villagers threw rocks at the members, and the response to them was warning gunfire.
However, local residents say the mobile brigade (Indonesian paramilitarist police) began shooting into the crowd, killing one person.
“There was panic, we were afraid of retribution,” 29-year-old Dominggu Badii, who lives near the hospital and witnessed the arrival of the injured, told TIME.
“I didn’t leave the house for two days.”
The Deiya parliament demanded the punishment of the officers involved and the withdrawal of the mobile brigade from the area.
Paniai has always been a thorn in the eye for the Indonesian government.
The lack of meaningful development only deepens the separation of the indigenous people of the Mee, Moni, Dani and Damal tribes, who live in the central highlands.
Many of them have joined the Papuai Liberation Movement (OPM) - an army of rebels that protect Papuans' rights by attacking and hijacking Indonesian policies.
Some of the OPM leaders come from Paniai. They include not Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
As a result of the attacks, the military in recent years has arrested and arbitrarily detained thousands of Paniai residents, all under the pretext of "ensuring national sovereignty."
Some of them will never be seen again.
Paniai is known among Papuai residents as a "tragic and forgotten place."
Poverty adds oil to the fire.
The little rice sold in Enarotali is too expensive for the locals.
Bread is equally unattainable.
The locals grow their food: mainly sweet potatoes, fruits and leafy vegetables.
There are women working in the fields; each of them cultivates four or five fields with sweet potatoes.
Most of the harvest is intended for the needs of the family, the rest are sold on the local market.
Ten pieces of sweet potatoes cost only 10,000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Since Nabire Airport opened more air routes and new immigrants began flowing into the country, there has been an increase in economic inequalities between native Papuans and immigrants.
Already so few jobs are usually occupied by more educated and wealthier immigrants.
Papuans rarely have the necessary capital and education in order to engage in business.
"Young people are moving from villages due to a lack of job opportunities and money," said John Gobai, chairman of Paniai tribal council.
Thanks to isolation, the province of Papua is hidden from the world.
Strict rules for foreign journalists also contribute to this.
At the beginning of this year, two French journalists were expelled from papuia - Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp - due to "lack of cooperation with the authorities", all despite the fact that they were given such a rare permit for filming.
Phelim Kine, deputy director of the Asian branch of Human Rights Watch, Jokowi vowed in his election campaign to repeal strict rules for journalists and achieve transparency and development. However, this has never happened.
"Foreign journalists who work in the rebellious easternmost areas of Papua and West Papu, are at great risk: non-recognition of visas and blacklisting," he said in a statement.
The lack of news from foreign journalists means that international pressure on Indonesia in Papua is largely limited.
In March, six pacific peoples - Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands - urged the U.N. Human Rights Council regarding investigations into "various and enlarged acts of violence" in Papua, including gunfire in Paniai.
These countries in the past have also supported the OPM.
Arrmanatha Nasir, a spokesman for Indonesia's foreign ministry, shook off the allegations when he told reporters in Jakarta that: "In Indonesia, a democratic system still applies, and the media is free, so it is difficult to disguise the evidence of cases of human rights violations."
Local people are calling for the participation of foreign governments.
When an official delegation from the Netherlands, headed by human rights envoy Kees Van Baar, visited Jayapurau on May 4, local people proclaimed, according to a source, who also attended the meeting but wanted to remain anonymous, the slogan “we want freedom.”
There are more elections scheduled in Indonesia for 2019, but papuans say they won't vote for Jokowi.
"Jokowi is a man who has good intentions but is surrounded by people associated with gunfighting in Paniaa," said Gobau, chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Jokowi to know that the residents of Paniai - in addition to living in constant fear of a robbery army - are rolling in poverty, miserable education and the health system.
Gobai argues that Paniaa residents, like the other Papuans, regard their voice as the “debt” that Jokowi must repay.
"They don't want money, they're demanding justice," he added.
Despite threats and intimidation to the family of the victims of the panic shooting, they continue their latest symbolic act of defiance: burying the bodies of the victims on the property opposite the police and army station.
Knowing that justice will never be done for enough, at least will not allow those responsible to forget their crimes.
"A member of our family has been killed," Yacobus said with his head down.
"What can we do?"
The French navy intercepted a huge shipment of cocaine to Australia.
The French navy detained a 1.46-tonne cocaine worth about $256 million in the Pacific.
The shipment went to Australia.
During the intervention, a four-member crew of the ship was arrested, apparently made up of people from Lithuania and Latvia.
The yacht, named Afalina, sailed from South America and was towed by police after their detention to a port in Nouméa, a metropolis of France's overseas Territory New Caledonia in the Pacific Ocean.
The Australian intelligence service said it would, in cooperation with its international partners, seek to determine exactly where and where the drugs were directed, and to uncover a "Lithuanian syndicate."
"We are grateful to the French authorities for the measures by which they stopped a large number of drugs going to Australia," its chief executive, Col Blanch, said in a statement.
The drugs were destroyed in Noumée this week.
Australia is an attractive target for their smugglers because of high street drug prices.
The country is also grappling with the problem of a growing number of people suffering from drug addiction.
The current raid is not the first recently.
Last month, three men were charged with transporting large quantities of drugs from the United States to Australia, which they tried to smuggle across the Pacific Ocean in a small plane.
Seniors do not feel thirsty, drinking must be reminded.
It can be a problem in homes for the elderly, according to Pavla Mádlová, a doctor from the Geriatric Clinic of the General University Hospital in Prague Pavla Mádlová, the solution could be an infusion of a saline solution, which is injected with a thin needle under the skin.
So far, however, they are applied only in medical facilities.
The older you get, the amount of water in your body decreases.
"In addition, it loses the feeling of thirst, so seniors generally take in fewer fluids and get dehydrated very quickly," Mádlová said.
They should drink two liters for sure.
"But getting so much fluid into an old man is a big deal," she added.
In homes for the elderly, it is often ignored by no doctor.
In hospitals, dehydration is commonly handled by infusions.
"I think even there it should be gradually introduced so that they can receive subcutaneous infusions," she said.
According to her, up to two pint bottles of saline can be delivered to old people in a day.
Lack of fluids can cause malaise, headache and reduced performance.
Dehydration manifests as a complete loss of self-sufficiency.
"One day, the old man says he can't get out of bed, he talks confused," she added.
In the case of negligence, it can result in a stroke.
The blood thickens and clogs the blood vessel in the brain, there is also a risk of kidney failure.
Lying patients should not be covered with duvets, maximally cotton sheets.
Disposable diapers can also be a problem.
"It's more nursing-intensive, but it'd be ideal to uncover the patient from time to time and have the skin ventilated," the doctor said.
For the second time in Dubai, The Torch skyscraper was on fire
One of the world's largest residential skyscrapers was on fire in Dubai on Friday. Firefighters had to evacuate all residents 337-meters high and 79-storey buildings.
In the last two years, it is the second fire of this high-rise building, which bears the apparent name The Torch.
All residents of the building were evacuated and there were no injuries. The cause of the fire is unknown.
“We were asleep and we were woken up by an alarm. We heared screaming.
It took us ten minutes to run down from the 50th floor," Geroge, one of the skyscraper's residents, told Reuters.
Dubai's civilian defense said firefighters managed to bring the fire under control around 4 a.m. (0:00 p.m
Government officials have added that they are providing substitute accommodation for disabled residents.
The incident could once again provoke a discussion about the safety of materials used for lining high-rise buildings around the world.
From the results of an investigation launched by Torch's skyscraper after the 2015 fire, it emerged that the most damaged claddings and external panels used to decorate or insulate.
British police said the Grenfell Tower London's isolation and cladding system may have contributed to the rapid spread of fires in a June fire in which nearly 80 people were killed.
The results of the survey showed that more than one in five passengers to the U.S
A survey among passengers showed that more than twenty percent of them knowingly or unconsciously uttered banned objects through a checkpoint of the U.S. government's Transportation Security Administration on board the aircraft. At least six percent of passengers boarded an aircraft with a knife or other sharp object.
Fewer than one percent of passengers say they have belatedly discovered that they have travelled with a firearm, ammunition or explosive.
From the results of a survey conducted by Stratos Jet Charters Inc. - and to which more than 1,000 inquirers responded - it emerged that younger passengers were more likely to break the rules.
Of the respondents who admitted that they tried to knowingly travel with an object that is banned by TSA, 19.7 percent were passengers with several,000 flying hours compared to less than 15 percent of Generation X members.
Among the most common items that have escaped TSA's attention are food and alcohol.
More than three percent of respondents consciously uttered a sharp object through security, while 2.2 percent of the women surveyed and 3.7 percent of the men surveyed also admitted that they deliberately uttered banned drugs aboard the plane.
The company said it was conducting research among passengers around the world to find out how many of them accidentally uttered unauthorized items through an airport check and how much it did so on purpose.
Unsurprisingly, most people who have transferred unauthorized objects - whether consciously or unconsciously - travel by plane very often.
As the company pointed out, the TSA directives are somewhat complicated and confusing.
People often don't know what fluids and in what amount they can carry to the plane.
Unsurprisingly, TSA confiscated in the largest amount just banned liquid.
For some time, people thought the TSA would handle books in the same way as smuggled goods.
The transport of fluids is allowed during domestic flights, not international flights.
EPA chief Scott Pruitt on delaying planned directives to curb Obama's administration's emissions
After being sued by the Environmental Protection Agency by fifteen states, its chief Scott Pruitt dismissed his earlier decision to delay the implementation of the former Obama administration's plan to reduce carbon dioxide emissions.
Pruitt introduced the changes as part of his plan, respondinging more briskly than previous governments to the needs of state environmental regulators.
However, he did not mention the legal challenge filed against his previous position in the Federal Court of Appeal.
This relates to the last deadline set for October 1, when states are due to start complying with 2015 regulations to reduce ground-level ozone levels.
Pruitt announced in June that he would postpone the deal by one year and order a more detailed study of the plan to avoid "interferences with local decisions or impending economic growth."
Pruitt, who served as Oklahoma's state attorney-in-chief prior to his role as EPA chief President Donald Trump, has long served as a reliable opponent of stricter environmental regulations.
Ever since he settled in Washington, Pruitt has repeatedly shifted or delayed regulations proposed by the chemical and fossil industries.
Sudden Wednesday's turnaround is the biggest legal failure for Pruitte's regulatory agenda.
Last month, a federal appeals court in Washington ruled that an EPA executor exceeded its authority in an effort to delay the Obama administration's incorporation plan, which required oil and gas companies to reduce methane leaks.
In a statement issued on Wednesday evening, Pruitt proposed its version of ozone standards and strengthened the EPA's commitment to cooperation with states using complex processes and meeting new standards within a given timeframe.
"During the government of the previous EPA administration, it often did not meet deadlines and was waiting to be sued by activist groups and others, agreeing to the settlement and further setting up plans," said Pruitt, who filed more than a dozen lawsuits at the EPA in his previous position.
“We don’t trust in regulation through litigation, and we take the deadline seriously.
We never joke about our status and the authority that results from it.”
However, the EPA states that Pruitt may, at some point, use "the possibility of delay and all other legally available authorities" to ensure that regulation "is based on sound politics and the most accessible information."
Republicans in Congress are pushing for a more extensive rewrite of ozone rules.
The White House's budget, approved last month, seeks to delay the implementation of 2015 rules for at least eight years.
The measures have still not passed the Senate.
New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman, who headed a coalition of states that sued the EPA this week, said the group did not intend to allow legal coercion.
“Turnaround in EPA’s views – followed by our judgmentary – is an important victory for the health and safety of those 6.7 million New Yorkers and more than 115 million Americans directly affected by air pollution,” Schneiderman added.
California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont, Washington and Washington, D.C
Ground ozone arises when common harmful substances emitted by cars, power plants, oil refineries, chemical power plants and other sources respond in the atmosphere with sunlight.
Resulting smog can cause serious respiratory problems in sensitive people and thousands of premature deaths every year.
Advocates of public health and environmentalists acknowledge Pruitte's sudden change with gratitude.
“It’s worrying what coercion we need to exert in order to change the mindset of those responsible and protect the air we’re breathing,” said Lori Ann Burd of the Biological Diversity Center.
“We have to continue to wrestle with an ideological campaign by the Trump administration, which seeks to help polluters and special interests.”
He poured the man with acid, yet is not dangerous, according to the court.
You never know what might happen to you.
At one point you're at home with your family and you're laughing, in the next your whole life will suddenly change from the ground up.
And the worst part is, there's nothing you can do about it.
Andreas Christopheros from the United Kingdom encountered a similar fate, whose face was etched by an acid-unfamiliar attacker, who was eventually acquitted.
This was reported by the Reuters website.
In December 2014, someone knocked on Christopheros' doors.
Andreas said he was going to open.
He thought there was probably a courier at the door who was supposed to deliver Christmas presents for his family that day.
There was a cruel surprise waiting for him instead.
A man he'd never seen in his life poured acid with the words, "It's for you, my friend."
The pain was indescribable.
The clothes fell apart on me like nothing and I just felt an incredible pain on my skin," Andreas said in a statement.
The family quickly transported him to the hospital, but his loved ones learned terrible news - according to doctors, he had no chance to survive.
A few weeks after the incident, doctors were constantly afraid that their patient would succumb to an infection that threatened to penetrate his wounds.
His injuries were very serious.
Ninety percent were struck by a face that doctors were trying to put together using skin from other parts of the patient's body.
A major complication for Christopher was the loss of his eyelids.
It's probably the most tormenting thing I've experienced.
"You can't hide from an unexpected light, you can't close your eyes," he captioned his torment.
Today Christopheros has had twelve surgeries that aren't nearly the last.
Accidents suffered by victims of acidic attacks may require hundreds of surgical procedures and years of treatment, according to plastic surgeons.
Even after a demanding and long-term treatment, victims of tissue are scarred for the rest of their lives.
"Sometimes people don't recover and throughout their lives they fight against deformation and disfigurement," Professor Peter Dziewulski, a spokesman for the British Association of Plastic and Aesthetic Surgeons, said in a statement.
The attacker was wrong.
As for the offense, Christopheros is sure an unknown man must have mistaken him for someone.
The perpetrator believed he was taking revenge on a man who had sexually abused his loved one.
Police properly investigated the case and later tracked down the attacker.
The man was initially sentenced to at least eight years in prison.
However, the verdict changed fundamentally to the amazement of the Christopheros family.
"Three judges eventually concluded that the sentence should be abolished because the man is not considered a threat to the public," Christopheros said in a statement.
Over the past few years, the number of corrosive infestations has increased considerably.
The Ministry of the Interior has commented on the issue in such a way that it plans to establish new instructions to prosecutors regarding the classification of corrosives.
They should be classified as dangerous weapons.
Victims of such attacks, however, do not intend to surrender.
They demand that the UK government enforce harsher and longer prison sentences on people who attack their surroundings with acid or other corrosive substances.
They also call for tightened controls on the sales of dangerous substances, which are often commonly available in different stores, as they are various detergents.
In Dubai's largest city of the United Arab Emirates at night, The Torch's famous skyscraper, which is one of the world's largest residential buildings.
According to the Dubai Civil Defense, residents of the eighty-storey building managed to evacuate in time and no one was injured.
According to a Dubai police spokesman, a fire broke out on the ninth floor and spread upstairs.
However, according to several residents of the house, it was the opposite.
"It started on the 67th floor, that's what they told us," one of the tenants said.
Also, according to another tenant quoted by Reuters, the fire broke out on the top floors, spreading down with falling hot debris.
We were asleep.
We were awakened by an alarm, people were screaming.
"We ran down the stairs, it took us about ten minutes from the 50th floor," tenant George described the events.
Another witnesses told CNN that about 400 apartments had engulfed the flames.
On the ground, a fire destroyed at least two cars when burning parts of the building fell on them.
After about two hours, according to the civil defense, the fire was brought under control.
The cause of its formation is not yet known.
According to witnesses in the surrounding buildings, the power went off.
A large-scale blaze was damaged by the Dubai skyscraper The Torch in February 2015, destroying more than a hundred apartments.
There are about 680 apartments in the whole building.
Even then, nothing happened to anyone, as they managed to evacuate the residents in time.
Built in 2011, The Torch became the world’s tallest residential building with its 352 metres.
He was deprived of the record the following year by another Dubai skyscraper.
The bodybuilder had an incredible 14 doping substances in his body.
He didn't win anyway.
According to Corriere della Serra, there was a varied collection of illicit means in a sample of twenty-three-year-old Ippolita - from steroids to diuretics to drugs for Parkinson's disease or anorexia.
Paradoxically, even this wild mix didn't help him with the title, he finished fourth.
Now the bodybuilder is facing a four-year sentence and prosecution.
The record was 11.
Last year, Italian rugby player Davide Vasta tested positive for 11 doping substances, which was a record number of caught athletes by then.
According to his words, the third-league player started taking steroids the year before when bodybuilding fell through.
Lucka Vondráčková in the Czech Republic.
She hugged passionately with an ex-lover!
Hardly Lucka Vondráčková has entered the territory of the Czech Republic, she is in one round.
It also attracts attention wherever it goes.
The last time she fell into the arms of her former lover Michal Dlouhý, with whom she lived for three years.
The actor was then 12 years older and had already longed for a family.
Lucie was only 19 years old at the time and she certainly didn't want to hang up her career.
So they went apart, but they remained friends to this day.
They also managed to do this at the TV Prima press conference, where they met after a long time.
Coincidentally, the two of them matched in blue.
At first they ignored each other, but then they bonded, and Lucie laughed at Michal's jokes until she grabbed her belly.
Then they hugged each other and posed willingly for the photographers.
However, it was not a coincidence for their meeting, because they will play together a lover couple in the new series Primy Captain Exner.
Islamists have been given life sentences in the UK for preparing an assassination.
On Thursday, a British court sentenced four members of an Islamist cell to life in prison, calling itself the Three Musketeers and, according to prosecutors, was planning a terrorist attack against the police and military.
Four men, two of whom took part in a training camp in Pakistan and had previously been convicted of similar deeds, were arrested last August.
At the time, the authorities thought he was about to attack immediately.
In one of the men's car, security agents found a partially finished pipe bomb, knives with inscriptions meaning "infidels" in Arabic, and a mock-up of the pistol.
This happened when an MI5 Secret Service agent placed a listening device in the car.
"Recent assassinations have shown what horror the defendants could have caused if they had not been stopped," prosecutor Sue Hemming said in a statement.
Three convicts, aged 28, 25 and 33, can apply for release after 20 years.
The fourth convict, who is 38 years old, can do so after 15 years behind bars.
During a trial in London, the men claimed to be innocent and that the weapons found in the car were placed there by an MI5 agent.
The court also questioned him, but then dismissed the defendants' claims.
Thursday was the warmest day of the year.
They measured 38.3 °C in Hodonínsko.
Tuesday's temperature record of 37.3 degrees Celsius remained at the top for only two days.
With 38.3 degrees Celsius, which was recorded in Strážnice in the Hodonín region, it surpassed Thursday, which became the hottest day this year.
This was confirmed by employees of the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute.
Compared to Tuesday, there were not many temperature records on Thursday.
On Tuesday, records were surpassed or leveled at 35 out of 145 weather stations measuring more than 30 years.
On Thursday, only nine long-term measuring stations recorded record temperatures.
For example, in Strážnice, Thursday's temperature was the same as the previous record from 2013.
In Lednice in the Břeclav region, Thursday's 37.4 degrees surpassed the original maximum by four-tenths of a degree, and in Pohořelice in the Brno region the current 37.1-degrees exceeded the record by three-tenths.
Both records, as in Strážnice, came from 2013.
It was ten degrees less in the west.
On Thursday, high temperatures appeared mainly in the south and east of Moravia.
After Strážnice it was the biggest heat in Dyjákovice in the Znojmo region and Brod nad Dyjí in the Břeclav region, where the mercury climbed to 37.8 degrees, but did not break the record.
By contrast, for example, in western and north-western Bohemia, according to ČHMÚ’s website, Thursday’s highest temperatures were only around 22 degrees Celsius.
"Temperatures started to rise there until later in the afternoon," the meteorologists said.
According to the CHMI, the current heat in the Czech Republic will gradually disappear, and more significant rains could occur on Sunday.
However, in most places in Moravia, high temperatures will remain on Friday and in the southeast of Moravia on Saturday.
China lacks tourists.
Beijing is losing billions because of visa policy.
For decades, China has struggled to exert its influence without the use of forces.
It spends tens of billions of dollars annually to support language schools and the construction of universities abroad, pushing its entertainment industry into foreign markets and trying to attract Western tourists.
That's not what Beijing is doing.
The local state media is talking about a plan to make tourism the driving force of economic transformation and modernization.
However, the number of arrivals rose just 3.8 percent last year, and roughly 80 percent of tourists to China came from Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan.
This is due, among other things, to the fact that, despite all its global aspirations, China does not welcome foreigners with open arms, evaluates the situation in the most populous country in the world professor of economics from the University of Beeking Christopher Balding in an article for Bloomberg.
An example that speaks for itself is Chinese visa policy.
Only 13 countries have visa-free travel with China, the citizens of others have to obtain a visa in advance.
At Chinese authorities in the U.S., crowds are therefore comparable to Chinese New Year celebrations.
The visa costs 140 dollars, but if you don't want to wait for long hours in a queue, you have to pay 100 dollars or more for additional services.
Such restrictions create significant economic obstacles.
The Journal of Economics and Financie has shown that the need for a visa reduces the number of visitors to the country by 70 percent.
In 2015, only two million Americans visited mainland China, while 1.8 million Americans visited Hong Kong, which accounts for less than three percent of China’s gross domestic product and less than one percent of the population.
China often exacerbates this problem itself when using tourism to realize its political goals, for example in disputes with Taiwan and South Korea.
When China curtailed traffic to South Korea in the spring as part of a rift around South Korea's anti-missile system, the South Koreans repaid it for it and avoided China.
In May, the number of visits fell 42 percent year-on-year, while Japan recorded an increase of 85 percent.
All of this affects macroeconomic stability.
According to a recently published report by the U.S. central bank, the Fed was able to leave China up to $190bn as a result of Chinese foreign trips, while China receives about £35bn as part of selling the currency to incoming tourists annually.
According to official figures on the development of the balance of payments, China had a deficit of 217 billion dollars in tourism last year, which is equal to 44 percent of China's entire surplus in trade in goods.
Therefore, a small number of tourists, which anxiously hinder the outflow of capital, deal a very painful blow.
However, the above-mentioned factors are only one manifestation of more general restraint towards foreigners.
In many places in China, virtually no one has heard of immigrants.
Less than a million foreigners lived across China in 2013.
There are several cities in the United States.
Even Japan, which is not exactly the crossroads of immigration, has double that.
A number of scientific papers show that immigrants usually revive economic growth, entrepreneurship and innovation.
You won't find them in China.
Solving these problems will be politically challenging, but the step being offered is to simplify the visa system.
Other Asian states, notably India and Vietnam, have been making great efforts lately to simplify and make visa procedures cheaper.
If China wants more foreign tourists and their hard currencies, it should be easier for them to get there.
At the Pilsen zoo, they multiplied deadly poisonous reptiles.
The Kingdom of Poison in Pilsen can look forward to the cubs of three rare, mortally poisonous reptiles.
In the exhibition, visitors will see only all nine newborn Turkish vipers.
This species lives in northern Anatolia, even at an altitude of 2000 meters.
"It also grows in meter lengths," zoo spokesman Martin Vobruba said.
The chicks of the other two species, mortals and cobras of red, will be gaining strength in the breeding facilities of the garden.
We can't tell yet when people will see them.
"We don't want to lose them," nurse Miroslav Dohnal said, adding that it could take a year or two.
Australia's vipers have come into the world by eight, the descendants of a breeding pair native to a population around Sydney.
"An individual that visitors can see in an exposition belongs to mortals from around Perth," Vobruba said, noting that in human care it is necessary for deathbears to winter 6-8 weeks at 13 degrees Celsius.
One hundred percent success was accompanied by a bunch of African spitting cobras red.
The couple in the Pilsen zoo hatched a total of four chicks from four eggs.
"The incubation lasted 65 days," Vobruba added.
This species, in nature, sprays poison on its victims, up to a distance of up to four meters.
It's aimed at the eyes, it can cause permanent blindness.
The chicks of all three species feed on mouse barbers in the zoo.
Within 40 days in the garden they still await hatching chicks from a bunch of mamba black, eggs are stored in an incubator.
Unverified transcript of the phone conversation, Trump is putting pressure on Mexico for paying for a border wall
U.S. newspaper The Washington Post published a transcript of President Trump's phone calls with leaders of Mexico and Australia.
In a January 27 telephone interview with Mexican President Enrique Peña Nieta, Trump urged him to stop publicly proclaiming he wouldn't pay for building the proposed wall.
The U.S. president reproached him for publicly condemning the construction of the wall, reportedly saying: "You can't talk like that in the press."
As part of his campaign, Trump pledged to build a wall along the southern borders, proclaiming that Mexico would pay for it.
A transcription of his first call with Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull has also been leaked to the public, revealing a tense exchange.
President Trump told Turnbull that the U.S
Trump ended the call after explaining to Turnbull that "this is my most unpleasant call of the day."
Tusk testified again at the prosecutory for Smolensk.
"He's got something to worry about," the dead president's brother Jaroslaw Kaczyński said.
"I have nothing to worry about, Kaczyński won't intimidate me," Tusk replied, reproaching the country's current leadership for trying to abuse the judiciary against political rivals.
On his way out of the prosecutor's manual, according to TVN 24, a crowd was waiting for Tuska, who chanted Donald Tusk! and wished the politician One Hundred Years!.
Tusk thanked for his support and assured that he had a clear conscience: "I have nothing to worry about and Kaczyński won't intimidate me."
According to prosecutors, he does not blame prosecutors, but he has no doubt that the judiciary is supposed to serve current Polish rulers as an instrument against opponents, the opposition or people they do not like, and I fear that I belong to this group.
He's been interrogated for the second time
For the second time, the ex-premier was interrogated at the prosecutor's office, this time the interrogation should have concerned misconduct in the autopsies of the victims of the Smolena disaster.
In addition, it came down to the replacement of bodies in coffins.
Families were banned from opening coffins and there must have been some reasons.
And the reason was that they were afraid of the scandal, because it would burden Polish-Russian relations.
"Maintaining good relations at all costs, despite such a terrible event, should have served to make Donald Tusk someone very important in the EU," Kaczyński said on Catholic Trwam television.
He added that a possible conflict with Russia would thwart Tusk's chances of getting the most important seat in the union, from which, however, Poland has absolutely nothing.
Kaczyński also said he had warned German Chancellor Angela Merkel that it might turn out differently with Donald Tusk, but did not acknowledge it.
Tusk underwent an eight-hour interrogation at the prosecutor's office on April 19.
Even then, he didn't testify as a suspect, only as a witness.
However, many regarded his summons for questioning as part of an apparent effort by the Polish government and PiS boss Kaczyński to discredite Tusk as a political rival by linking to various scandals.
The prosecutor's office is subordinate in Poland to the Minister of Justice, who plays the role of attorney general.
The summons for interrogation came soon after the election of Tusk as the head of the European Council, to which Kaczyński tried in vainously but in vain to prevent, among other things, during Merkel's visit to Warsaw.
In the end, only Polish Prime Minister Beata Szydlová protested alone at the EU summit against the extension of Tusk’s mandate.
In this case, Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán also left Warsaw in the lurch.
Burma and Senegal.
Where can Czechs vote?
In the autumn parliamentary elections, registered voters will be able to vote on the composition of the Chamber of Deputies from Burma, Senegal or Luxembourg.
In these and several other countries, since the last parliamentary elections held in 2013, the Czech Republic has opened its embassies.
It flows from the ordinance, which regulates the delivery of ballots abroad.
The State Department has prepared its update, it is currently under comment procedure.
Four years ago, 10 thousand Czech citizens voted for MPs abroad.
"Since the last amendment to the Decree in 2013, there have been some changes that need to be taken into account in these lists," the ministry said.
"In some countries, new embassies have been established, some offices have been closed, or there has been a change in the level of representation," the material reads.
The Decree contains two annexes, the first of which shall include embassieving offices to which paper ballots shall be delivered.
This group of offices includes the representation of the Czech Republic in neighbouring countries, i.e. Germany, Poland, Austria and Slovakia.
More than a hundred Czech authorities will receive a ballot in the print version.
It is their list that is changed by decree.
Four years ago, Czechs could vote, for example, at the Czech embassy in Libya or Zimbabwe.
Today, both embassies are closed.
The Czech Republic has also abolished its representation in Bamako and Kyoto, Japan.
There will be no voting at the Consulate-General in Donetsk.
The Czech Republic closed in 2014 because of the war in eastern Ukraine.
They are now on the embassy list in Nairobi, Kenya, the Senegalese capital Dakar and Zambia's Lusaka.
In Asia since the last parliamentary elections, the Czech Republic has expanded its representation to include the Consulate-General Consulate in Iraqi Irbil and Chengdu, the capital of China's Sichuan province.
Ballots will also be available to registered voters at Czech embassies in Burma and Cambodia.
Czechs abroad had the opportunity to choose MPs at polling stations at embassies or consulates four times so far.
Before each election of the state electoral commission by lot, it will designate a county for whose candidates are voting abroad.
It was for the first time in 2002, when it was decided among South Moravian candidates.
In the next two elections, the Czechs voted abroad among Southeric candidates.
In 2013 the Central Bohemian Region was selected as well as this year.
The number of Czechs voting abroad is steadily increasing.
About 3800 people came to vote at the première 15 years ago, in 2006 there were around 6700, four years later 8222.
There were 10,571 voters attending embassies for early parliamentary elections four years ago.
Moore's Ford Bridge Massacre: Activists Reminded Racist Lynching
The 1948 Ford Mercury drove past onlookers of Monroe, Georgia, corced towards Moore's Ford Bridge.
However, by the time a car with four black passengers and a white driver could reach the bridge, a group of whites with weapons blocked the way.
The group's leader - a middle-aged man in a striped suit - switched long from a cigarette and stared at the windshield of the car.
Inside, the passengers trembled with fear.
"We've gonn't want Roger!" the man yelled.
"Bring it to me!"
The silence interrupts the roar of a young black man being pulled from the passenger seat.
Fortunately, this scene is a mere reconstruction of events that took place on July 22.
But when one of the men throws a noose over "Roger's" neck, the audience sighs with excitement.
Since 2005, Moore's Ford has returned regularly to a group of activists who reconstruct the fateful night of 1946, when two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom and George and Mae Murra Dorsey - were killed by Kukluxklan members.
No one has yet been tried for murder.
“It’s surprising that not a single person has been arrested in all these years – even though we’re still seeing them in our community; even though we know their identities,” said Tyrone Brooks, who helps organize the reconstruction of the event.
“It’s a stain on our history and the burden in our souls.”
Brooks is a 71-year-old former congressman of the state of Georgia and a lifelong civil rights
In his eyes, the reconstruction of the event represents a dramatic call to action. Every year, the Monroe community is reminded that justice has not been done for enough.
"We demand prosecution and closure of the case, we want peace of mind and reconciliation, but justice above all," Brooks said.
“Until then, the process of healing and reconciliation cannot begin.”
On July 27, 1944, a medical examiner covered one of the bodies of lynching victims at Moore's Ford.
According to FBI writings obtained by NBC News, up to 50 men from Monroe and surrounding counties were expected to take part in lynching.
However, no one has yet been prosecuted.
While suspects have remained free for decades, civil rights advocates argue that both reasons for lynching - politics and sex - were known to all residents of the city.
In April 1946, based on the decision of the Supreme Court, residents of the black state of Georgia first acquired the right to participate in the elections, which were scheduled for the summer.
And it was during the summer election, according to the FBI, black farmer Roger Malcom was supposed to stab the owner of Barnett Hester's land during a scuffle - allegedly because of his wife.
In the city, according to Brooks, it was said that Hester communed with Malcom's wife Dorothy and brought her to a different state.
According to the FBI, on July 25, 1946, Loy Harrison, the then-relevant owner of white-skinned land for Malcoma, lodged $600 bail.
He was accompanied to the prison by Dorothy and his cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey.
Returning from the prison, just before entering Moore's Ford Bridge, a group of suspects gardened their path.
"As it turns out, Harrison planned and orchestrated the murders," the FBI said.
“It is also clear that there has been a collusion between state and local law enforcement officers who not only participated in lynching but also disposed of evidence and withheld the identities of witnesses.”
Brooks commented more succinctly on the matter.
“It was a massacre because of the right to vote,” he said.
“Murders had a clear subtext for all black locals: ‘This is how you’ll turn out if you sign up and vote.’
Cassandra Greene has been orchestrating an annual event at Moore's Ford for almost a decade.
Her performance is figurative, eye-catching and aptive. It shows a deep-rooted racism that many would like to consider extinct in modern America.
It's not easy to monitor this reconstruction, and it's even more difficult to avert your eyes, but Greene says it makes sense.
"Nobody wants to talk about lynching, because in doing so, we have to face the unpleasant feelings that are hidden in all of us," Greene said.
“There can’t be a relationship between us if you don’t hear what I had to go through.”
The main rehearsals of this year's reconstruction began in June, and on the very first day, about 30 actors - a dozen whites, the rest of the blacks - flocked to the room where Greenee introduced them to a very carefully planned performance.
Wade Marbaugh serves as an auxiliary director.
For many years he played Kukluxklan's leader, but according to him, the role is exhausting.
"I have a bad feeling about it, because it contradicts who I am," Marbaugh said.
“I think it’s very important to remember this part of history regularly, because we never want to go back to that time.”
Actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona, who play Roger Malcom, or Barnett Hester, are rehearsing their roles.
The show begins with a skirmish - Malcom argues with Hester and blames him for having sex with his wife.
"Get off my property n---e!"
Rascona can't.
There's a scuffle.
At that moment, everyone (at least every black actor) bursts into laughter.
Greene laughs most of all.
"What's what happened?" Rascona asks.
He's confused.
Have I been too rude?
"No," Greene says cheerfully.
"I just don't think white people were saying 'n--r' in such a tone at the time."
"Accurately," one of the black actors blurts.
“You have to put more emphasis on R.”
The energy in the room is suddenly better; hostility replaces a sense of rapprochement.
They all want the performance to be authentic, just to be done for the four victims of the massacre.
The actors repeat the scene.
Rascona this time puts emphasis on the correct utterance of the word on N.
No one's laughing anymore.
The show ends violently and quickly.
Roaring, gunshoots... and silence.
A woman dressed in black stands above the actors' bodies and sings Billie Holiday's mournful song "Strange Fruit."
With every word, there is sadness in the room.
In the background of all this, about a dozen onlookers wipe sweat and tears from their eyes.
There are practically the same number of blacks and whites in the audience.
One couple came all the way from Ithacy, New York, and many onlookers are from nearby.
Each of them endures the local damp climate for almost eight hours, and Brooks leads them in the throad to Malcolm and Dorsey's graves.
Until this moment, murders may have been viewed with distance, but now, standing in the same place where lynching took place, the atmosphere is scary.
With the song's last words, Greene prompts a crowd onlooking to inspect the bodies.
The children knel at each other's heads of the victims, follow false blood and motionless bodies and whisper something in their ear.
"They're not dead," the little boy says.
It'll be a few minutes, and Greene's gonna thank the audience, it's over.
Actors clean the rope and wipe their tears.
Bradshaw embraces Marbaugh, the man in the suit playing the Kukluxklan leader.
Nick Rascona hugs his two actor's friends and cries in their arms; in the end, emotions touch him.
“It’s all right,” one of the women whispers.
"We know you're overwhelmed by the role.
But you had a reason for it.”
Later on the same day, the audience meets with the actors for dinner.
At one table, a young white couple is interested in talking about a performance with a black couple they met today.
Denise Duplinski could barely find words that would express her feeling about today's performance.
“It’s difficult to listen to those nasty words and look at those hideous deeds that people who look like you and do it because they look like you,” she said.
“It makes me realize that lynching is still a topical issue,” she added, “it’s just called police skirmishes.”
Tyrone Brooks is sitting on the other side of the room in the courtroom, enjoying dinner, talking about civil rights.
In his own words, he's already started planning a show for another year.
“The reconstruction of these events is inspiring because it describes the fear we had to experience – but it’s also a moment to think,” he said.
“If we don’t learn from it, who knows... it could happen again.
People who don't know and don't study the history of their country are doomed to repeat it."
I love instructions for use.
One of my friends says that only cowards read the instructions for use.
I don't agree, on the contrary.
It is one of modern forms of art, often pure surrealism.
With nostalgia, I remember times long past, where the instructions for use of anything was rather a rare phenomenon.
For example, my first gramophone contained only one paper, on which were hieroglyphes depicting the wiring of some components, there were some secret marks.
When something went wrong inside me for years, I offered the document to a shaman who wasn't afraid to unscrew the backboard.
He told me with a grin, what the paper with the labyrinth of lines was suitable for.
Yes, it was an accustomed aid in the chronic depletion of toilet paper.
He playfully finded a disconnected wire and allowed me to listen once again to expensive vinyl pancakes.
The new era has brought new tutorials.
Even a relatively simple fruit juicer, where he simply adhered to cut grapefruit, required about ten pictures.
Particularly good was a sketch with a description of how to insert one piece into another.
The kit was a two-piece and a different procedure was simply not possible.
Giving it the other way around required considerable effort with the need to break off certain plastic components.
My contempt of instructions ended at my self-moving Seat Leon.
The year of production 2003 still had such a book with instructions for minor repairs, which included, among other things, the replacement of the blinker bulb.
It's been simple.
A total of three steps and the work should have been completed.
Unfortunately, the author of the manual neglected to state that while the bulb on the right could really only be replaced in three steps.
1. separate the cover.
2. unscrew the cracked light bulb and screw in a new one.
3. slide the cover.
The bulb on the left had a problem in the form of the location of the battery cover.
Initially, I thought that by inserting the right upper limb into the bowels of the car, I was going to catch a spring, a screw, by which the cover was easily removed, and then I would be guided by points 1-3 of the energy-saving author of the service book.
Focal error.
The shelter had no place, no room even for the slim hand of Cinderella, let alone an obese Slezan.
I've lost half an hour of my life, without any success.
The condition was compounded by my wife's entrance to the garage, which was summoned by a cry and words, which he hears from my mouth quite exceptionally.
"What are you doing here?" she asked helplessly.
My answer lasted about 90 seconds, but if I omitted vulgar terms, there were only two publishable words left.
I'm changing a light bulb.
"And why does it take you so long?" she risked with a sentence, which in a long-running volume may be her last.
Slowly I explained the problem to her, and I placed the main emphasis on the torture of the author of the manual, where the burning iron on her hand, which she wrote the instructions, then by shredding her, so that he could never write anything again, was just an introduction to a wide range of instructions of the Witch Hammer, including his compulsory burning on the top above the Losinský chateau.
"How about dismantling that box?" the woman meaned to remove the battery.
In sheer despair, this occurred, and then step 1-3 was as easy and fast as on the right side.
I didn't think anything could surprise me.
And that's until the day when I got a call from a colleague who had just been discussing from a collapsed state after the inspection of the two officials of the State Department for Drug Control.
I know him as a balanced colleague who has experienced the possible and the impossible in the field and who can tell even wild stories with an unexcited voice.
"Imagined I've been fined for not putting up a hand-washing manual," he told me in a weak voice.
Gradually, I got out of it the whole problem, which stuck in the fact that above the sink in the toilet of patients does not have a picture instructions on how to wash your hands.
His arguing to a commission official that adults usually know how to wash their hands and that there was no need for pictograms in order to master the accustomed activity of decent people did not have a healing effect and cost him considerable money.
"It's not just that," she told him.
He understood that the simple rinsing of soapy palms with running water is a primitive act at the level of a cromañon, that man modern, Homo sapiens sapiens, performs this ceremony in ten steps.
It is necessary to state that it is necessary to release water from the tap.
The whole process has a total of eleven points, but at the end there is a joyful realization that a person washed his hands exactly as he is commanded by the Hosudar from SÚKLU.
In the end, I have to point out that I was inspired and I am preparing a detailed plan to operate in the toilet.
On the issue of defecation, the procedure is uniform, there the problem does not arise, in urination, due to certain differences of both sexes, it will be necessary to put up instructions two.
Those pictograms remain a problem, because to create the perfect procedure is also an accurate sketch of all anatomical details, which could provoke a certain outrage among a prudish visitor of the toilet, despite the fact that he can finally learn that throughout his life he wiped the relevant opening through a completely wrong procedure.
Pearson lays off an additional 3,005 employees and cuts dividend in bid to revive trade
Britain's Pearson education group said on Friday it would lay off an additional 3,000 employees and cut an interim dividend by 72 per cent in response to a further restructuring programme to change the business model.
Another wave of redundancies, according to CEO John Fallon, will occur at the end of 2018 and the beginning of 2019, is followed by the company's previous decision to lay off 4,000 employees as part of a previous restructuring.
Pearson announced a plan to cut costs by an additional 300 million pounds (394 million dollars) in May as part of a third cost-reducing program in an effort to resurrect declining trade as a result of the sharp development of digital education.
The company, which last month agreed to sell its stake in Bertelsmann's Penguin Random House, said the outlook for this year remained unchanged after announcing a one-percentage increase in basic sales in the first half of the year to 2.05 billion pounds.
Kabul convoy attack: NATO soldiers killed in suicide blast
A suicide bomber attacked a military convoy in Afghanistan's capital, Kabul. One NATO soldier died in the attack and several others were wounded, official officials said.
Five soldiers and an interpreter were injured, according to NATO.
According to unofficial reports, three civilians also died in an attack in Qarabagh district.
This month, an Afghanistan bombing struck - more than 30 people were killed on Tuesday in an explosion at a Herati mosque.
On Monday, a suicidal attacker killed two people near the Iraqi embassy in Kabul.
NATO said in a statement that Tuesday's explosion occurred after the patrol, along with members of the Afghan army, were attacked by a "local person with a home-made explosive device."
The statement further states that all the wounded soldiers are out of danger to their lives and are located at the U.S. military hospital at Bagram air base.
According to the rapporteurs, the growing power of the Taliban and a group known as the Islamic State (IS) in the Qarabagh region is an important source of concern for NATO allied forces in nearby Bagram.
According to the United Nations, at least 1662 civilians died in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017, of which 20% died in the capital.
At the end of July, a bomb placed in a car killed at least 30 people in Kabul's Shia neighborhood.
The Taliban claimed responsibility for the attack.
On May 31, a major explosion in the city center killed more than 150 people. It is the country's deadliest militant attack since U.S. troops overthrew the Taliban government in 2001.
Violency is undermining the already dubious situation in Afghanistan, and U.S. President Donald Trump is considering increasing the number of U.S. troops who should be assisting the country's military forces and police.
According to reporters, suicidal attacks have shaken schools in recent weeks by the public's confidence in the government, its regulations and, in particular, the reputation of President Ashraf Ghanin.
The EPA, in response to the lawsuit, reconsidered its decision to delay smoking rules
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency called off its decision to delay the Obama administration's law, which ordered states to reduce emissions, just a day after the agency was sued by 15 states.
The EPA announced on Wednesday night that it was presenting a commitment by the agency to cooperate with states.
"We believe in dialogue with our state partners and will listen to their views," EPA Director Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
Pruitt in June announced an effort by the EPA to delay ozone standards - which measure actual air pollution in specific areas of the country and, based on measurements, determine limits that must be met by October 2018.
A group of fifteen out of most democratic states, along with Washington, D.C., filed a lawsuit on Tuesday on the grounds that EPA's actions were illegal.
President Donald Trump's administration is seeking to prevent many environmental regulations proposed by former President Barack Obama - in Trump's words in an attempt to kick up economic growth.
However, efforts by the new government have launched countermeasures by Democrats and Conservative groups worried about the impact of emissions on public health.
On its website, the EPA states that air-contained air breathing “may reduce lung function and increase respiratory symptoms,” such as asthma.
Singer Ladislav Bubnár received a blow and there was blood!
You may have already registered this relatively new singer in June of this year, when this previously almost unknown singer organized a monstrous event on Czech conditions, at which he hosted such leading personalities as master Karel Gott, Lucie Bílá, Simona Krainová and a number of others, and the event was not moderated by anyone other than Leoš Mareš.
The purpose was simple, to show yourself to the public and to present their music video for the song Sara, whose baptism was performed by just appointed personalities.
Few people know, however, that the actual shooting of the video clip, which took place in Russia, did not happen without problems, and the singer Ladislav Bubnár himself had to be treated.
It was his first big shoot with the stars of Russia's national ballet and couldn't do without much embarrassment.
The song Sara is very emotional and is based on the unfortunate love of two lovers.
Because of the drama of the scene, the singer's rival was supposed to indicate a slap stroke in the video clip.
At one point, however, he hit him so hard and straight into his nose that the young singer had to be treated by doctors.
"It was a blow, I didn't expect it, blood was immediately lowered from my nose," Ladislav commented.
Colleagues and producers decided that it was an unfortunate accident and Ladislav did not react in time.
Luckily, the injury wasn't serious enough to affect the sequel to the next shoot.
We now bring you the above-mentioned video clip of a young artist, you can also record the scene with a stroke.
Unsuccessful version with blood, however, it was not used.
Mikey Garcia and Vasyl Lomachenko spoke at a meeting about the possibilities of mutual duel
Vasyl Lomachenko and Mikey Garcia, the two boxers who could stage the most thrilling light-weight boxing match, met for the first time at Tuesday's meeting.
Each of them was in a separate room in ESPN offices in Los Angeles, until Garcia - an undefeated World Boxing Council lightweight champion - didn't come down to the lobby, where he welcomed Lomachenko, the World Boxing Organization's featherweight champion.
After Garcia's victory based on a unanimous decision against former four-time world champion Adrien Broner in Saturday's match at Showtime, the two boxers made the list of the top five light-weight boxers.
Garcia stretched out his right-hander and said, "How's it going, champion?
Good luck.”
Lomachenko defends his title against Miguel Marriaga on ESPN on Saturday at 19.
Boxers pose for photographers and Lomachenko manager Egis Klimas adds that a potential boxer fight would be a "perfect rehearsal."
"I'm a former featherweight champion," Garcia adds.
“Because of the match with Broner (67 kg), I had to gain weight, but I’m not that big.
I'll be small for my 63 pounds
I'd like to go back to the light weight category.
Lomachenko has to gain weight again in order to get slightly... then we can compete.
That's what fans want.
Why not meet them.”
Lomachenko agrees.
"Boxing would be good for this game."
Klimas made the demand for payment for a view - which is part of his strategy. Lomachenko is expected to make his lightweight debut at the end of this year, and next year to compete with Garcia (37-0, 30 knockouts), who also expressed an interest in the match against duel winner Jorge Linares vs. Luke Campbell, who will compete on September 23 for the light-weight title in the Forum Arena.
"The match should take place in the style of payment for viewing, that's what the fans demand," Garcia said, who later commented that it was open to the possibility of broadcasting the match through a television station that would come with the best offer.
“We are the main attraction.
No one else draws such attention.
We’ll wait for the right opportunity...”
Garcia said he recognizes Lomachenko as an "amazing boxer" and could take part in his Saturday fight.
Lomachenko, however, did not lure Garcia with praise when he said that the champion of three divisions from Riverside looks “very slow... I’m sorry Mike, but you’re asking for a knockout.”
Garcia said his goal was to show strength and only then boxing.
Both boxers said they felt rising tensions ahead of each other's duel.
“You won’t forget this game.
No other struggle attracts so much attention.
And I'll be very pleased to put it in the fire," Garcia said.
Lomachenko said: "I've been waiting for the best boxers and this is Mikey."
It will be a great match.”
The young couple disappeared without a trace 12 years ago.
Their car has now been found at the bottom of the Volga.
A pair of young Russians Ilya Zhirnov and Kira Cherkasova have been missing since December 2005.
There was an extensive search for them, but without success.
And the police eventually ended the investigation for lack of evidence.
Families of the lost couple lived in uncertainty that they wouldn't know what had happened to the couple.
After 12 years came the sad ending of the whole story.
The wreckage of their car was found by a lay diver at the bottom of the Volga River in Cheboksary in Chuvachsk.
Only thanks to documents left in the car, the officers managed to identify the couple.
The documents survived for a decade underwater, without which it would take police much longer to find out the identities of those found.
The couple was last seen when Zhirnov borrowed a car from his father so he could pick up his girlfriend from work.
Meanwhile, the diver who discovered the car remains anonymous.
He reported his finding to Cheboksar police, which pulled the car to the beach the following day.
Investigators believe the young couple's death was an accident caused by a frozen road leading along the river.
However, the investigation is ongoing, further details will be released after its completion.
The latest test results to set the age limit for asylum applications in Sweden
The latest results of tests by asylum seekers in Sweden, who have been subjected to a new method judging their age, show that over 80 percent of people were surveyed as over the age of 18, but not everyone believes their accuracy.
The Swedish Forensic Medical Agency (RÃ¤tmedicinalverket) began conducting new tests earlier this year.
The aim of the method is a more accurate assessment of applicants in the evaluation of asylum applications. The measure was approached after the Swedish Migration Agency (Migrationsverket) was criticized for failure in determining the correct age of certain refugees who lied about their age.
To date, the Swedish Migration Agency handed 6880 cases for investigation, and the Forensic Medical Agency has now published the results of a total of 2481 tests, carried out from mid-March to July 31, 2017.
80 percent (2002) were assessed as over the age of 18, while in another 25 cases the Forensic Medical Agency concluded that the inquiry was "possibly over the age of 18."
In another 423 cases it was decided that the interviewee was "perhaps younger than 18."
Tests are only approached if the age of the interviewee is questionable.
According to Johan GÃ¶ransson of the Forensic Medical Agency, the results correspond to the previous month.
“Compared to last month, statistics are stuck in tracks, and the numbers are very similar.”
A higher number of applicants whose age has been estimated to be over the age of 18 does not necessarily mean that asylum seekers are lyinging about their age.
In May, the agency released the results of 581 tests and said that in 442 cases it was found that "the enquired person is 18 years or older."
Newspaper Svenska Dagbladet investigated 581 published cases and found that in 243 cases, the respondent stated that he would be 18 years old this year.
Criticism has also been criticized by the method of determining age, which includes an X-ray of wisdom teeth and MRI scans of the knee joints, which are subsequently assessed by dentists and X-rangeologists.
One of the skeptics is Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute, who, among other things, is engaged in assessing the biological maturity of people to determine the age when the person went through puberty.
“I’ve had reason to think about this issue, and I don’t think these types of measurements to assess age are correct.
The assessment of biological age is, in a sense, very uncertain.
“If we can’t determine age with precision, we shouldn’t pretend otherwise,” he told Metro.
Forensic medical agency Agency's GÃ¶ransson defended the decision by saying: "In general, it can be said that the risk of misjudging a child as an adult is small.
The highest risk is the fact that the person interviewed is just below the 18-year limit, which in this case corresponds to 10 percent of all those questioned.”
Trump threatened the president of Mexico on the phone.
U.S
Trump said in an interview that the funding would somehow be arranged, although he admitted it would have to be secured from other sources.
Trump has similarly spoken openly with Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbulle.
That's according to transcriptions of interviews published Thursday by The Washington Post.
Trump made the construction of a wall on the southern border for Mexican money with one of the main vows in his pre-election campaign last year.
In his first interview with the Mexican president shortly after taking office, Trump undiplomatically rebuked Peñu Nieta for putting himself critical of his plan to build a wall.
He portrayed the southern neighbor as a growing political issue and pressured the Mexican president not to publicly say that his government would never pay for the wall.
"You can't say this to the press," Trump said repeatedly in a January 27 interview, threatening cutting contacts with Mexico's president if he continues to issue defiant statements.
The funding will come out somehow, and in the end, it will turn out well.
"But if you say that Mexico won't pay for the wall, then I don't want to see you gentlemen anymore, because that's unacceptable to me," Trump said.
He described the wall as the least significant thing we are talking about, but politically it is perhaps the most important.
An executive order to build a wall was issued by Trump shortly after taking power.
Mexico refuses the construction and financing of a 3200-kilometer wall, because of which relations between the two countries escalated.
At the end of March, Trump said the U.S
In the end, Trump met with his Mexican counterpart for the first time at the July G20 summit in Hamburg.
The Washington Post also reported on Trump's phone conversation with Australian Prime Minister Turnbull on January 28.
The head of the White House called the conversation ridiculous.
Such a conclusion prompted an exchange of views on an agreement under which Australia grants asylum to Central American refugees in exchange that the United States will accept persons from internment facilities in Pacific island states.
At first, Trump considered the deal stupid, but later acceded to it.
"Certainly you can say that this is not a deal that you created, but you're going to have to stick to it," Turnbull told Trump.
In some passages, the telephone conversation with Turnbull was almost jovial, for example, when Trump praised Australia's refusal to accept refugees arriving in boats.
"You're worse than me," Trump joked.
Suddenly, however, the tone of the conversation escalated.
"I hate accepting these people," Trump noted.
I assure you're bad.
That's why they're in prison now.
"They're not going to be great people who will go to work for local milkmen," he said.
In an interview, he also noted that the fugitive could become a Boston bomber in five years.
At the end of January, about a week after coming to the White House, Trump conducted telephone conversations with a number of world statesmen, often several in one day.
In the end, the conversation with Turnbull was described as the least enjoyable for the whole day.
Before ending it, he highlighted that he had previously had a pleasant conversation with Russian President Vladimir Putin.
In front of the crowd, they executed Rana's killer.
Her father was watching, too.
The execution took place in the square in Yemen's capital Sanaa.
Muhammad al-Maghrabi chose the little Rana Almatari, who lived in his neighborhood.
Al Maghrabi kidnapped the girl, took her father to the house, where he raped her and strangled her with his bare hands.
He then raped her body, which he then dumped near the mosque.
The violent death of a little girl has affected an entire country facing the threat of civil war.
Yahya Almatari stood in the front row of a thousand-headed crowd, which came together to witness the execution.
Muhammad al Maghrabi was sentenced under Sharia law.
The victim's father communicated his feelings to the media after the execution.
I feel reborn.
I'm relieved.
It's a new day of my life
Other members of the family were also present, together with the crowd shouting the slogan "long live justice."
The rapist in the square was brought by a police van, from which he was dragged.
The officers put him tied on his belly.
One of the members turned his weapon against him and opened fire from point-blank range.
The mob present was celebrating every blow that had been fired.
Pan al Almatari told the MailOnline website that justice has been done in accordance with the country's legal system.
"Justice, Alah and Sharia law have prevailed," he added.
I was standing in Sana Liberation Square, waiting for my daughter's killer to be executed.
Then my family and I went to the morgue.
We took Rana's tiny body and buried it in a nearby cemetery.
Me and my wife Jamileh are grateful that justice has been done.
The police feared that a crowd gathered in the square would fall into hateful madness, grabbing and lynching the killer.
The execution was ordered by the court, which is under the administration of the Shiite Houthis.
Initially, however, Maghrabi was sentenced immediately after his arrest to 100 lashes, but his act provoked such outrage that the judge decided on the death penalty.
Al Maghrabi was arrested two days after the girl's disappearance on June 27.
Rana's family knewed him well.
When police searched the cellar of his house, they discovered evidence that the man was planning his act.
Mr. Almatari described it as a monster.
What could have been his motive?
Even an animal would have been more merciful to Rana.
"Our family doesn't have enemies, and those wounds that borrowed Rana's body were horrific," he told the media.
"Rana disappeared in the afternoon, that day we reported it to the police, we told the newspaper and to all our television stations, the posters with her photo hung everywhere," he continued.
They all believe that the police got a tip from the killer's parents, who suspected their son of the gruesome act.
The girl's mother, Jamileh, had to be hospitalized after she learned that her daughter's body had been found.
Rana used to go playing outside with the other kids in the neighborhood.
After 10-15 minutes, I went out to see where she was.
We searched everywhere, but Rana was gone.
We walked past our block, our friends took cars and drove around, but searched in vain.
The people of Yemen were disgusted by the crime and began to gather in front of the police station where al Maghrabi was held, demanding immediate punishment.
A representative of the Ministry of Justice has read to the crowd present footage of the investigation to reassure them.
Apparently, the accused did not show regret over his act.
Just before serving his sentence, officers read his sentence, but the convict only glanced at his executioner.
The entire event was filmed for national television, but there have been many recordings on the Internet made by the crowd.
West Virginia's governor has announced a change to the political party
West Virginia Democratic Governor Jim Justice announced Thursday night at President Trump's rally that he was moving to another party.
"Whether you like it or not, Democrats have turned their backs on me," Governor Justice said.
“Today, after a long prayer and deliberation, I announce to you... today, as a resident of West Virginia, I tell you that I can no longer support you from the position of a democratic governor.”
A crowd of onlookers exploded into joy.
"Tomorrow I'll sign up with the Republicans," Justice added.
“As a coach, I can tell you it’s time to jump into a different game.”
Trump has previously vowed an important announcement to the people at a rally in West Virginia.
Trump won in West Virginia with 42 points and Justice did not support Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton.
Justice, who according to Forbes magazine is the only billionaire in the entire country, was elected in 2016.
Forbes adds that Justice inherited a large fortune from his father and is the owner of several coal mines.
The value of his assets is estimated at $1.59 billion.
The Republican National Committee, meanwhile, issued a statemen on Thursday night saying: "The defection of the Governor of Justice is another blow to the Democratic Party, which puts obstacles in our way, rather that it is about uplifting our country."
"For the Democratic Party, which has mere 15 governors and controls the fewest legislative bodies in history, an announcement by the Justice governor is the last straw for an already vicious party without a leader who seeks in vain to find positive and unifying reports," RNC President Ronna McDaniel said in a statement to CBS News.
Sebastian Coe praises Russia's "sincere" apology for doping scandal
Former outstanding athlete Sebastian Coe praised Russia on Thursday for sincere apologies for the doping scandal, but reiterated that it has not yet been the right time for Russia to return to the sports world.
The head of Russian athletics, Dmitry Shylakhtin, told the IAAF on the evening of the World Athletic Championships - that the expulsion of his country from the world of sport was correct and that he was determined to fight doping.
He apologised to "all athletes who lost gold and silver medals in this way."
Despite his comments, members of the IAAF Congress voted to maintain the ban, which came into effect in November 2015 after the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) report showed that Russia supported the doping of its athletes.
"I think today we have witnessed a very sincere response and presentation," IAAF president Sebastian Coe told reporters.
“All members of the council and Congress were pleasantly surprised that Russia acknowledged its mistake and is working with all its strength to correct and restore the proper function of the entire federation.”
Coe said he was also "pleased" that Russia had accepted the criteria for its reintroduction.
"I think today has been very successful and we have progressed to the next level, but the Congress supports the recommendation of the crisis staff to maintain the punishment that has been cast on Russia," Coe added.
“In the next few months, we need to do everything we can to stabilize the situation.
Our effort has always been to separate honest athletes from dishonest athletes," he concluded.
At the World Athletics Championships, which begins on Friday in London, 19 Russian athletes will compete under a neutral flaw who have complied with strict IAAF rules.
IAAF crisis chief Rune Andersen said on Monday that Russia still had to meet several criteria before being admitted back.
Tests for the presence of drugs are still unsatisfactory and excluded coaches continue to carry out their activities, he told reporters in London after the IAAF board's presentation.
The CzechTrade government contract was awarded to František Savov, whose state is prosecuting.
The public contract of the state pro-export agency CzechTrade was awarded to the Mladá fronta František Savova publishing house.
Officials awarded him one hundred percent points in all qualitative criteria.
They did not mind that the Czech state prosecutes Savov for tax fraud and asks for his extradition to the Czech Republic.
In April, the pro-export agency of the Ministry of Industry and Trade CzechTrade announced a public contract for the four-year provision of editorial content for the BusinessInfo portal.
The contract in the total value of 30.8 million crowns was awarded to the publishing house Mladá fronta entrepreneur František Savov.
Czech police have already charged him with tax evasion in 2014 and prosecuting him on the run.
Savov has fled to the UK and the Czech Republic is requesting his extradition.
News server Aktuálně.cz pointed out the case.
According to the original criteria of CzechTrade, the award of the contract should have been decided 60 percent by the price offered and of the remaining 40 beauty contest, i.e. the quality of the project.
It was Savov’s company who was greatly helped by the second criterion.
According to Dotyk.cz, it received one hundred points in all partial quality criteria.
As a result, it moved it before the next candidate, Vltava Labe Media VLM, which offered a better price.
While Mladá fronta operated with an amount of 404 900 crowns a month, VLM offered 324 350 crowns.
In contrast to Mladá fronta, however, the assessors received a below-average rating in all quality criteria, a total of 62 points.
The tender was also attended by the consulting firm BDO Advisory and the digital agency R-Media, which, as well as server Aktuálně.cz under the publishing house Economia.
VLM has filed several objections against the outcome of the tender, stating that it considers its evaluation to be unfair and insufficiently justified.
After their rejection, it decided to contest the competition with the Office for the Protection of Competition of the Office for the Protection of Competition.
He believes that CzechTrade acted in violation of the law.
"The petitioner is convinced that the contracting authority has reached a decision on the selection of a public contract supplier on the basis of a purely subjective evaluation, unambiguously favoring a participant Mladá fronta, a.s., at the expense of the petitioner and other contractors," reads the proposal to initiate the proceedings filed at the Office this week, which was published by news server Aktuálně.
According to Michal Klíma, CEO and Chairman of the Board of Directors of VLM, the scoring of the selection committee appears to be unreliable and one hundred percent assessment of the Young Front project for all its members as extremely suspicious.
"Every independent observer must consider such miraculous achievement by one of the competitors to be unlikely and suspicious," Klíma told report.
CzechTrade has not yet commented on the situation.
CzechTrade does not fundamentally comment on public contracts prior to their conclusion.
All information about the contract mentioned by you is published in accordance with the law in the profile of the contracting authority and in the Public Procurement Bulletin.
"After the completion of the contract, a full written report will be published, but during the duration of the contract, we cannot comment on its course," Štěpán Dlouhý, the agency's media representative, told Dotyk.cz.
Mladá fronta reacted with a statement, according to which she considers it extremely problematic that Economia has intervened with an article in Aktuálně.cz in the competition, where she has her own business interests.
However, this fact was mentioned in that article.
It may have helped her that she provided editorial services to the BusinessInfo portal in the previous four years.
The contract was first awarded in 2013, when the Ministry of Industry and Trade was led by Martim Kuba ODS.
František Savov accuses the police of tax evasion in connection with excessive VAT deductions of his companies, causing alleged damage to the Czech state of up to 770 million crowns.
The Czech Republic has been asking Great Britain for its extradition since 2014.
According to Savov, the head of the ANO movement, Andrej Babiš, under whose influence the Mafra publishing house falls, is responsible for his criminalization.
A London court rejected Savov's extradition last year, but the businessman appealed.
"From our point of view, the case has not moved anywhere since last year, we have not received any notification from the United Kingdom to this day," Štěpánka Zenklová, a spokeswoman for the Prague Public Prosecutor's Office, told Aktuálně.cz.
In addition to the ownership of Mladá fronta, Savov was located at Harvestor Limited, based in Cyprus, which controlled the Samson Brewery in České Budějovice, and in the Cypriot company Maraflex, which profiles itself as an investment fund that buys at-risk assets.
It should also control the failed company Heavy Machinery Services, formerly Legios, which owes more than three billion crowns.
In the past, Savov was also associated with the removal of property from the former Socialist Youth Union.
The motorcyclist drove along the sidewalks and through the park, ended up in tumblers.
The motorcyclist drew attention to himself by ruthless driving on the streets, sidewalks and park paths in Úšovice.
When the patrol began chasing him, he decided to drive her away in the direction of Habitat.
"Officers chased the man with increased caution through the city when he did not manage to drive on one of the side roads behind Mariánské Lázně and with a motorcycle drove into a field where he crashed," police spokesman Daniel Dvořák said.
In the end, a rescue helicopter had to arrive for the accident, which transported the injured motorcyclist to the Pilsen hospital.
Subsequently, officers discovered that the motorcyclist did not own a driver's license, the license plates on the motorcycle were stolen, his serial number polished, and the production label itself removed.
It's possible the machine was stolen.
The origin of the motorcycle is under further investigation.
"By a breath test, the presence of alcohol in the driver was eliminated at the scene, but a positive result for the substance methamphetamine was detected by the test," the police spokesperson added.
How football's financial rules will affect Neymar's move to Paris
The rules limiting the funding of European football clubs will not disrupt Neymar's record-breaking transfer from Spain's Barcelona to Paris-Saint Germain, France.
Any penalty for Paris Saint-Germain will be carried out by EUFA, which oversees European football teams and has the power to ban teams from participating in the prestigious Champions League.
The Financial Fair Play (FFP) rules introduced by UEFA in 2011 stipulate that PSG will have to prove that Neymar's transfer was not funded without major losses.
Barcelona paid 222 million euros just to redeem Neymar from his contract and Neymar will demand a salary of several tens of millions of dollars a year.
This is followed by several financial conditions for Neymar's transfer to France.
Since the PSG team is funded by a wealthy Qatari family that has become rich on energy sales, it can afford to bring Neymar into an already stellar football family.
Clubs can spend huge sums on the purchase of players, but on the other hand, they have to prove statutory sources of income, which allows them to cover the overhead costs of business activities related to football.
The FFP introduced Michel Platini after being placed in the seat of UEFA director in 2007.
The former French player believed that clubs, which in pursuit of new reinforcements are ultimately cheating and risking bankruptcy if banks or owners refuse to secure them.
In 2011, FFP rules redirected their attention from the club's debts to monitoring their losses.
This change meant that the business model used by Roman Abramovich with Chelsea in 2003 - to buy a club with potential and spent horrific sums on players to build a global brand - was suddenly not as profitable.
As part of the current three-year trial period, which expires in 2018, clubs can report a loss of up to 30 million euro.
And despite Platini having to leave his post in 2015, his successor Aleksander Cheferin is determined to advocate a ban on excessive spending.
"FFP rules still apply, and we are determined to strengthen them in the future," Andrea Traverso, who oversees compliance with FFP rules, told the Associated Press last week.
Although PSG has reimbursed Neymar’s redemption amount in a one-time payment, it may, for the purposes of accounting, spread the amount for the entire duration of the contract.
Neymar signed a five-year contract, so the transfer can be broken down to 44.4 million euros annually for five years.
The first wave of FFP's top sanctions hit PSG in 2014 along with Manchester City, owned by Abu Dhabi.
PSG took a fine of EUR 60 million (US$82 million) - which was later reduced to 20 million euros - and an order to reduce the Champions League team to 21 players in the 2014-2015 season, instead of 25 players.
UEFA is looking out for teams that can try to circumvent financial regulators by increasing their revenues through companies linked to club owners.
Thus, even if the Qatari company sponsors PSG once funding exceeds the value of the standard market rate, UEFA has the right to prohibit billing the amount.
UEFA agreed to a similar measure in 2014 after deciding that the amount of funding provided by the Qatar Tourism Authority of the PSG team was too high.
Part of Neymar's income at PSG, according to some reports, could be paid from the 2022 World Cup organizing committee in Qatar.
PSG and the 2022 World Cup officials in Qatar, however, denied the fat that Neymat should become an ambassador of the World Cup in football and that such a manoeuvre would be easily uncovered by UEFA officials.
The previous sanctions imposed on PSG mean that UEFA will closely monitor any attempt to circumvent the rules,
According to Tuesday's expressed insiders, UEFA did not launch any investigation into Neymar's transfer to PSG.
A source wished to remain anonymous as he is unable to comment on the situation with PSG, according to The Associated Press.
The last published financial statements show that PSG closed 2015-2016 with a net profit of 10 million euros and a turnover of 542.4 million euro.
Financial statements for the current financial year have not yet been published.
PSG expect Neymar's arrival to be a huge opportunity for sponsors, merchandising and jersey sales.
The club promises to increase its turnover by up to 30 percent from signing the contract.
Representatives of the Spanish league, obviously concerned about the departure of one of the biggest stars, sought to thwart Neymar's departure, arguing that PSG would violate FFP rules.
“It’s a clear case of ‘financial doping’ of both the club and the state,” La Ligy president Javier Teba told the Spanish newspaper AS, referring to the ownership of the PSG club by a Qatari syndicate.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger, whose club is owned by an American sports tycoon, shares La Liga's same stance on club funding.
"This is the result of ownership, which has fundamentally changed the football world in the last 15 years," Wenger said on Thursday.
“Once the owner of the club becomes, anything is possible.
Complying with the rules of financial fair play is very difficult, because the state can have different interests in the participation of such players in its club.”
Spartan midfielder Rosicky: The changes are painful, but we have to believe.
Sixteen thousand applauding fans on their feet.
Tomas Rosicky goes to the set.
The same scene as in Sunday's game against Bohemians.
The Spartan midfielder moved the game against Crvené Zvezda, devising a number of goals, but his teammates did not go into the black.
The Letna team, pumped with new multi-millione reinforcements, thus ends after the opening two-match in the European Cups.
Was it difficult to do anything else with the match after the goal of Belgrade?
After the goal, it was necessary to score four, which was difficult for us.
At least we wanted not to lose, raise our self-confidence, so that we have something to bounce back to the next matches.
We've had chances.
We have to work together as a team and shift our game.
Why didn't Sparta be able to get into the match for so long?
The game's terribly worked.
Combinations of the opponent were much better than ours.
We didn't get to anything, at half-time we fired maybe once.
That's, of course, terribly few when you have to win by two or three goals.
The second half was already much better, in the final third we managed to play with them.
But you have to push your opponents into positions like this more often.
Is the problem a large number of changes in the line-up?
Of course, it's one of the pains when a new team is formed.
We've only played three competitive games and the line-up is always changing.
It's about getting the coach to find the perfect eleven to sit down.
It's an encouragement for you that you've already played parts of two games in a row?
They're still just parts of the game.
I don't know how my condition will unfold and when I could finally play from the beginning.
Are you still optimistic about the changes that have taken place in Sparta?
We have to be, there's no other way.
It's the same with an injury.
If you don't believe, you can't go back.
Sparta has made some decision, we have to be optimistic, otherwise the situation will be even worse.
Fans came again in large numbers, but left in embarrassment.
What can you tell them?
There was an amazing visit here again, as well as with Bohemia.
I'm terribly sorry we can't make them happy with a better performance.
Shouting something they'll come next.
We can thank them for coming.
I don't remember visits like that when I played here fifteen years ago.
Can a season without cups at least be an advantage for the league?
In the meantime, we're struggling with ourselves, we're messing around, so it's not such an advantage for us.
For extreme tax evasion, the entrepreneur is supposed to serve ten years.
The Constitutional Court has rejected the complaint of businessman Jiří Toman, who is supposed to serve ten years in prison to a large extent for reducing taxes.
The damage is 645 million crowns.
In the complaint, Toman argued that the judiciary violated his fundamental rights, the factual findings of the courts in Zlín and Olomouc are said to be incomplete.
However, constitutional judges described the complaint as unfounded, ČTK found from the court's database.
It is not another court of appeal.
"It cannot, as a fourth instance, assess factual or legal misconduct allegedly committed by the general courts if they do not constitute a violation of constitutionally protected rights and freedoms," the resolution said.
Toman was tried as a fugitive.
The verdict has been final since November 2015, when it was upheld by the High Court in Olomouc.
At that time, Toman was in extradition detention in Argentina, where, according to the resolution, he found himself even at the time of the ÚS’s decision-making.
The imposed sentence is at the upper limit of the legal rate, which according to the verdict corresponds to the resulting damage.
The High Court described the case as an extreme tax evasion, which has a macroeconomic impact on the state budget.
The man served as chairman of World Servis from 2005 to 2008.
According to the expert, the damage of 645 million crowns was incurred only during the period from January to July 2008, according to the expert.
However, for procedural reasons it was no longer possible to judge Toman for the full amount.
According to the indictment, World Servis did not include all invoices in its tax returns.
Instead of monthly VAT and excise tax in the order of at least tens of millions of crowns, it paid taxes in just hundreds of thousands.
World Servis and some of the companies with which the company did business are related to the entrepreneur Radomír Vybíral, to whom the judiciary assessed 13 years in prison for VAT machinations in fuel transactions.
His complaint was also rejected by the Constitutional Court some time ago.
"Summer to Survive" and "Comrade Detective" play a nostalgic string
This iteration of the story is nostalgic both for characters from the famous 80's and for the dirty of the 1990s (when geniuses David Wain and Michael Showalter introduced on MTV the comedy sketch "The State"); and, of course, for modern times, specifically 2001, before stars like Elizabeth Banks, Amy Poehler and Paul Rudd fled to bigger projects.
As a cultural artifact, it is an easy parody and at the same time the embodiment of our modern Facebook era of permanent memories.
However, from the perspective of the franchise, it seems that someone is running out of breath.
“First Day of Camp” worked surprisingly well – not only because of absurd humor (an example is the story of the creation of a talking can of vegetables voiced by H. Jon Benjamin).
The authors moved away from the main funny point - middle-aged actors playing teens horny children - and couldn't make fun of previous events.
A sequel to "Ten Years Later," in which adults play adults don't put so much on the emotional level and satire is sometimes sporadic, including a parody of thrillers from the 90's as "Hand on the Cradle."
At one point, the character finds an Apple Powerbook, roller skates and “lots of other things from 1991” on the pile.
Many of the main story lines are repeated.
Good-hearted Coop (Mr. Showalter), now a writer, still hasn't moved over his love for Katie (Marguerite Moreau).
Victor (Ken Marino) is still a panic.
Not to mention the crazy plot that includes Ronald Reagan (also Mr Showalter), who is incited by George H. W. Bush (very well-occupied Michael Ian Black).
As always, many side stories are the best part of the franchise.
It's especially funny to watch the ever-young Rudd with his long hair alias the character of Matt Dillon from the movie Youth, whose once-responsive character Andy is now behind the old pepper.
The franchise could be triggered by moments of accidental madness, such as the "Seven Up" series, until it achieves what I suppose will be a logical denouement: "Wet Hot American Summer: Twenty Years Later," when, in the last scene, the whole group embarks on the premiere of "Years to Survival."
“Comrade Detective” on Amazon, meanwhile, appeals to a different type of nostalgia: the revivalism of the Cold War series “Such Normal Americans,” “Atomic Blond: No Regret” and even the “GLOW” series, in which the icons of the eastern and western wrestling scenes collide.
A comedy directed by Rhys Thomas, a comedy by Brian Gatewood and Alessandro Tanak is intended to act as a television production: a drama about two Bucharest policemen from Romania in the 1980s who fight for truth and atheism in the manner of Marxism-Leninism.
The main roles - tenacious Gregor and idealistic Iosef - are played by Florin Piersic Jr. and Corneliu Street, which they nabbed into the English-language Channing Tatum (executive producer of the series) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt.
It's an amazing idea.
Unfortunately, that’s not much more.
For some jokes, you'll laugh (like cops watching chess on TV - a parody for American football).
The series was bestowed by many well-known actors - among them Jenny Slate, Nick Offerman, Mahershala Ali and Chloë Sevigny.
"Comrade Detective", however, focuses too much on clumsy propaganda drama - which also includes an assassin with a Reagan mask - and looks like that, too.
Six long episodes are too lengthy, and comedy moments aren't enough.
In the case of shorter footage, the propaganda satire in "Comrade Detective" could work, such as in a scene where Gregor and Iosef repeatedly visit the U.S
A clear message for the imaginary communist audience is the fact that Western culture can turn everything into food and orgies.
Thoughty Romanian creators weren't wrong: we're doing so with our own culture, as well as with theirs.
Russian athletes did not see a ban on launches from the IAAF.
The International Athletics Federation (IAAF), at a congress in London, upheld an extension of an international ban on the start-ups of Russian competitors because of a state-backed doping program.
Voting in the venue of the world championship, which will begin on Friday, turned out very unequivocally as expected.
166 delegates voted in favour of extending the ban, with only 21 votes opposed.
The main reason for the rejection is, in particular, the fact that Russia fails to meet all the requirements for the introduction of an anti-doping system, according to the head of IAAF Special Investigation Group Rune Andersen.
His group has recommended that the ban continues.
Some older doping cases remain unresolved, a number of suspected athletes haven't been interviewed yet, and coaches are still working in the country with a damaged reputation.
The Russian anti-doping agency RUSADA, which does not meet the standards of the World Anti-Doping Agency, is not yet fully operational.
RUSADA, however, can already conduct testing under the supervision of foreign experts and the British Anti-Doping Agency.
"While progress has been made, there is still a lot of work to be done," IAAF chairman Sebastian Coe said.
The Russian Athletics Federation was suspended in November 2015. Russian athletes lost last year's Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro.
Despite the ban, 19 Russians who have previously shown that they have been tested outside the Russian anti-doping system for a long time will participate in the upcoming World Championships.
However, they will not be able to compete in national colours at the championship and will not play the Russian anthem in the event of victory.
The Bank of England will hold the interest rate at 0.25%
The Bank of England is expected to announce that the interest rate will remain unchanged, followed by lackluster economic growth and the weakening of inflation, which has suffocated speculation about sharp growth.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain "in the background" in the wake of maintaining an interest rate - despite squeezing family budgets.
Policy officials of the Committee on Monetary Policy (MPC) of the Bank of England voted six in two to maintain the interest rate at 0.25%. The results of the vote for raising the interest rate supported a lackluster economic growth backed by a weak increase.
In its annual inflation report, the bank predicted growth to 1.7% in 2017 and 1.6% in 2018 and warned that reductions in single-family budgets will continue. The bank expects inflation to rise to 3% in the fall.
At the same time, the bank hinted that growth should occur over the next few years as a result of inflation caused by Brexit, and it also stated that rents from movement should rise more than expected in financial markets.
Committee members also voted to withdraw part of the mammoth package, whose task was to boost the economy and which was put into practice a year ago in response to Brexit.
As a result of the foregoing, it is expected to take effect from next February, which will offer cheap subsidies to banks, although the Bank of England said it would now offer £££15 billion less compared to its planned £115 billion.
Just before the decision, the bank said: "The MPC predicts that gross domestic product (GDP) will stagnate in the short term, together with pressure on real household incomes and the burden of consumption."
On the topic of rates, the bank said it would be necessary to "tighten the belts of monetary policy" to soothe inflation, "at a higher rate" than markets expect.
Markets are currently predicting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and a further increase in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that any increase would be "graduating" and "limited."
The bank lowered its growth forecast for this year and next year compared with 1.9% and 1.7% predicted in May.
Projected growth in 2018 of 1.8% remained unchanged.
After the publication of estimates, the pound fell against the dollar and the euro.
The pound fell 0.5 per cent to 1.31 dollars and by 0.4% to 1.11 euros.
The decision to preserve the current state came after a disappointing increase buried hopes for further growth - GDP stalled at 0.3% in the second quarter compared with 0.2% of the previous three months.
The increase is very likely to remain at 0.3% in the third quarter, although by the end of the year it is expected to increase slightly, according to the bank.
The latest inflation report is a slight consolation for households affected by rising inflation and poor pay rises. The bank said the situation would get even worse before the rise.
The report goes on to say that the monetary policy "can't prevent" income cuts in the coming years, but wages are expected to rise "significantly" towards the end of the three-year forecast.
Economics is also expected to grow thanks to great interest in British goods thanks to the weak pound, composing a lower percentage of consumer spending.
That decision came a year after rates were cut to 0.025% last August in response to a shocking vote on an EU referendum, causing the pound to fall.
The bank estimates that the full unification of prices and the economy will take at least four years.
Prior to the bank's decision on the latest rates, many demanded a rise in rates, along with rising inflation, putting more and more pressure on households. Three political figures demanded an increase to 0.5% in June.
Inflation fell back to 2.6% last month from June's 2.9%, even though the bank said it had expected such a change and inflation should rise again over the next month.
The bank said exceeding relative to 2% of the target was "completely" due to a weak pound.
Andrew Montlake, director of Coreco Mortgage Brokers said: "Due to the recent fall in swap rates, it looks like competition in the mortgage market will only rise once creditors start competing for contracts looking at a good outlook towards the end of the year.
The market looks especially good for those whose existing contracts and re-mortgages end - there are options free of charges on offer, as well as low rates that are popular."
NHS to Prescribe Controversial HIV Drug to 10,000 Patients
From September, patients will gain access to 'transformative' treatments for HIV prevention on the basis of the Supreme Court's decision to fund the drug NHS.
The English National Health Service (NSH) has announced that the pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) pill, which is taken before sex and reduces the risk of infection in high-risk patients by up to 86 percent, will be offered through a health service to approximately 10,000 patients within a three-year trial period.
NSH agreed to fund the probationary period after an appeals court backed the Supreme Court's decision last year, commenting that the NHS disposes of funds to pay for PrEP despite a statement that the drug should be funded by local authorities.
The decision, which was announced on Thursday, was taken by the NHS of England from the mouths of CEO Simon Stevens as a 'significant intervention', while the National Aids Trust (NAT) described the decision as a 'decisive moment'.
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to begin accepting patients. Other clinics will join in October and by April 2018 there will be a full implementation across England.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is an HIV medicine given before sex to reduce the risk of HIV disease.
Tests have showned that PrEP significantly reduces the risk of HIV transmission among at-risk patients.
The application of PrEP before sex means that there is a sufficient amount of specific drugs in the patient's body to prevent infection.
These medications can be taken every day or at least two hours before sex.
Tested patients include homosexual or bisexual men, transgender people and people with HIV-positive partners who have not been successfully treated.
Clinics will identify eligible participants who sign consent to participate in testing, including men, women, transgender people and individuals with a partner whose HIV status is not yet controlled by appropriate treatment.
People living and registered with GPs in the UK can also apply in their local sexual health clinic and apply for potential participation in testing.
The programme will cost £££££££ £10 million and NHS England has signed a treaty this week to distribute medicines following an international public contract.
The trial period is designed to gather clinical evidence and assess the full potential of PrEP, its targeting, use and implementation on a large scale.
Simon Stevens added: 'This important intervention should complement and fully burden the ever-expanding and increasingly successful effort to prevent HIV.
It's another milestone in a more than thirty-year-old effort to stop one of the greatest challenges to human health.'
Deborah Gold, chief executive of NAT (National Aids Trust), said: 'We are witnessing a pivotal moment in the fight against HIV.
If PrEP is administered correctly and to patients at risk, it will offer the possibility of changing the English HIV epidemic.
Since September, NSH will offer patients who are at high risk of HIV infection with a new powerful tool that is individually controlled and does not succumb to the need to persuade a partner, which will improve the quality of many lives.
We are pleased to receive this statement.'
Ian Green, chief executive of Terrence Higgins Trust said: ‚Priority number one must be the rapid deployment of tests across the country; we must ensure its availability to each patient.
We now have the PrEP test drug at our disposal, and we are well on our way to protecting more than 10,000 people at risk from contracting HIV.'
Sharon Hodgson, the shadow health secretary, said: 'The start of PrEP's test period is a welcome and long-awaited change after months of delays and delay by the government.
Evidences indicate how transformative this drug can be as part of our approach to preventing HIV and ending the transmission of this life-threatening infection.
Subsequent tests will bring us one step closer to a full understanding of PrEP benefits.
It is now important that we start the tests as soon as possible across the country to protect people who are exposed to HIV and together end the spread of HIV in society.”
A life on the high leg: mezzanine and smart design have turned this small apartment into an Earls Court into a spacious home
Joanne Leigh, a former banker with a passion for real estate, traded a big house in Knightsbridge in 2014 for an apartment on the Earls Cour.
Located on the first floor of Queen Anne's clever terrace - is an illustrative demonstration of modern design, which uses the maximum available space.
"Before, I lived with my ex-husband in a much larger house in the Knightsbridge neighborhood," Joanne said.
“I was looking for something at a more reasonable price.
I came across this apartment in Earls Court and it had the same ceiling height.
I immediately fell in love with him, and I nodded to him the same day.”
Since then, an apartment of 1000 square meters has undergone a complete renovation.
A one-room apartment with a complicated layout - to get to the bedroom, you had to go through the kitchen - Joanne wanted to add storage compartments and a mezzanine to take advantage of the high ceiling.
“I longed for a high ceiling, large windows and a lot of light.
Such were my demands," she said.
To that end, she employed Erfano Azadi from the design and design studio Duck & Shed, based in Notting Hill, and used the nearly four-metre ceiling to create a home that was not merely a project, but also a place to live.
The ceiling did not reach such a height, so that two gaps fit above each other - the challenge for the architects was to create a mezzanine on which you could walk upright.
Erfan had to engage his creative thinking.
“The steel structure is quite complicated,” he explained.
“We had to organize both spaces so that we could walk upright.
It wasn't just to build a simple platform, but at the same time to bend the steel structure so that at the right height above and below the ceiling."
This arrangement allowed Erfan to construct two full-size rooms on top of each other - while adding to the dramatics at the same time.
On the mezzanine overlooks a beautiful living room of old copper window frames in Retrouvius design.
Mirror furniture in golden color, silver lines and Serge Mouille retro chandelier creates a unique style.
The use of mirrors plays an important role in drama, space and lighting.
The short corridor is equipped with mirrors on both sides and looks longer.
The mirror bar in the kitchen looks like a window to the next room.
At the entrance door there are storage spaces equipped with mirrors for the storage of cutlery and glasses.
The advantage of the living room, which gives the whole apartment the impression of a significantly larger space, is the ability to separate it from the kitchen, if it is not used.
Tilting kitchens aren't anything new.
However, what is unique about this solution are the folding door.
“We had to solve the technical side,” Erfan talks about the reinforced veneered door.
“The doors are provided with counterweights so that it is very easy to remove.”
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage space, Joanne gained another 16 square meters, a second room and a better overall layout of the apartment.
"When it comes down to it, what you sell is the living area - or the valuation of your property," Erfan said.
“A real estate agent basically calculates its price on the basis of a residential area when entering an apartment.”
In this case, it was possible to increase the living area while maintaining high-quality materials that give the space a luxurious appearance.
“Material is very important because you touch it and create an emotional bond with your home,” Erfan added.
“You won’t love the electricity in the walls.
Yes, they're important, but they won't get your hearts.'
As it turned out, a quality design and an increase in living space can turn an ordinary apartment into an exceptional work.
And, like Joanne, you can ultimately choose to stay.
The United States is disconnecting.
The cultural and political gap between blue and red states is deepening.
California has declared a ban on the travel of its citizens to the state of Tennessee and seven other states.
The state of Tennessee repaired it and declared a retaliatory ban on their citizens traveling to California.
Of course, this is not a ban in the full sense, U.S. citizens can travel freely throughout all fifty states.
California has banned spending government money on their citizens’ travels to eight other states, Alabama, South Dakota, Kansas, Kentucky, Mississippi, North Carolina, Tennessee and Texas.
Who's going to stop this madness?
California is strong for LGBT people, and any expression of disagreement with LGBT lifestyles is considered discrimination.
Tennessee, however, has passed a conscious reservation law, according to which a psychotherapist, who has a moral problem with the therapy of patients from the LGBT community, has no obligation to provide it, but has an obligation to provide these patients with another therapist who does not have such a problem.
Texas and other states have passed laws requiring religious charities that have received babies from mothers after birth for adoption are not obliged to provide same-sex couples for adoption.
Because of these Confidence laws, California has banned the funding of its citizens' travels to eight states of the union through its means.
In practice, this means that California professors are not allowed to travel to scientific symposiums, for example, reimbursed in Texas from their universities because they are co-financed by the state of California.
Similarly, school sports teams cannot travel to tournaments or competitions, for example, to Tennessee with the financial support of their schools.
They must receive either federal grants, but it is absurd for the federal government to finance citizens’ trips from State A to State B, or private sponsorship.
The state of Tennessee has run out of patience, and its parliament has passed a resolution that it doesn’t like California’s policies of high taxes, high state spending, high deficits, and the tolerance of illegal immigrants, but it doesn’t impose a travel embargo on California because of it.
He's imposing them as retaliation for a California travel embargo on Tennessee.
And there will be the same against any state of the union that would impose a similar travel ban on another state for any reason.
At the same time, he called on the parliaments of all states to stop this madness.
That's the facts, now a comment.
The situation is toxic.
First of all, all of this is constitutional, nothing is unconstitutional.
States have the right to adopt laws on the reservation of conscience, as well as to decide what their money will go to and what they are not allowed.
Secondly, what's wrong with a reservation of conscience?
If the butcher has a religious problem selling pork, why couldn't he offer only beef, lamb, poultry and fish?
Why force him to sell pork under the penalty, when butchers offering pork are enough?
In the state of Colorado, one baker is facing anti-discriminatory lawsuits and hefty fines for refusing to bake cake for a gay wedding.
But he's also refusing to bake cakes for Halloween or containing liquor.
Why punish him and not leave him alone and order the relevant cakes from hundreds of bakers of others?
Thirdly, the beauty of the federative system is that the constitutional rights of all citizens of the federation are guaranteed, but individual states can adjust internal, domestic politics in powers that are not federal, according to their own, i.e. according to the preferences of their inhabitants.
Then there is diversity, diversity, diversity.
California could be California and Texas could be Texas.
But California wants California to be California and Texas, and seven other states are California, too!
Fourth, members of the LGBT community once rightly sought tolerance for their lifestyle.
Today, many members of this community follow the rule of living and leaving to live, which should be governed by all reasonable people, but some activists strive not for tolerance towards themselves and others, but for extreme intolerance towards others.
According to them, no one is ever allowed to express moral, religious or philosophical disagreement with LGBT lifestyles again, and should be criminalized if they do.
The mentality of these activists is totalitarian.
They are modern-day enemies of freedom.
Fifth, I don't know the situation in American history, when states did such intentions because of political issues, except in the case of one.
Even in times of struggle for the civil rights of blacks, where the North and West were on the side of integration and the South on the side of segregation, the states did not impose sanctions on each other.
The only case was in the 50's of the 19th century, the political case was slavery and the war of the North against the South.
Of course, nothing like this will happen now, but the situation is still toxic.
The cultural and political divide between blue-democratic states and red republican states is deepening, and the U.S. is dismantling itself mentally internally.
Unauthorised agitation.
Criticist Putin has been given a greasy fine.
Russian opposition politician Alexei Navalny has been fined 300 thousand rubles for violating campaign rules ahead of the presidential election, which will take place next year.
The court imposed a fine on him for calling on people to participate in unauthorised demonstrations.
In the past two months, Navalny has organized two major anti-government street protests and announced that he wants to apply for the post of head of state in March next year.
Kremlin incumbent chief Vladimir Putin has not yet announced his candidacy, but he is widely expected to do so and win again.
Navalny said police came to his house in the morning and took him to court.
He fined him for calling on people for demonstrations through videos placed on the YouTube website.
The fine is three hundred thousand.
"Later we're going to take them off the judge's salary," a Kremlin critic joked on Twitter.
Russia's electoral commission recently concluded that Navalny could not participate in the presidential election because he had previously been convicted of embezzlement.
Navalny, who is considered an anti-corruption campaigner, considers the sentence to be politically motivated.
A fine of 300 thousand rubles, or 25 thousand rubles, was also imposed by the court on Thursday against two members of Navalny's pre-election team, Leonid Volkov and Nikolai Lyaskin.
Navalny entered Russian politics as an anti-corruption blogger and gradually became the dominant figure on the fractured opposition scene.
Authorities are trying to defend Navalny in the campaign.
In February, he was sentenced to a five-year suspended sentence for robbing the forestry company Kirovles.
In May, the verdict was upheld by an appeals court.
The verdict is so final, which prevents Navalny from running under Russian laws.
Brothers are fighting for an empire worth over $500 million
More than four decades after the founding of the empire by their Italian family, the two brothers are tug-out with one of the most expensive Victorian properties worth more than US$500 million.
The former Carlton Football Club director and developer Colin De Lutis resides on Toorak estate, only three rooms from his younger brother, Paul, who drove the dispute over common property and commercial assets all the way to the supreme court.
Paul wanted to leave the family business as early as 2014, but the sharp dispute is steadily laundered before the Supreme Court.
Along with his mother Anna, the brothers own a total of 38 properties worth more than half a billion dollars. The first business was bought by the family in 1973 on Smith Street in Collingwood.
At this point, the family founded Westco Jeans, which has grown into a chain with 160 brick-and-mortar stores.
In 1999, the family sold the entire company for $85 million.
Other properties have been purchased for money, and De Lutis' family's portfolio now includes offices, building sites in Brunswick and Sorrento, apartments on the Gold Coast and Mt Buller, and shopping centres in the suburbs.
Paul owns about one-third of the company's assets, while his father, Colin, who, according to Paules, is "a "significant driving force and dominant personality" of the De Lutis family, owns less than two-thirds of the assets.
The court files state that Colin owns 316-million-dollar properties, while Paul manages properties for £176 million.
It was the division of property and funds that became problematic for Paul.
"Colin's suggestion sounded significantly in his favour," Paul wrote in an affidavit in court in 2015.
Last Friday, after several unsuccessful attempts at reconciliation, the first session of the supreme court was held.
The brothers are now also competing over who will control the existing business - from which, according to his statement, Colin cut off Paul.
Colin contradicts that this is not true and accuses Paul of mishandling certain properties.
In a previous affidavitation, Colin said he believed in a successful resolution of the conflict.
"My Italian background dictates to me that family is the most important of all," he wrote.
The cost of the trial is rising. Hundreds pages of savvage statements, emails, and messages from companies including Deloitte, Pitcher Partners, and Charter Keck Cramer are flooded by attorneys, including Allan Myers, QC, and defense attorneys from both parties.
Ms. De Lutis and her late husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who had been running dairy bars and delicacies since the 1950s and bought a shop in Brunswick in the 1970s.
With the money raised by building the property, they bought a property in Collingwood, where Colin opened his first family business with jeans.
Colin said that when he founded Westco "in his final year of college, Paul was still a small, about 10-year-old boy."
Anna De Lutis said that, along with Luigi, who died in 2012, they hoped that the transfer of property to sons would take place well.
"Luigi often used to say that he never wanted his sons to end up in court," she wrote.
The other two brothers, Mark and Luke, are no longer part of De Lutis’ group of companies. Colin stated that Mark was compensated with £10million and Luke’s $2$26 million when he left.
According to Paul, both Luke and Mark "weren't satisfied with the terms of their financial compensation."
Colin has been holding back ever since, despite being a former member of the AFL's board of directors, as Eddie McGuire, a well-known television personality and Colina's best friend, said in his 2015 autobiography.
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul de Lutisova and their lawyers on Friday.
Only Colin responded through a text message stating that given the fact that the dispute is being settled before the court, "I have nothing to add to it."
"It is very sad that my brother Paul has decided to deal with the matter in court," he wrote.
In mid-2016, in one of the statements in court, Colin said he was trying to adapt to his brother's demands.
“Our father would be very dissatisfied if he saw our current situation,” he said, adding that “he still hopes for a successful solution.”
On Friday, all hopes were in sight.
"Our disputes will be resolved in the near future by a court hearing," Colin wrote.
Heat in the Czech Republic.
Teachers shower children.
Public transport drivers enjoy water and summer clothes.
In kindergarten Na Chodovci in Prague 4, where about 35 children now attend, they go out only in the morning.
The nursery is after reconstruction and planted trees do not yet provide a large shade.
There's not a swimming pool, but the children's teachers shower in a fountain.
In the afternoon, the children remain in the classrooms, where the blinds are pulled down and curtains closed.
The air is trying to cool with an air purifier because they don't have air conditioning.
It's also challenging for teachers and cooks.
"Temperature, which is indoors and outdoors, exceeds the hygienic limits for kindergartens, so we should not even have open operation during this period," said Miloslava Makovičková, the headmistress of the kindergarten.
There's a glazed corridor in the nursery, where, according to her, the temperature is around 40 degrees.
We ventilate in the morning after 6 o'clock, the windows close and we have blinds on the southern sides to protect ourselves.
"But it doesn't help much," she added.
They have better conditions in the forest nursery Na Hrázi in Hostivice, where children spend most of their time outdoors.
We've got trees on the property, so we're underneath.
"We have a beautiful walnut tree there, there is a table under it, and that's where we do various things," Barbora Kompanová, the nursery's principal, described.
Teachers with children try to walk into the woods, where there is a bigger chill.
Children's wet hats and they have various games with water on the agenda.
"We've got a hand-held pressure sprayer that's used to spray flowers, and we're using it to shower babies," Ms Kompan said.
Jitka Vilimovská, the director of a nursery in Novoborská Street in Prosek, Prague, also said that the teachers try to keep their children in the shade and sprinkle them with water.
"We've got a fairly mature garden, so they're under the trees," she said.
He walks out in the morning and then after 15 o'clock.
Every child must have a headgear.
Teachers also remind children that they should have a drink.
They can choose, for example, low-sweetened tea or diluted juice, and in the garden they have a drink with water.
They also have a vegetable salad to eat every day, and fresh fruits and vegetables, which are sometimes brought to them by their parents.
"Perhaps today a mother brought about a ten-kilo watermelon for children here," Makovičková added.
Drivers are given water or summer uniform.
The Prague transport company DPP provides water, ionic beverages or summer uniforms on hot days.
In most cases, tram and bus driver cabins are air-conditioned, DPP spokeswoman Aneta Řehková said.
In addition to the cabins, some trams are also air-conditioned in the passenger area.
So far, tram tracks have not been seriously damaged by high temperatures.
Minor damage occurred in a loop in Hostivař.
At current high temperatures, we make sure that drivers have a sufficient amount of drinks at their disposal.
"Cooled drinking water is available in all loops, alternating points and depots," Řehková said.
In depots and on some alternate locations, employees have so-called sodobars, which produce chilled soda.
Drivers are also receiving soluble protective ionic beverages.
"They can also take advantage of the summer uniform, which increases the comfort of drivers during their daily shifts," the spokeswoman added.
The hot and dry weather of recent days has resulted in an increased number of fires.
On Tuesday, firefighters in the whole of the Czech Republic disposed of 108, i.e. twice the long-term daily average.
On Tuesday during the day, when temperatures in the Czech Republic climbed over 35 degrees Celsius, firefighters were mainly engaged in fighting fire.
"Firms prevail in the natural environment, as well as those associated with harvest work," fire department spokeswoman Nicole Zaoral said in a statement.
Relief for firefighters did not bring even an evening change in the weather, when thunderstorms and torrential rainfall were replaced in a number of places.
In connection with thunderstorms, firefighters drove to 236 hits.
It was mainly the removal of fallen trees and branches.
"They intervened the most in the Central Bohemian region," Zaoralová continues.
However, the help of firefighters was also needed in Stvolínky in the Českolipsko region, where due to the storm they evacuated 60 people from a children's camp.
Tropical temperatures have also given work to Prague paramedics.
On Tuesday and at night on Wednesday, ambulances drove to a dozen cases of carbon monoxide poisoning from kara.
Therefore, people should have their water heaters inspected, paramedics warn.
Carbonic oxide is an odourless gas that is released during imperfect combustion.
The symptoms of poisoning include nausea, vomiting, headaches, and fainting.
In case of suspected poisoning, it is necessary to leave the infested room.
Trump's new chief of staff plans to curb the president's media diet
Many have tried before him, and they have always failed.
Maybe John F. Kelly can actually do it.
If it is, it will be the first.
Politico reported that the new chief of staff at the White House plans to curb President Trump's flow of information - including reports on television news - in hopes of keeping Trump away.
Here's part of reporter Josh Dawsey's reporter:
“When the new White House chief of staff, John F. Kelly, met with employees on his first day in a new post, he outlined the key issue of Trump’s White House, which intends to correct: incorrect information that comes into the president’s hands.
Kelly told the staff that he had to personally approve all the information—whether on paper or a spoken word—because the president could make a better choice based on the right information.”
Kelly's diagnosis actually makes sense, but many of his predecessors have broken their teeth to control Trump and his presentation in the media.
Trump's relationship with the media dates back several decades - and now that he's sitting at the White House, his habit of watching TV news is even stronger.
"If Trump, as a candidate, was upset by honest reports, it was convenient to show him that a lot of other media outlets were talking about him in a positive light, which was easier to persuade," Sam Nunberg, a former campaign adviser, told Politico in February.
Tara Palmeri, of Politico, wrote at the time that "the key to keeping Trump's tweets at bay - at least in the words of six former organizers of his campaign - is to ensure that Trump regularly watches the media that sings praise on him."
All right.
However, the idea that Trump’s tendency to publish his views on Twitter was ever “under control” is ridiculous.
Perhaps these campaign organizers know something that others don't know about - that Trump's tweets would be more outrageous if they weren't checked.
We'll probably never find out about tweets that Trump has never sent.
If his employees managed to keep him in check at least several times, then their efforts did not go to waste.
But so far, no one has been able to regularly prevent Trump from instigating controversial topics.
Part of this problem is that in the White House, which consists of rival factions, people regularly try to strengthen their agenda by subjugating Trump’s materials – which may or may not be reliable – that support their worldview.
Politico portal, which covered the issue from all sides, in May published a report on the inclination of advisers to submit questionable information to the head of state.
This is an example of such information from reporter Shane Goldmacher:
“Trumm’s current and former officials argue that the president often responds very angrily to negative media reports, especially reports of information leaks, and seeks to find their origins.
The situation occurred at the end of February, when someone slipped a copy of an article from GotNews.com - the website of provocateur Charles C. Johnson - who accused Deputy Chief of Staff Katie Walsh of being the 'source of many leaks of information' from the White House.
Notwithstand that Johnson has forever been banished from Twitter for harassment; or that he has not backed up his claims with any evidence; or that he has already made false allegations in the past and later dismissed them.
Trump read the article and began to ask Walsh.”
Goldmacher added that then-Chief of Staff Reince Priebus and White House Secretary Rob Porter "tried to implement a system to manage and document the information that President Trump will receive."
Would that work?
Kelly is now, three months later trying to pursue the same strategy.
It may turn out to be a more effective manager than Priebus, but Trump’s impulsivity doesn’t just disappear, and his helpers still compete for power.
These unchangeable factors will certainly make Kelly's mission more difficult.
Tourists negotiate ditch and return to 'Millionary Quarter'
A number of most movable homeowners in West Midland, England, are once again embarking once with determined tourists negotiating with local authorities over the rights to reclaim the field behind their mansions.
Tourists parked 20 caravans in a field by a thriving street in the town of Solihull, dubbed the 'millionary district'.
Bringing horses, children and an army of caravans, tourists return after being banished in May. They didn't even get scared of the ditch, which was dug at the site on the basis of regulations by the authorities in order to prevent their incursion.
The tactalous group found gaps in defense and penetrated the field with caravans through an unprotected gap, which served beyond the path.
Angry residents of the borough have attacked Solihull's board of directors, saying 'not able to solve the problem'.
One of the residents, who wished to remain anonymous, said: ‚They occupied the place on Monday night after they were moved to Bentley Heath, where they threw stones at other people.
The Board of Directors should be ashamed of its inability to solve the problem.
All tourists were moved to a different location and have now returned.
Last night, more arrived, and there will probably be even more today.'
She added that : ‚Birmingham board of directors knows how to deal with this problem. It uses court orders to protect parks.
I live on Streetsbrook and I don't want to pay taxes until the board of directors deals with tourists.'
Another of the anonymous residents said: ‘It’s ridiculous. Just recently we were watching the last departing tourists and now they’re back.
And this time there are even more of them, and this time they brought horses with them.'
Solihull, spokesperson for the city's board of directors, said: "We are aware of an unauthorised stay on the grounds near Streetsbrook Road and, in cooperation with local police, we are taking the necessary legal steps to successfully resolve the issue.
In order to gather as much evidence as possible, it is essential that as many locals as possible report any non-sociable or illegal behavior to the shelf at number 101.
This will give us an overall picture, which we will present in court.'
The husband of the MEP was hit by a car in Brussels.
He was brought in by a government special.
The government special transported the injured Róbert Žitňanský to Bratislava on Wednesday after noon.
Two ambulances were waiting at the airport and he was under the constant control of Slovak doctors aboard the plane.
The long-planned flight went uncomplicated.
Róbert Žitňanský, according to TV JOJ, had a serious accident in Brussels at the beginning of the summer.
He is said to have been hit by a car and has been in serious condition ever since.
After being transported to his homeland, he was transported to a specialized workplace of one of Bratislava's hospitals.
The economic analyst is the husband of the Slovak MEP Jana Žitňanská from NOVA.
She didn't want to comment on the incident.
The couple have three children together.
Italians toughen in fight against NGO lifesaving ships with immigrants on board
Italy has announced that it will prevent the arrival of NGO rescue boats arriving on Italy's coast with immigrants from the Mediterranean unless NGO signs new rules regulating their operations.
"If the NGO does not sign the new rules of conduct, it will be difficult for us to imagine that they could continue their operations," Interior Minister Marco Minniti said in an interview with Turin Daily La Stampa.
Minniti's warning came a day after Italian authorities seized the ship of the German rescue organization Jugend Rettet on suspicion of human trafficking, which significantly facilitates illegal immigration.
The rescue organization, which operates for the first year, declined to comment on the matter because of allegations made by Italian prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosios.
"For us the number one priority will always be to save people's lives. So we are very sorry that we are not currently operating in rescue zones," the organization said in a statement on social media.
“We can only comment on all current accusations after collecting all the information and evaluating the whole situation.”
Italian authorities have been tracking Iuventa's Jugend Rettet since October.
Her garrison is suspected of transporting immigrators on the boat, which were handed over directly by human traffickers, repeatedly.
At least one meeting occurred, according to the plaintiff's file, reportedly only 1.3 miles off the coast of Libya. The course of the meeting was published by Italian media.
The ship's crew is suspected of breaking the law and bypassing the Italian coast guard, which oversees rescue operations in the area - out of humanitarian enthusiasm, rather than anything else.
The Code of Conduct establishes the presence of an Italian police officer aboard ships such as Iuventa to monitor activities.
Only three of the nine non-governmental non-profits involved in rescue operations have so far agreed to adhere to the code: Save The Children, Malta's MOAS and Spain's Pro-Activa Open Arms.
The latter organization said on Thursday that the new rules were not necessary, but acceptable, as they did not represent any "substantial change or impediment" in terms of rescue operations.
The adoption of a new code of conduct has also been rejected, for example, by Doctors Without Borders (MSF), which is the winner of the Nobel Prize.
Minniti, meanwhile, has been told that a sharp drop in immigrants in recent weeks who have disembarked in Italy as part of rescue operations suggests that efforts to strengthen patrols off the coast of Libya and cooperation with local mayors bears fruit.
"In recent days, we are beginning to see light at the end of the tunnel," the minister said.
It also revealed plans for further talks this month with the mayors of Libya on an economic development initiative, as well as representatives of Chad, Niger and Mali on measures to reduce the number of migrants leaving these countries in hopes of reaching Europe.
Shameless Mesarošová: Silicone and cellulite crawled out of her overalls!
Hot summer days completely tempt you to wear only the most necessary things that will cover the most intimate parts.
Only Dominika Mesarošová does it the other way around.
At an afternoon meeting in the Prague Zoo, where the actors, presenters, singers and all those who had something to do with TV Prima were teeming, their body was wrapped in a mini jumpsuit.
And only a few normal movements were enough, and the model showed everyone her silicones, but also her butt.
Her jumpsuit cut between the halves many times, moreover, he got so high that she had to adjust it all the time.
There was no shortage of the attention of gentlemen and photographers present.
Michelle Carter sentenced for sending text messages prompting Conrad Roy's suicide
A woman from Massachusets has been sentenced to imprisonment of two years for inciting her boyfriend to commit suicide through text messages and phone calls.
Twenty-year-old Michelle Carter will serve 15 months from a two-year sentence behind bars and will then spend five years on parole.
Carter was sentenced in June for inadvertent manslaughtering Conrado Roy, who took his own life on July 13, 2014.
Roy's father testified in court on Thursday: "The whole family is devastated.
My son was my best friend.”
Carter has been left free until the decision.
She faced up to 20 years of imprisonment, but her lawyers argued that she and Roy were suffering from mental illness.
Dressed in red pants and a creamed blouse, Carter stood in place with tears in her eyes clasped and eyes pointing to the ground.
"Her actions killed Conrad Roy," the prosecutor told the judge.
“She ended his life to relieve herself.
She couldn't accept her own responsibility," she said.
"She didn't show any regrets."
Carter's attorney, however, said these were "unfortunate circumstances that he regrets immensely."
Judge Lawrence Moniz added that "her age or level of maturity, or even mental disorder, does not have a significant impact on her actions."
The plaintiff read a statement from Roy's mother Lynn Roy: "There won't be a single day so I don't grieve for the loss of my son.
I want him to be proud of me - I take care of his sisters, and together we will endure the immense pain that we will carry with us forever," the statement said.
Roy's sister Camdyn Roy testified that she was "persecuted" by the knowledge that she would never attend his brother's wedding and would never be his children's aunt.
“The loss of the person I’ve been seeing since I was born is the pain I’m going to carry with me for life,” she said.
The state of Massachusetts does not have a law in force to punish a person assisting in another person’s suicide. Thus, this case establishes a legal precedent.
Carter was 17 years old when Roy was found in 2014 in the seat of a car, poisoned by carbon dioxide.
The case drew the attention of an entire nation after Carter's text messages were published, inciting suicide.
“Perhaps, jump off the building or stab myself, I don’t care,” she wrote in the text messages Carter sent just two weeks prior to his death when Roy was on family leave.
A few minutes before suicide, she wrote to him, “You have to do it Conrade” and “Just turn on the generator and you’ll be single and happy.”
Judge Moniz said Carter longed for attention through Roy's death and ordered to refrain from any comments in the press.
The case raised many questions.
One of the people who sided with Carter was Amanda Knox, who was convicted of murdering her British roommate in Italy in 2007. Two years ago, she was acquitted.
Ms. Knox (30) posted in the Los Angeles Times: "Michelle Carter deserves understanding and help, neither prison."
Audi will adjust up to 850 000 diesel vehicles in response
Just the day before the announcement of Audi car manufacturer BMW said it would offer its customers a software update in more than 350 000 vehicles.
The BMW chairman said the company believes "there are more smart options than driving restrictions" in response to European cities - including Munich, which is BMW's headquarters - that are considering banning or restricting diesel vehicles.
Daimler announced on Tuesday that it would adjust three million Mercedes cars across Europe to reduce diesel emissions.
None of the companies called this decision a withdrawal.
European automakers have promoted heavily the use of diesel cars in Europe and the United States to comply with the Carbon Diocarbon Emissions Regulation.
Nitrogen oxide, which is emitted by diesel engines, however, is considered a carcinogen and can cause asthma.
At the same time, there is an increase in the cost of automakers to install equipment to neutralise gases emitted by diesel engines, which makes it difficult for cars to compete.
While German automakers are facing careful inspections, Chancellor Angela Merkel is facing accusations of pampering powerful car companies and ignoring their problems.
The companies are trying to avoid Volkswagen's mistakes, which in an effort to meet emission limits installed software in its cars, which reduced the amount of emissions emitted during testing, but under normal conditions the software was deactivated.
Several senior Volkswagen officials have been charged in the United States and the others are being investigated on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, a former head of thermodynamics at Audi's engine development department would be arrested in Germany.
Former Zaccheo manager Giovanni Pamio is of Italian nationality and is therefore not subject to an extradition, so he may face trial in the United States.
Earlier this year, Audi became involved in a criminal investigation by German police in Volkswagen after authorities accused Audi of installing a system to circumvent Europe's emission limits - extending allegations faced by Audi in the U.S.
Audi's involvement in the investigation may have a detrimental impact on Volkswagen: the luxury automaker makes up a disproportionate share of Volkswagen's profits.
The plane made an emergency landing on the beach.
A man and a little girl were killed.
The accident occurred on Praia de São João beach in the Costa da Caparica area, about 30 kilometers south of Lisbon.
One of the witnesses told the SIC Noticias television channel that the plane had flowned just over people sunbathing on the beach.
Some people from the beach were reportedly fleeing danger into the sea.
Unfortunately, two people were unable to escape from the machine.
A 50-year-old man and an eight-year-old girl were killed on the beach, according to local media
"The plane hit them and they were dead at the scene," local police chief Pedro Coelho Dias described.
Their nationality is not yet known.
There were two people aboard the plane, both of them escaped alive from the accident and officers took them for interrogation.
D11 or how not to build a highway.
And are highways even complete in the Czech Republic?
I'll give you a riddle.
It's unfinished, even though it started building as early as 1978.
Even though it's unfinished, it's constantly being repaired.
And it can't be built anymore, even if it's a building permit.
What's that?
Yes, the D11 motorway in Hradec Králové.
The construction of the next section of its section for at least a few months has been stopped by the Children of the Earth movement.
We East Bohemians get the highway in pieces, like when salami is sliced.
The wheel is cut, and then it remains to be seen when and if we get another one at all.
We don’t want anything miraculous.
We just want to shorten the driving time and we want to get trucks going to Poland or Poland from villages and towns.
Endless currents of trucks go directly through the cities of Jaroměř or Náchod, which is not reasonable to circumnavigate.
But so far, the last section of our dream highway was put into operation in 2008, an incredible nine years ago.
Not a knock since then.
Therefore, when the remaining four kilometers of the unfinished section to Hradec Králové began to be built recently, and on other sections to Jaroměř, an archaeological survey began with the fact that construction will begin at the end of this year, you could hear a loud banging of earplugs in eastern Bohemia.
I mean, nobody believed it anymore.
Last week it turned out that it will not be built this year.
And maybe even next year.
The Children of the Earth-Club movement for sustainable transport from Brno has filed decomposition.
The Children of the Earth are repeating their D1 tactics.
They challenged the building permit decision in its entirety.
In disarray, they call for a review of all already issued binding opinions of all the institutions concerned.
For example, the Hradec Králové Municipality, the Ministry of the Environment, but also the Ministry of Defence or the Railway Authority.
So far from D11 we are descending near the village with the eloquent name Burial.
So we're descending, we're just driving down the highway and suddenly we're standing on a side road, and we prefer that we then put ourselves in a column.
The worst thing is that you see similar stories all over the country.
Horrifying, constantly clogged road to Benešov, highway D1 or D8.
Everyone's got their funeral.
What does the Children of the Earth require?
What is the essence of the problems of road construction in the Czech Republic?
And why is the motorway network virtually unfinishable in the current system?
Read in the new Reflex.
Lavender from Provence threatens climate change
Lavender has become a symbol of Provence in the south-eastern France region, but they are now threatened by climate change.
Lavender is declining as a result of dry climates and harmful insects, causing dismay on the faces of researchers and farmers looking for solutions.
Eric Chaisse, an expert at CRIEPPAM Agricultural Research Institute in Provence said: "Growing lavender in Provence is at risk because in the last few years this plant has been exposed to climate change, which brings with it fewer precipitation in the spring and autumn.
This affects the lifespan of lavender.
It is also blamed by cicadas and grasshoppers transmitting micro-bacteria that thrive in the dry climate.”
There's a lot at stake.
Lavender attracts tourists, and the oil produced from them is an important component of perfumes and cosmetics.
Eric Chaisse said: "Instead of long and beautiful flowers, smaller, undeveloped and often weak plants grow.
The amount of oil derived from these lavenders is, of course, smaller.”
A possible solution can be a more efficient distribution of water and deceleration of evaporation of water from the soil, but also lavender spray by a protective mixture of clay and water and the breeding of multiple species resistant to dry weather.
Journalist's murder highlights growing threats in Mexico
Employees of the weekly Riodoce meet regularly every Wednesday to discuss news about the riots taking place in the Sinaloa area with the participation of organized criminals, corrupt officials and ongoing drug wars.
Today, however, under the onslaught of recent events, they meet mainly for their own safety.
They learn that it is important that they change their habits.
Two deserving journalists are talking about what would be safer: taking their children with them to an office that was attacked with a grenade in 2009 or left at home.
Security experts wrote three simple words on the blackboard: adversaries, neutrals and allies.
They ask reporters to give names for every word - there's no need for any evidence, it's enough to use imagination and premonition.
Choosing them from what - drug traffickers, politicians, traffickers and journalists are suspicious of taking money from the government or drug cartels - a catalogue of criminals tasked with covering up criminal conduct.
Violence does not cease, and bodies are piling up across the country; journalists among them are steadily increasing: according to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, there have been at least 25 since President Enrique Pena Nieto came to power in December 2012; 589 journalists are under federal protection after attacks and threats; and in all seven states, seven have been killed this year.
Among them, the co-founder of Riodioce magazine, editor and inspiration for colleagues, Javier Valdez Cardenas.
"The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico and work as a journalist," Valdez wrote in one of his many books about violence related to drug trafficking.
His loss is noticeable, although his presence is all-pervading - a large photograph of Valdez with the inscription "Justice", on which he points the middle finger, hangs in the facade of the building; two journalists - Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez - over the age of 30, have a T-shirt with a photo of Valdeze with a smile on his face, glasses on his nose and cap Panama.
The meeting takes place less than two months after his death; journalists speak of mutual traumas, nightmares, insomnia and paranoia.
Despite the fact that in 2010 a special federal prosecutor's office was established to deal with the cases of journalists, so far, according to a statement from the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, it has sued two suspects.
Most of the thousands of murders that occur every year are associated with drug trafficking and organized crime, so the killers of journalists go to court very rarely.
Journalists from Riodoce insist that they will report on the violence taking place in Sinaloa; even though they are destroyed by grief and this territory is very treacherous at present.
Without information about murderers and without a vision of justice, a meeting on security is pointless, Ibarra says.
“It’s foolish to waste time on meetings like this,” he adds.
“If we don’t get more information, you can’t trust anyone.”
On the morning of May 15, Valdez left Riodoce headquarters in Culiacán, the capital of Sinaloy.
He managed to escape several blocks when his red Toyota Corolla was stopped by two men who pulled him out of the car and killed him with 12 shots, apparently because of the title of the article - which can be translated as the Twelfth River.
The shooters then boarded his car and crashed him not far from the scene.
Valdez died at the age of 50.
He left behind a woman and two adult children.
It seems that in Culiacano, rumours are spreading rapidly.
In Valdez's case, however, there is a suspicious silence.
Since Guzman's arrest last year and his January extradition to the United States, Sinaloa has become one of the country's bloodiest battles, as rival factions are trying to fill the void.
Every day, several people are killed in the streets of Sinaloi, and the cemetery is full of embellished two-storey mausoles for drug kings, many of which are larger than houses.
Forget for a moment that the word “truce” means that one drug cartel controls a coastal state with precious ports and important routes through which drugs travel to the United States.
Despide this, as well as the fact that Valdez has been intimately acquainted with the risks of his employment, Ismail Bojorquez (60), co-founder and director of Riodoce, is stilling that he did not protect his friend.
He believes two misconducts have contributed to his death.
The first was the February issue of an interview with Damaso Lopez, the leader of one of the rival drug cartels that waged war with Joaquín Guzman's sons.
Reconciliation may have annoyed the sons; suspected gang members bought up all the copies of the newspaper the moment they landed on shelves.
The second mistake was not to force Valdez to leave the country for his own safety after the seizure of other newspapers in which the same story was published.
Valdez has become a legend in both Mexico and abroad, and his death is seen as an important milestone in the struggle of Mexican criminals against journalists.
He managed to survive for so long, and all of his friends and colleagues believed it would never change.
In Noroeste, he was practically a veteran when he decided to sell his $50 shares in 2003 and set up along with five Riodoca colleagues.
“It has been impossible to do journalism in Sinaloe without mentioning the drug trade,” Bojorquez said.
Over time, Riodoka gained a good reputation for brave and true reporting; newspapers began to thrive.
Journalists liked to embark on a search for problematic topics and published non-censored texts - the reader was fascinated by reading stories that no one else dared to publish.
Eight years after its founding, Riodoce won the prestigious Maria Moors Cabot award for a report on Latin America.
In the same year, Valdez won the International Freedom of the Press award to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists for his brave reports on Mexican drug trafficking.
Valdez openly admitted that he was afraid.
“I feared for my life,” he stated at receiving the prize.
Drug trafficking in Sinaloae is a "way of life," Valdez told Rompeviento TV in October last year.
“You have to take on the role of a journalist – or play stupid.
I don’t want anyone to ask me, ‘What have you done in the face of death... why didn’t you say what’s going on?”
Riodoce employees miss Valdez. The man was known for his joking and indiscriminate language. He was a good friend who liked to give advice and hugs - a teacher who knew how to survive.
Everyone relied on his daily routine.
He's always worn his hat.
He often went to El Guayabo's bar opposite the offices and sat at the same table.
Now they ask: did this routine cause his death?
His death in them also raised questions about one's own assumptions - how to do his job in the best way possible and stay alive?
“They don’t like it when you’re braided with their wives, their children, their clean shops, their secret landers.”
"These things were forbidden," Bojorquez said.
As a result, there is a high level of self-censorship and self-preservation.
You have to trust your instincts.
If you feel that something isn't playing here, hands off.
The trouble is, Riodoce editor Andres Villarreal said that “smell is a sense that can be fooled... and then that thing with Javier happened.”
According to him, as according to others, in Sinaloae no longer applies - as in the states of Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states that are home to a toxic mix of lucrative smuggling trails, weak governments and corrupt politicians.
The landscape is constantly changing.
No mobile phones are allowed in the room where Riodoca employees meet; a few days ago, spyware was discovered, which was bought by governments to monitor Mexican journalists and activists.
In front of the building, two police officers find relief in front of forty-degree heat in the shade of a nearby tree.
They were chosen by the state to protect Riodoce's offices, located in a four-storey building in Culiacanu's bourgeois district.
Journalists are teasing about whether these cops aren't among the 50 percent of the law's protectors who, according to the government, are corrupt.
It's been months since reporters last ventured into mountainous areas that essentially control drug cartels.
There are three main stories on this week's agenda.
The first is the murder of former boxer Julio César Chávez's brother in Sinaloa.
They are also enlightened for suspicious government spending, which took place in the state governor's hometown.
There have also recently been several abductions in one of Culiacan's most luxurious restaurants, just a block from the prosecutor's office.
Officially, no one announced the identities of abducted people or the circumstances of the kidnapping, so reporters were careful when they were preparing to write about what every resident of the city knew: that the restaurant was a popular destination for drug traffickers and politicians.
One of the journalists read from public records that this restaurant was registered under a name associated with a certain politician, who is a member of the Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI), controlling all levels of the political scene for almost the last century.
Several PRI governors have recently been charged with corruption in senior posts.
Villarreal asked reporters to write about earlier incidents that took place at the restaurant, including an incident in which one of Joaquin's "El Chapo" Guzmana's sons escaped the army's task force.
Just a month ago, readers would have read about this topic in Valdez's section, which was the best source of information about kidnappings.
"Valdez would certainly decipher the case," said Villarreal (46), nicknamed "El Flaco" for his slim figure.
"Now all the informants have turned their backs on us."
Valdez's office turned into a storehouse of signs and stickers for protests against the killing of journalists. Employees certainly didn't expect them to become activists on behalf of the press.
Journalist Miriam Ramirez grabbed several signs and set out to demonstrate in front of the prosecutor's office the following morning for another journalist.
El Salvador Adame disappeared just three days after Valdez's murder in the western part of Michoacán state.
Only charred bodies have been discovered. Authorities say, based on DNA analysis, this is Adame.
The activity of journalists escalates across the country. Many of them organize protests and scribble on sidewalks and “SOS press” buildings.
Bojorquez, the newspaper's director, is visiting Washington today, where he is trying to gain international support and achieve justice for Valdez's death.
At a meeting with the governor of Sinaloy, which took place the day after Valdez's murder, Ramirez accused authorities of tracking journalists and their murder for proclaiming the truth.
In the meantime, she asked to be transferred to another department out of concern that her anger could jeopardise her impartiality.
Valdez has repeatedly stated that Mexican journalists are "encircled" by organized crime, corrupt officials and the indifferent public.
In his latest book “Narco-journalism,” he wrote that journalists are dying not only at the hands of drug cartels, but also on the orders of politicians and security forces working with organized crime.
The media surveillance group Articulo 19 has blamed more than half of all attacks on journalists by police officers and public officials.
"You're going to die in Mexico because they want to silence you," Ramirez added.
Murders aren't working.
No one forgets the death of blogger Marie Elizabeth Macías, which occurred six years ago near the northern borders of Tamaulipas.
Her body was found along with a note purposedly signed by the Zetas cartel: “This is how I ended up because of my messages.”
Next to the severed head lay a keyboard and headphones.
Some branches decided to cease operations, such as the newspaper El Norte, based near the northern borders of Chihuahua in response to the March murder of correspondent Miroslava Breachová.
Others are still continuing, just as El Manana of Nuevo Laredo have done after killing their director in 2004.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez addressed publicly drug cartels with an article on the introductory page titled “What do you want from us?”
Some journalists have escaped from their homes or even across borders.
It's a difficult decision.
Finding a job in exile is difficult, as is getting rid of the feeling that someone is constantly stalking you.
And journalists are persecuted and killed, such as Ruben Espinos, who was murdered in 2015 along with four women in an apartment in Mexico City, three months after fleeing Veracruze.
For those who have chosen to stay and not give up their jobs, every day poses a big risk.
Ibarra, who once wanted to be a poet, admits that writing about drugs scares him.
“Mexico is going to hell, that’s why I became a journalist.”
It's Friday midnight, and Riodoca's employees are sitting on the pavement in front of the office - the latest edition shipped to the printer - drinking beer when the phone suddenly starts ringing at it.
They learn that 19 people have died in a shootout between gang members and police officers near a beach resort in Mazatlán.
The war is escalating, just as many of the messages left behind in this area by members of the syndicate.
Journalists publish this news from a mobile phone on Riodoce's website.
Not a single day goes by, and the opening headline changes again.
The sounds of police sirens point to another gunfight.
Bojorquez casts a glance at the police patrol to see if they're paying attention.
If they're afraid, they don't show it.
Under a huge portrait of the fallen co-founder of their periodical - whose middle finger is an eloquent slogan for the whole Sinaloe - all Riodoce's employees follow his footsteps.
“How could we think about leaving,” Bojorquez said, “when, on the same day Javier was killed, an internist asked me to send me to the streets?”
Dinosaurs used camouflage to avoid enemies
At first glance, it looks like a statue, however scary.
The researchers have now uncovered the conclusions of the first findings of an amazing rhinoceros-sized creature whose perfectly preserved remnants - which experts have dubbed 'Mona The Dinosaur Lisa' - were found by a Canadian miner.
The remains were preserved to such a high rate that experts were able to determine the reddish-brown color of his scaly skin and said the dinosaur had a 'problematic past'.
An article in the journal Current Biology said it was 'the best-preserved armored dinosaur found so far and one of the world's best dinosaur species.
The fossil is a new species of the Nodosauridae family. Dinosaurus lived in the Cretaceous period, about 110 to 112 million years ago.
His well-preserved condition literally shocked the researchers, who described him in the words 'really remarkable'.
According to National Geographic, armored white-eater is still the best-preserved fossil of its kind.
Shawn Funk, who uncovered the fossil on March 21, 2011 while drilling at the Suncor Millenium mine near Alberta, Canada.
He encountered something that looked like a foreign object and decided to explore it more closely.
The fossil was shipped to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Paleontology.
The researchers spent the next six years working to uncover an animal from a huge piece of land weighing 1100 kg.
Upon completion of the works, the dinosaur is ready to be discovered.
'Just narrow your eyes a little and you'll believe he's asleep,' said work supervisor Caleb Brown and naturalist of the Royal Tyrrell Museum, where the animal is exhibited.
'I'm surely going down in history as one of the most beautiful and preservative species of the dinosaur - the Mona Lisa of Dinosaurs."
By studying his skin, the researchers found that this herbivore - despite being covered in armor and resembled a walking tank - most likely faced a serious threat from carnivorous dinosaurs.
The heavily armoured dinosaur used red and white camouflage to protect against predators and used a camouflage system used by many of today's animals.
Experts say this has allowed him to blend better with his surroundings and avoid enemies. The camouflage system suggests that it was a favorite food of larger dinosaurs.
Reports of a 110-million-year-old Nodosauridae dinosaur first appeared in newspapers in May. It is currently available at the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology.
The researchers gave him the name Borealopelta markmitchelli after museum technician Mark Mitchell, who, along with the team, spent more than 7000 hours carefully uncovering the fossil.
Thanks to the amazingly preserved state of the fossil, Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists were able to document the patterns and shape of scales and tingling all over the body.
They uncovered a method of camouflage commonly used by today's animals in defense - the top of the animal was darker than its lower part.
Although this camouflage effect is common in nature, the researchers’ findings are surprising, because Borealopelta’s size far exceeds today’s animals using the same technique.
It is therefore possible that the dinosaur was so many predators that evolution prefered masking in survival over confrontation.
Many contemporary camouflage animals - such as deer, zebra or armadillo - are much smaller and also more vulnerable as prey, suggesting that nosodaurus had to literally fight for its survival.
'Prediction of a huge and heavily armored dinosaur illustrates the danger posed by predators during the Cretace period," Brown said.
Chemical analysis of the organic components contained in scales also revealed that dinosaur skin probably had a reddish-brown coloration.
The researchers continue to study the animal and are searching for further knowledge about his life, including the preserved contents of his stomach, to track down his last meal.
He believes that the dinosaur fell into the river after his death and was carried into the sea, where it sank to the bottom.
There was the same climate in the Alberita region at that time as in present-day Florida, and rivers and oceans stretched much further inland than it is today.
“This nodosaurus is truly remarkable in that it is completely covered in perfectly preserved scaly skin, and at the same time it is preserved in three dimensions and so we can see its original size,” Brown stated.
“For these reasons, the animal looks practically the same as it did at the time of chalk.
You don't have to use your imagination to reconstruct it; you just need to narrow your eyes a little, and it seems like he's just asleep.'
In the Cretaceous period, giant theropods, carnivorous dinosaurs who moved on two legs, walked on the Earth.
Although the king of all dinosaurs - Tyrannosaurus rex - lived several million years after Borealopelta, this armored dinosaur may have been the favorite prey of its equally fearsome ancestors.
These include Acrocanthosaurus, an 11.5-meter-long monster with a weight of over 6 tons.
The researchers, whose latest findings were published in the journal Current Biology, believe Borealopelt's body was washed into the sea after being mummified in mud.
Shawn Funk, who uncovered the fossil on March 21, 2011 while drilling at Suncor Millenium mine near Alberta, Canada.
According to the museum, it is the world's best-preserved armoured dinosaur, including leather and armor, which is additionally complete from muzzle to hips.
The animal reached an average length of 5 meters and weighed up to 1300 kg.
Two 50 cm spines protruded from his shoulders.
Researchers believe this herbivore ploughed the territory of present-day Canada until it ended up in a river that washed it into the sea.
His underwater tomb preserved the body in perfect condition.
Fossil remains of this species are also preserved to the extent that the armoured sheets around the skull still cover the skin residues.
As Michael Greshko wrote to National Geographic, “finding such a preserved individual can be compared to winning the lottery.
The longer I follow him, the shocking I find.
Fossil remains of this species are also preserved to the extent that the armoured sheets around the skull still cover the skin residues.
At his side lies his right front leg with his fingers pointing up.
It's easily I'll count the scales on his foot.'
How did one activist find out the answer to the question: ‘Whose Streets?’
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrell shook all over her body.
The day after she arrived in New York City, the St. Louis native sat down on a bed in an Airbnb room and went through Facebook posts on her phone.
She came across the contribution of a high school student.
"Police have just shot an 18-year-old, whose body is still lying in the street," it said.
Surprised that no one else had written this post to her, so she closed Facebook and opened Twitter.
User @TheePharoah constantly retweeted her wall posts.
Rapper from St. Louis posted on Twitter his posts about the death of Michael Brown, an unarmed 18-year-old young man who was shot dead by a white-scale police officer in Ferguson, Missouri.
He posted a photo of Brown's body lying lifeless in the street, from which no one took him even after four hours.
"That's crazy," Ferrell thought.
However, death was nothing new to her and her community.
She postponed the phone to return to him in the evening watching tweets about people gathering on Canfield Drive.
One of the men claimed he was the victim’s father and was holding a sign in his hand saying “Ferguson police killed my unarmed son!”
She watched the live reports - one of the officers stood in front of a group of protesters, holding a leash in his hand and a barking dog at its other end.
In another video, another police officer, as she stated, held a gun in his hand with which he was aiming at the crowd and yelling, "I'm going to kill all of you... animals."
"It's not normal," Ferrell said to herself.
“This isn’t the kind of murder we’re used to and can call normal.
I could feel goose bumps all over my body.
“I was angry and I cried.”
Immediately she booked a return flight and travelled home the next day.
After landing at 23:00, she picked up her six-year-old daughter from her father and decided to head to Ferguson in the morning.
The story of what happened to Ferrell and the city of Ferguson over the next three years describes the new documentary “Whose Streets?”, which will be released in cinemas on August 11.
It's a story about survival and protests, love and resentment, power and resilience told from the point of view of people living in the community who take to the streets demanding answers.
On her way to Ferguson, Ferrell introduced a dialogue with her daughter - dressed in a floral jersey and the same floral headband - about a topic they've discussed many times before - "about the experience with black people."
“Do you remember when I taught you how black people had to fight for what they believed in?”
"We're going to Ferguson right now because the police have killed an 18-year-old and that's not right."
"It didn't seem necessary that I didn't take her with me," Ferrell later said.
“Today’s events are the climax that has occurred in the past.
She had to know and see it with her own eyes and grow into it.
It needed to develop not only in activism and organizing, but at the same time it had to know the difference between the different layers of experience with black people, and what we had to do to free ourselves.
Despite being able to provide her with conditions in which she will not experience racism firsthand, she does not differ in any way from another black child-woman.
I feel that if I protected her from this experience, I'd just hurt her.'
After arriving in Ferguson, the first stop of South Florissant Road was across from the police department, which became a base of activists who were awaiting the words of Officer Darren Wilson about his eventually involvement in the incident.
This is where Ferrell and her daughter met a local businessman who was preparing food for others.
Together they prepared food in bags and helped to distribute it to the Protestants.
A new type of activism slowly began to emerge in Ferrell's eyes.
"My activism turned upside down after August 9," Ferrell said, adding that while studying at the University of Missouri-St. Louis, she was president of Minority Student Nurses Assn. and sought to introduce health care and food in black communities.
“I didn’t have any experience with the organization.
It was natural.”
A few weeks later, Ferrell became acquainted with the later co-director of the documentary, Sabaah Folayan, during an evening demonstration.
Folayan said he and her cameraman Lucas Alvarado-Farrar wanted to "only document" the ongoing events.
Ferrell thought, “Are you working on a documentary or trying to find a story that could be used?”
This type of skepticism penetrated the community through many people, often whites, who wanted to capitalize on pain and determination.
“We’ve heard that the voices of people who are born and growing up in Ferguson are very often neglected, and that’s what we wanted to change.”
Folayan has already established cooperation with co-director Damon Davis, who is an acclaimed local performer who deals with the subject of prisoners on death row.
His participation in the project was also attended by Ferrell and, along with six other local residents, who were followed by the "Whosse Streets" team in the years following Brown's assassination.
"No one speaks for us in this documentary, but it's our testimony," Davis said.
"That was important to me - how these people will be represented - because in the same way I'll be represented."
While the documentary's aim wasn't to concentrate on Ferrell's experiences - in an effort to show that the movement "is not about a savior leader, but about bringing the community closer together," Folayan said - it turned out that she was the most open to participating in filmmaking.
“Whose Streets?” describes a very personal aspect of Ferrell’s life, for example, how she fell in love with her wife.
“I felt naked,” Ferrell said with a smile on her face at the thought of the first moment she saw the final version of the documentary.
"But I understood that in order to do this job and humanize blacks, I had to open up."
In addition, Ferrell's story highlights the role of the eccentric black woman in the broader aspect of the Black Lives Matter movement.
Two of the movement's three founders - which, in response to the killing of teenager Trayvon Martin in 2012 on social media campaigns under #BlackLivesMatter - call themselves weirdos.
"Black eccentric women in leadership positions are ultimately behind preserving the movement," Ms Ferrell said, "because we're constantly fighting on multiple fronts.
Black eccentric women must endure the weight of these events.”
Folayan agrees with the statement, adding that "the movement is, in fact, supported by black women and a large number of eccentric black women."
He insists, however, that "this is not just an affirmative matter" and Ferrell is not the main heroine of the documentary because of her views.
"She was the driving force and the source of energy," she said.
“it’s not a coincidence that she’s on the front lines, because life at these junctions in the skin of a black weirdo woman is dangerous.
You can feel the rush of energy in front of the screen.”
Meanwhile, back in Ferguson, the cameras of the television stations have vacated their positions, but the heavy racial bonds between the community and the police persist.
Ferrell is serving a two-and-a-half-year probation for blocking the highway during demonstrations.
The social political atmosphere in an area she calls home has been described as "Clutter."
Still, she continues her quest, to which she has pledged since she took to the streets of Ferguson three years ago.
“It is our duty to fight for our freedom.
It is our duty to win.
We have to love and support each other.
The only thing we have to lose is our chains.”
There was a field burning in Cologne.
Dozens of firefighters went to the scene, helped by a helicopter.
Near Český Brod in the Kolín region, between the villages of Bylany and Liblice, a field with an area of about 15 hectares burned from 14:00.
The fire ceased to spread around 15:15, with firefighters declaring a third-degree fire alarm out of four.
According to fire spokesman Ladislav Holomčík, a helicopter also helped extinguish the fire from the air.
At 18:00, firefighters managed to extinguish the blaze, the fire caused damage for 700000 crowns.
Dozens of professional and voluntary firefighters were battling flames at the scene.
The fire occurred due to a technical malfunction in the engine compartment of the harvester.
Fire was also assisted by a helicopter, which carried out a total of six drops of water.
"Instead of intervention, we handed it over to the owner, until tomorrow 8:00 a.m. it will be guarded by a unit of volunteer firefighters," Holomčík said today.
A Becoming Resemblance: the artist creates portraits using Chelsea Manning's DNA
In the center of one of the rooms of New York’s Fridman Gallery, there are several faces of different skin tones—white, black, and brown—hung from the ceiling on the wires. Each of them seemed to fall out of the previous one.
All 30 portraits were created by artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg from swabs from the oral cavity and hair sent to her by Chelsea Manning.
Manning sent her samples from Fort Leavenworhort Prison, where a former analyst is serving a 35-year sentence for unauthorised publication of secret cables on WikiLeaks.
The new exhibition, launched on August 2, is called A Becoming Resemblance.
It's the result of a two-year correspondence between Dewey-Hagborg, who combines her experience with technology, computer science, and art, and Manning - a transsexual and groundbreaking dissident whose sentence was reduced by Obama three days before he retired as president of the U.S.
Manning's algorithmic DNA analysis, usinging 30 portraits to illustrate what a man with this genotype would look like by Dewey-Hagborg, if not an intellectual commention about DNA malleability - as well as the many ways in which DNA can be interpreted - and the inherent determinism of these interpretations, as well as identities.
Manning, who was born Bradley Edward Manning, has spoken since her youthing that she considers herself a woman. She was convicted in 2013 on the basis of twenty indictments, including violations of six espionage laws, computer fraud and theft.
All of her photographs were withheld by prison staff. Only one low-quality black-and-while photograph was leaked to the public, on which Manning, visibly concerned, sits in the driver's seat in a platinum blonde wig, with which she is now associated.
In 2015 there were still positive reactions to Dewey-Hagborg's 2012 project Stranger Visions.
As part of this project, Dewey-Hagborg used various forensic objects - such as cigarette butts and chewing gum - to create an image of possible forms of these people.
And it was at this time that she received an e-mail from Paper Magazine.
"They were planning an interview with Chelsea Manning in prison and wanted to complete the article with a portrait," Dewey-Hagborg explained on the occasion of the opening of the new exhibition.
However, visits and photographs were not allowed, so they approached Chelsea asking if she would be interested in a DNA portrait.
The artist and her imprisoned muse became friends within two years through correspondence.
They even created a comic called Suppressed Images, illustrated by Shoili Kanung, who presented a vision of what would have happened if Chelsea had been pardoned and could take part in the exhibition in person.
A few days after the comics were released, President Obama actually applied for Manning's release, which after several intolerable years spent in a Kansas military prison was a welcome change.
It's really interesting that the exhibition is taking place just at a time when Donald Trump is waging war with people seeking to disclose government information and transgender soldiers while trying to find new ways to incite resentment with his tweets in areas he should support.
In response to a question about this special timing, Dewey-Hagborg said that "everything happens for some reason."
Under the statement of the artist on one of the walls of the gallery, Manning calls with her enormous signature (the letter “i” in the surname is dotted with the heart) after the end of “selful factionalism, which is based on gender, race, sexuality and culture.”
On the opposite side are sequences of her mitochondrial DNA - a centipede of letters C, G and A recorded in pencil.
By including 200 letters (or nucleotides, according to the artist's sophisticated scientific dictionary), Dewey-Hagborg wanted to illustrate how surprisingly similar, at least from the biological point of view, we're all.
“I hope that people take away from it that our genome doesn’t care who we are, and that genetic data is subjective and everyone can interpret it in their own way,” Dewey-Hagborg said
“DNA tells many different stories, this is 30 of them.”
The last specimen of the exhibition, located in a separate room, is a single page from the above-mentioned graphical novel.
It depicts Manning embroidering in the manner of King Kong from United States Displinary Barracks with a megaphone in his hand.
"As soon as he prevents you from speaking, they'll win," she says.
“Never be silenced.”
"It's a direct transcript from one of the letters," the Philadelphia artist said.
“When I think about it, I get goose bumps.”
Manning has not yet personally visited the exhibition - which has been predicted in comics - but when it does, she will be greeted in the center of the room with her masks, her own genome portraits, lined up like a crowd of Protestants.
Which was also the purpose of the exhibition, including the nearly 160 cm high statue of Maning, on which her genetic haplogroups are recorded in pencil.
"In the past, they were placed on walls, but this time I wanted to make them feel like a crowd," Dewey-Hagborg said.
“I wanted to give the impression of a mass movement that takes the form of Chelsea and liberates it.”
Northwestern University professor and Oxford University employee sought for murder
These days, a nationwide search is underway for a Northwestern University professor and an Oxford University employee being prosecuted on suspicion of murdering a man who has been stabbed to death in a luxury Chicago apartment, police said.
On Monday, an arrest warrant was issued for first-degree assassination against a man named Wyndham Lathem (42) and Andrew Warren (56) for their alleged complicity in Trenton H. James Cornell-Duranleau's death, according to Cook County.
The document says Cornell-Duranleau (26) died from several stab wounds.
Chicago's police said the body of the deceased was found on July 27.
Officers were alerted by phone. Upon arrival at the Grand Plaza Apartments, they found the victim in one of the apartments with several lacerations.
Cornell-Duranleau has been pronounced dead at the scene and detectives are investigating the case as a homicide. Police, meanwhile, are searching for both suspects, authorities said.
Lathem has worked as an associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Cubbage, vice president of public relations, said in an e-mail statement that Lathem was now on administrative leave and was banned from entering the university's school premises.
"The relevant authorities are now investigating the matter and the Northwest University is fully cooperating with them," Cubbage said.
Warren is an executive financial manager at the University of Oxford.
A spokesperson for the university and Somerville College said in a statement sent e-mail: "We have been contacted by the British police, and we are ready to assist the U.S. police as well."
The statement also states that Warren's colleagues were shocked by the whole fact, wishing him to turn himself in to the police.
Anthony Guglielmi, head of public relations with the Chicago Police Department, said he was appealing for suspects to surrender to the appropriate authorities.
"The police have information about their whereabouts and efforts to find the suspects are escalating," Guglielmi said.
Guglielmi added that local, regional and nationwide authorities were assisting police in the search for suspects.
According to Guglielmi, the police received a phone call from the manager of the Grand Plaza building, which was alerted to events that should have occurred in apartment No. 1008.
Police described the whole scene as very violent.
Guglielmi said it was unclear if the incident took place on July 27.
He added that Lathem and Warren were captured by security cameras.
"We believe Professor Latham knew his victim," Guglielmi said.
The Grand Plaza management issued a statement to all residents: "Police are now working to compile a timesheet and the victim's profile and are investigating various motives, including domestic violence."
“In the near future we will complete the WADA plan” – Deputy Prime Minister of Russia Mutko
Vice-President Vitaly Mutko said it would not take long to implement the remaining World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) criteria and the re-establishment of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA).
"Currently part of the criteria of the new plan has already been completed, and RUSADA has already been granted planning and testing law in collaboration with the British Anti-Doping Agency (UKAD).
In view of this, WADA's plan has been updated," Mutko said on Thursday.
The Vice-President commented on the second part of the WADA plan to comply with the Codius - which describes the criteria for resumpting RUSADA - which was recently published on the organisation's website.
According to Mutek, the code does not contain anything that Russia should worry about.
“Everything has been announced and everything will be implemented.
The results will be available to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) and WADA in the near future," he added.
One of these criteria, however, calls for a public announcement of the results of the WADA investigation investigated by Richard McLaren about the alleged sponsorship of doping by Russian athletes by the government.
Mutko said: "We are conducting an investigation because our anti-doping system has obviously failed.
All measures have been taken.
However, there have been no state programs in our country, and we cannot confess to something that has never existed.”
Mutka's words were confirmed by Vitaly Smirnov, director of the Russian Independent Public Anti-Doping Commission.
In an interrogation for Russia's RSport Smirnov, he acknowledged the problems of Russian anti-doping officials, but added: "On many occasions, we have stated that the report contains controversial attitudes and regulations.
No one plans to accept the report completely without reservation," Smirnov concluded.
WADA suspected RUSADA of carrying out doping controls within Russia in November 2015 immediately before the doping scandal.
However, the organization has authorised planning and unification of testing under the supervision of international experts and the British Anti-Doping Agency (UKAD) this June.
After a visit to the organization's Moscow headquarters in July, WADA CEO Olivier Niggli said RUSADA was "on the right track" to restore validity.
Police have charged three people, one is a juvenile.
Police have charged three men with setting Wednesday's fire of a rare wooden church in Třinec-Guty in the Frýdecko-Místecko region.
One accused is a juvenile, all three are Czech nationals.
They face up to 15 years in prison for the offence.
The Moravian-Silesian police director Tomáš Kužel told reporters today.
According to him, it was an intentional crime.
"It's a very fresh matter and the police are currently working on the motive of the whole matter," Kuzel said.
He said the police would discuss the possible detention of the accused.
According to the director, further information cannot be disclosed.
"I can't say any more yet, because the criminal police and investigation and prosecutors who supervise the matter are actually working on the matter," Kuzel said.
The Wooden Church of the Body of God in Gutech came from the 16th century.
It was a valuable monument with many original furnishings and paintings from the first half of the 16th century.
According to preliminary estimates, the material damage will go into tens of millions of crowns, but the historical loss is incalculable.
Soon after the fire, the restoration of the church began to be considered.
Most of the costs should be covered by the insurance, other costs are ready to be shared by the Ministry of Culture and the city, a public collection is also being prepared and a contribution from the owner of the monument, i.e. the local parish.
A similar 16th-century wooden church in Ostrava-Hrabová was also destroyed by a fire fifteen years ago.
This was caused by a fault in the wiring.
An exact copy was made in its place.
The state counts on citizens in the defense of the state.
It will support their training.
With financial support for associations usable in the defense of the state, with voluntary shooting training or with a team of training supervisors, it calculates the substantive intention of the law on the readiness of citizens for the defense of the state.
The Ministry of Defence, which has been tasked from the government, promises to increase the physical prowess of Czechs and their abilities, which could then be used by the state in crisis situations.
This is according to a proposal prepared by the Ministry.
Should the law be adopted in the future, the state would expend hundreds of millions of crowns in support of the military.
According to its authors, the basic objective of the proposal is to create an effective system that would help to increase the readiness of the inhabitants of the Czech Republic to the defense of the state, beyond the performance of military duty and on the basis of voluntary.
For example, the state would financially or material support associations of radio amateurs, shooters or cynologists and would make it possible to increase the capabilities of their members.
In return, it would obtain data on people who are willing to come to the country in times of crisis for help if necessary, and about their abilities or material equipment.
There could also be accredited instructors who would help associations and other interested parties in their training or education.
It also envisages organizing discussions with war veterans or people who have behaved heroically.
One of the possible options assumes that a new office would be established, which would be in charge of the administration and coordination of the project.
The authors of the proposal emphasize that the participation of people should be voluntary and the state would use their abilities only after declaring a state of threat.
"The whole concept has to respect the gradual blurring of previously obvious boundaries between different types of threats," it said, citing the dangers of so-called hybrid threats.
Ministerial officials noted that it is therefore necessary to focus on supporting the widest possible set of capabilities, not just the military ones.
"The basic objective of the intention is to create a very broad base, which will allow every citizen to respond in a qualified manner to situations endangering the sovereignty of the state or the lives, health and property of persons, to protect themselves, or to contribute to the protection of others, and by misconduct not to make it difficult to carry out tasks for the armed forces of the Czech Republic," the intention states.
The adoption of the law would bring considerable financial demands.
According to the Ministry, their amount will depend on the chosen option.
For example, the Office foresees financial support for projects to prepare citizens for the defense of the state up to 100 million crowns.
If an administrative and coordinating center was established, its operation could cost around 60 million crowns a year.
Further money would go to salaries or to build an information system.
The proposal also foresees that it would have to expand the scope of regional military headquarters, which together would employ an additional 42 soldiers.
Representatives of the ministry have previously stated that the law should also help resolve the disagreement of some Czech politicians with the introduction of a European Union directive on gun ownership.
Its adoption would allow shooters, who will be part of the system, to keep some of the weapons forbid by the Directive.
Startups that will help your company don't become the next Uber
Amy Errett wanted to measure the level of employee satisfaction of her e-commerce startup, but didn't find an adequate survey.
The answers were often inaccurate, unhelpful, or even deceptive.
And even though promising the anonymity of the survey, some employees expressed their distrust throughout the process.
“I’ve been looking for consistency and objectivity,” said Errett, Madison Reed, owner of San Francisco-based e-commerce, which employs 75 people.
She asked for help from the outside.
There are constantly new startups from the human resources industry on the market to help companies with employee satisfaction surveys.
By creating and licensing the software with the specific intention of measuring the obligations of employees, startups allow companies to conduct quick voting, target specific teams and demographic groups, as well as offering complete anonymity of employees and a hotline for complaints, and, in some cases, also allow informants to bypass supervisors and contact directors directly.
“There are tools in the market like Strava and Fitbit that track your health, but where is Fitbit for your company?” said Jim Barnett, co-founder and CEO of Reedwood City-based startup Glint.
Errett said she has gained more awareness of what her employees are thinking and feeling in three years thanks to Glint.
In addition to rapid surveys and voting specific teams, Glint offers a company temperature map and a cursory overview of the groups with the highest number of complaints, as well as an overview of managers with a low rating - which allows the user to work on a remedy.
Companies are beginning to realize that it's necessary to pay attention to the culture of the working environment - in case they become the next Uber - whicken in scandals after a former employee posted on their blog a post describing the company's environment and the harassment of employees who dare to speak openly.
For startups like Glint, the demand for corporate culture surveys represents a lucrative business opportunity.
According to the Let's Talk Payments website dealing with technical and financial progress, the value of the global control, regulation and compliancy sector can exceed $118.8 billion by 2020.
Startup Convercent of Denver, Colorado, which helps companies prevent and detect inappropriate behavior, saw a small increase in interest and activity at the beginning of the year, just ahead of Uber's scandals.
Nearly 600 Convercent clients include Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
It has also recently accepted Uber under its wing.
Like Glint, Convercent allows companies to submit customizable “lightning” surveys, collect real-time confidential information, and display temperature maps of problem areas.
It also offers an anonymous text hotline that allows employees to report inappropriate behavior.
If the Director-General is involved in the issue, the complaints shall be sent directly to the Management Board.
"The Court of Public Opinion has empowered regulators," said Patrick Quinlan, founder and chief executive of startup Convercent.
If a company is convicted of mishandling its employees or unethical conduct - and if regulators don't intervene - it can face costly consequences as a result of customer boycotts, employee exhaustion or legal proceedings.
The Ruby Tuesday restaurant network, which employs over 25,000 people across 500 locations, has been using Convercent for more than a year to increase employee awareness of corporate policies and procedures and ensure an easy way to contact corporate leadership.
Previously, if an employee wanted to report any problem, he had to trace a phone number or e-mail to the management of the company, officially file a complaint and hope for a successful resolution.
It was often a deterrent and unpleasant experience, said James Vitrano, Ruby Tuesday's attorney general, according to which there had not previously been a reliable way to track employee complaints.
Now, when a company uses Convercent, rapid detection is ensured, and solutions to problems that have never previously reached managers sitting in offices, miles away from the actual resolution of complaints - for example, discrimination, harass or unfair treatment.
“It’s getting closer to a holistic, overall perspective on employees’ experience,” said Vitrano, who heads Ruby Tuesday’s group overseeing risk management.
“At the same time, we protect shareholders from litigation by employees.”
The companies ceased to take ethics, values and employee participation lightly in 2002 after accounting firm Arthur Andersen collapsed after Enron's fall in the abuse of employees' ethical rights, Quinlan said.
But it wasn’t until “social media shine a light on the whole thing,” that companies began to realize that they couldn’t simply hide their skeletons in a closet in the online world.
“Before people started using technology to track ethics, they relied on faith,” Quinlan added.
Glint and Convercent offer their software in the form of a service for which the company pays regular fees.
It's a business model and an opportunity endorsed by daring capital investors supported by both startups.
As part of his February campaign, Convercent withdrew $10 million from companies such as Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital in total, and eventually took home $47 million in capital.
Glint received $10 million from Bessemer Venture Partners in November and earned €££££££££££€££££
These investments are hardly surprising given the mutual nature of societies, culture and venture capital.
The increasing number of research clearly confirms that today’s employees expect more from their workplace than ever before.
And in competitive markets such as Silicon Valley, high salaries and interesting projects are not the main advantage.
Employees want to perceive that they are accepted and appreciated and that they devote their time to a company that strives for a positive mission.
“If people are happy in their work, they perceive that their voice is heard, and that their work is rewarding, they strive for the company’s success,” said Nina McQueen, vice president of global benefits and employee benefits at LinkedIn, which leverages both Convercent and Glint.
Investors make money if companies thrive and companies thrive if their employees are satisfied.
And when an analytical tool emerges in the market to ensure employee engagement and commitment to the company, it’s no wonder that there are a lot of investors to support this effort.
According to work culture experts, it is important to have data on employee satisfaction.
But the data itself is pointless if the company does not take the necessary steps.
The reality is that once the company asks for feedback from its employees, there can be major changes.
And if change doesn't happen quickly - in the worst-case scenario at all - it can cause disappointment and disinterest among employees
"Once you ask for 4000 opinions, you have to be prepared for 4000 conversations," said Russell Raath, director of consulting at Kotter International, which has witnessed many companies relying too heavily on the data itself.
“Since once you don’t get things done, the staff will start to wonder, ‘Have they heard me at all?’
Are they interested in me?
And if not, then why should I care?" "
Madison Reed, which now conducts an employee survey on a regular basis every month, can solve the problem within a day thanks to Glint, Errett said.
After gathering feedback from employees frustrated by slow decision-making and quality of communication, Errett was able to rearrange several teams within the company and begin communication training to successfully solve the problem.
Ruby Tuesday gains a very important view of its employees and hopes that in the long run it will achieve high employment in the field, which is known for frequent layoffs.
“If you don’t strive to create a transparent working environment, employees won’t trust you,” Vitrano said.
"And once you've started losing employees, you'll lose customers.
And with the outflow of customers, the whole company goes bankrupt.”
Thursday became the hottest day.
Thursday, with 38.3 degrees recorded in Strážnice in the Hodonín region, is the warmest day of the year in the Czech Republic.
It surpassed Tuesday, which was originally the hottest day of the year with 37.3 degrees.
This was reported by employees of the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute.
Compared to Tuesday, however, there were not many temperature records.
The heat mainly affected Moravia, it was mostly cloudy in the Czech Republic.
On Tuesday, records were surpassed or leveled at 35 out of 145 weather stations measuring over 30 years.
On Thursday, only nine long-term measuring stations recorded the record.
For example, in Strážnice today's temperature was the same as the previous record from 2013.
In Lednice in the Břeclav region today's 37.4 degrees surpassed the original maximum by four tenths of a degree and in Pohořelice in the Brno region the current 37.1 degrees exceeded the record by three-tenths.
Both records, as in Strážnice, came from 2013.
High temperatures today appeared mainly in the south and east of Moravia.
After Strážnice it was the biggest heat in Dyjákovice in the Znojmo region and Brod nad Dyjí in the Břeclav region, where the mercury climbed to 37.8 degrees, but did not break the record.
By contrast, for example, in western and north-western Bohemia, according to the CHMI website, today's highest temperatures were only around 22 degrees Celsius.
"Temperatures started to rise there until later in the afternoon," the meteorologists said.
According to the CHMI, the current heat in the Czech Republic will gradually disappear, and more significant rains could occur on Sunday.
However, in most places in Moravia, high temperatures will remain on Friday and in the southeast of Moravia on Saturday.
We've had such trouble here.
Billionaires are not satisfied with the same vote in elections as every full-time person in the country.
They want to decide directly, and because not everyone is as brazen as Andrej Babiš, who wants to own a state, they buy political parties.
Penta owner Marek Dospiva, together with other colleagues behind the water Martin Burda and Antonín Fryč, are sponsoring Robejšek’s Realists.
The second life of the Civic Democratic Alliance is financed by the owner of the insurance company and real estate entrepreneur Pavel Sehnal, who is also the chairman of his party.
Convicted corrupter and king of Czech gambler Ivo Valenta has acquired the Private Party.
The Agrofert political movement, as Andrej Babiš’s project was mistakenly named by Kroměříž mayor Jaroslav Němec, is a chapter for himself.
The mayors have also recently caught their billionaires.
It is Dalibor Dědek, the owner of the Jablotron security equipment company.
According to his own words, he enters into politics to do good.
Entrée did not do very well in the mayoral jersey.
"I've never compromised small goals," he said at a press conference.
This means that my goal is for the Yes movement to gain twenty percent of the mayoral movement in the elections.
Some people smiled.
Grandfather's mischief, however, is rather symptomatic.
Billionaire Dalibor Dědek is a man of firm opinions.
Although he intends to compete for the votes of voters in the jersey of the Mayors, he continues to support Andrej Babiš.
His grandfather supported Babiš in the past parliamentary elections.
"Babiš is not a seasoned speaker and does not rule the pleasing rhetoric of political snakes," he wrote about it before the 2013 elections.
However, it certainly does not go into politics because he wants to improve himself personally.
For me, Babiš will remain a hero.
He's willing to step out of his world that's in control and do something for all of us.
Shortly after the Mayors introduced him as a new reinforcement, he confessed his admiration for Babiš again.
And he said he wanted the mayors to learn how to collaborate with Babiš.
Some may appreciate that Dědek does not change his opinions and stands up for himself, no matter what jersey he wears.
Others may speculate whether Babiš sends to the Mayors to build his B someone so clumsy.
Others may ask if Jablotron's boss, for example, hasn't broken up with Agrofert's boss in a bad business and isn't looking for any other way to defend himself against his business rival.
Dědek's first public appearances, however, didn't just revolve around Babiš.
Dědek promised to send ten million crowns to the Mayors for the campaign.
And if they need any more money, he'll send more.
Several years ago, it would have been difficult for anyone to pause over such a statement.
After all, the court-appointed thug Zdeněk Bakala was throwing millions on all sides before the 2010 elections.
The political division of Vít Bárta’s security agency, TOP 09 and civic democrats came together in almost thirty million.
But today is a different time.
The law prohibits anyone from giving more than three million to a political party.
When Dědek enters politics, he could at least study the rules of the game.
He might not have to wonder why politics has such a bad reputation in our country.
Dědek crowned his first days in politics with a proposal for a tax advantage for families with studied children.
People with a university degree come to about fifty thousand per month.
Those with the basics for less than twenty.
A man with fifty thousand is rich in his circumstances.
The one with the twenty poor.
In Dědek’s vision of society, the poor honestly pay taxes and the wealthy enjoy the benefits of the functioning of the welfare state, which they do not participate in much.
The riches simply have to be helped so that they do not suffer misery with poverty.
The mayors can congratulate each other on their new acquisition and Babiš can rub their hands.
The rest of us, thanks to Dědek, can confirm that billionaires are not good people.
The question remains of who to vote for.
For me, those who know the rules of the game and want to follow them.
Those members of their party, neither citizens and citizens of the state have for their employees and country for their property.
And finally, those who want to live well in a rich country for all people, not just some.
No one wanted Trump's paradise.
The villa complex had to cheapen by 234 million.
