NCAA has decided to put an end to two workouts a day. For the Bruins, there have been long days
The eleventh morning is approaching and UCLA American football players are squeezing at the sidelines of a new school playground.
Some of them take off their jerseys and reveal a sweaty back, an unmistakable emblem on the first day of the training camp.
For most team members, these few minutes of doing nothing is going back to a lunch break.
Players enrolled in the summer semester will retire to rooms after a three-hour workout to study for final exams before returning to Wasserman Football Center for a meeting that takes place the coming morning at 6:30 p.m.
“There’s going to be a good job for these guys for the next three days,” Bruins coach Jim Mora said just before Monday swung Tuesday.
By eliminating two training sessions in one day, the NCAA wants to relieve the players - paradoxically, the training days are even longer - at least until Friday, when the final trials end.
That's why UCLA's first three workouts are scheduled for 19:15 - to squeeze into the already crowded diaries of many students.
For the first time in six seasons that Mora has spent as a coach at Westwood, the Bruins training camp begins a week earlier than last year, and training sessions are limited during the summer semester. The decision to limit the number of training sessions from two to one a day also has its impact.
A total of eighteen workouts are scheduled until August 20, after which formal preparations begin for the first game against the Texas A&M team, which takes place in September.
Mora said he supported the NCAA's decision, despite adding another item to the players' already packed diary.
"Three or four days the players are under a lot of stress and it's very exhausting for them," Mora said.
“But no one gave up, everyone tries. They’re great.
I think on Saturday, when we have more time, everything will calm down.”
UCLA will be closed after the end of the training camp, which won't be easy looking at the clear lines of surrounding land from the adjoining parking lot and the luxury Luskin Center hotel, which was occupied by players after harvesting the protective net behind a goaliness stick.
Bruins players should occupy the hotel starting this week, but Mora acknowledges that when the rooms are emptied during the season, there could be a problem.
"The land will be guarded by the security service," Mora added.
“The safety is taken care of, but it’s not excluded that someone can rent a room and record our workouts.
Such material is well traded.
Fortunately, the hotel will be guarded by security, as well as car park #8.
We will make the most use of our resources.
I believe that everything will turn out well.
It’s all planned.”
Facebook plans a more thorough scrutiny of the facts to limit the number of fake news
Facebook will send more potentially fake articles for review to third parties and display findings under original posts in an effort to combat so-called "fake news," the largest social network said Thursday.
In a statement on its website, the company writes that it will start using updated machine learning to detect possible hoaxes that it will send to third parties for verification and potentially display the results of the survey under the original posts.
Facebook has been described as one of the main sources of fake news, and many believe these unconfirmed news has affected last year's election as president of the United States.
In Europe, political debates are being held about the matter. French voters are flooded with an avalanche of fake news about the May presidential election, and Germany plans to impose a fine on the social media network in advance of the September elections if it does not urgently remove angry posts.
Facebook said in a statement issued in Germany on Thursday that it was planning to launch testing a new feature to control fake news in the U.S., France, the Netherlands and Germany.
"In addition to displaying third-party verified reports, people are asking for more content to be better informed and to be able to make the right decisions about what they can share," Sara Su, Facebook's news product manager, said in a blog post.
She added that Facebook continues to test the function of "related articles" and is also working on other changes to reduce the number of fake news.
Creation of a graphic novel: a story about A Castle in England
Scotney Castle has recently starred in Squeeze's music video, and Margaret Thatcher also found refuge there during the 70's and 80's.
Scotney, however, has witnessed from his cosy spot by the Bewl River in Kent, Britain, a 700-yeary tumultuouous history that inspired the curly-hungry Jamie Rhodes of the York county - a well-known penchant for historical documents - to create a graphical novel.
“The sight of a man who is 300 years after death fascinates me,” says Rhodes with a serious face.
“When I hold a letter in my hands, I feel a personal connection.”
The graphic novel A Castle in England documents the lives of families who dwelt at Scotney Castle from the Middle Ages to the Edwardian period. Each story is illustrated by a different British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg.
But it’s not the first Rhodes book inspired by archives: he’s also the author of Dead Men’s Teeth and Other Stories (2014), a collection inspired by the British Library – a project that was very emotional for him.
“I was intrigued by the thumbprint on the letter written by a double agent in the 70’s who worked for Jakobits,” he said.
"That's his thumbprint!
And letters always smell like smoke, because in those times you had to light a candle to read.
If I sniff the letter now, 300 years later, it's like I'm going back in time.
“I love the archives!”
For someone who is so fascinated by the old papers, support from National Trust and Arts Council England and the opportunity to stay in the castle for almost four months almost like a dream. (“To tell you the truth, I’ve extended my stay a bit.”)
Rhodes spent his time exploring the Victorian “new” castle, going through the archives, and studying many treasures and curiosities, such as the hyena’s head on the wall and bottles that had been tossed in a ditch for centuries.
He walked along the property and explored the ruins of the old castle, talking to gardeners, gardeners, and occasionally with artists who worked on illustrations for his book.
At last night, “he drank whiskey and wrote.”
Scotney's intimate history is a relatively large unknown, as National Trust gained access to documents only in 2006 following the death of the last heir, Elizabeth Hussey.
When Rhodes arrived at the site ten years later, employees were just beginning to penetrate the archives, which Rhodes described in words, such as when “aristocrats of many centuries thwastle with their hands, saying, ‘Take it to the attick.’”
Diaries, letters from the days of the British Empire, maps depicting property: “You may think the dispute over land is boring,” Rhodes said, “but I came across one case where two families were tugging at birches.
Leave it to the people!"
Some of the stories in the book use the castle as a springboard and delve deeper into English history, including a story titled The Labourer that follows a man who leaves a newly built castle to join the English Uprising in 1381.
"I have found a record of a mution at a castle from 1380 that has been dubbed Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex in the book," Rhodes said.
“The English uprising actually started in this area and it occurred to me that rebellion was a good starting point.
“Maybe he’s got something to do with each other.”
Other stories are closely tied to the history of the castle, such as The Priest, which tells the story of a Jesuit priest who has been hidden by Darrell's family for seven years during the English Reformation.
Or The Smuggler about an 18th-century smuggler named Arthur Darrell, who allegedly staged his own burial when he filled the coffin with stones (which wasn't discovered until several years after the coffin was uncovered).
Rhodes's favorite family are Darrell's: "I was intrigued by the male generation - the men of that time were always indebted and spent money on things they couldn't afford.
The different generations of the Darrell family dealt with this by marrying wealthy or old mistresses - I imagine they were good-minded gentlemen who liked to mottle the heiresses."
Ending his stay at the castle, Rhodes searches for more adventures.
He recently returned from a two-week stage in the Finnish wildress, where he soaked inspiration for his next book about Elämänmäki, a former early 20th-century sanatorium that was absorbed by nature.
But it's a strange place for Scotney.
“I was sad when I left,” he said.
“I was like, ‘Now to fucking go back to real life.”
Washingland is changing the terms of granting a green card
The media has released President Donald Trump's words about a new U.S. immigration plan under which there will also be a change in awarding a green card.
The number of legal immigrants is projected to fall by 50 000 year-on-year due to stricter rules on the granting of temporary working visas and restrictions on family visas.
New rules are proposed by Republicans and the president.
The bill will soon be submitted to Congress.
“This law will reduce poverty, increase wages, and save billions of dollars for taxpayers. We will achieve this by changing the rules for issuing green cards to residents of other countries.
The law will limit the number of immigrants and replace the current scheme.
The new application process will prioritize English-speaking applicants who abound with funds and demonstrate capabilities that will benefit our country," Trump said, quoted by the media.
Sexual robots are on the rise - we should be careful
Just when it looked like 2017 couldn't be more bizarre, there'd be a report online about the technological development of sex robots.
Yes. Yes.
Sex robots are really popular right now.
The Foundation for Responsible Robotics (FRR) recently released a statement on the development of sex robots within the next five to ten years.
According to former professor of robotics and artificial intelligence at Sheffield University and FRR co-founder Noel Sharkey, it is necessary to take the rise of sex robots seriously.
Customers can choose the color of the eyes, the shape of the nipples and the appearance of the robot's nails.
"People are teasing about them, but sales are growing, and we have to be prepared to see them more and more often," Sharkey said in a statement.
Modern sexual aids are transformed from an inflatable pan into robotic women with silicone leather, which is pleasantly warm to the touch - of course there is artificial intelligence that mimics the behavior of a person.
The user can "adjust" the robot to his own image - from the color of the eyes and the shape of the nipples to the appearance and color of the nails.
What is very disturbing, however, is the fact that many robots have their own “personality” that would shame Stepford’s mistress.
Modern inflatable dolls have evolved into robotic women with silicone leather.
RealBotix's owner can set different character traits for his surrogate woman, including shyness.
And then there's Roxxxy Gold's sexual robotic woman from True Companion, which comes with pre-programmed personalities, including "frigid Farrah," characterized by shy nature and "wild Wendy" with an "adventurous" nature.
“Roxxxy Gold is set to suit your personality to the maximum extent possible,” reads True Companion’s website.
She likes what you like and vice versa.
She's even moody, just like people.
It can be tired, talkative or “in the mood.”
The rise of sexual robots is alarming for several reasons - among which there is no confusion for real interpersonal relationships. Worrying are rather inferiorary feminine character traits and violent innuendos - e.g. frigid (read defiant) Farrah.
On its website, True Companion likens Roxxxy to a vibrator for men.
“If a woman can have a vibrator, why can’t a man have Roxxxy?” the page reads.
Sexual robots, however, aren't merely a fetish or a sexual aid.
Their emergence and almost perfect sophistication point to something darker and deeply rooted in our culture - an expulsion from ideal or sexual equality towards the desire for captivating sex with an optional mark-up.
Most men, of course, don't buy a robotic woman, and while FRR talks about their growing popularity, sex robots still remain on the margins of interest of the general public.
However, this marginal interest can become a mirror set by contemporary modern times.
And most people aren't weird or violent until they're given an excuse by the free market.
It is no coincidence that these sophisticated sex robots appear at a time when women around the world are winning their rights and in the White House sits the president, who often expresses himself very derogatory to a woman’s address.
The most horrific aspect of the series The Handmaid's Tale is not what we see - hanged bodies and gouged-out eyes, but rather the reality of these visions.
The Book's Discussion represents a step in the right direction, but at the moment it seems to be a small step.
Sexual robots are supposed to resemble real women as much as possible (albeit with the rates of a porn actress).
Their creators design them so that they are pleasant to the touch and act at first glance like a real woman.
However, when it comes to their “personality”, the robot cannot replace a real woman.
They can't break up with their partner or just leave.
They have no work or history, nothing to incite their independence or undermine their loyalty to their master.
In other words, they're pleasant slaves.
Speech of the 21st century: maids.
An NBA star on a green arm.
Curry's battling in California for cut.
Star basketball player Stephen Curry did not get lost, despite a botched first tee at his premier launch among professional golfers.
The two-time NBA Most Valuable Player award winner played the lower Web.com Tour series at the Ellie Mae Classic tournament in Hayward, California, for the first round for 74 shots and will fight for the cut.
The Golden State quarterback, who won his second NBA title with the Warriors this season, started very nervously, and his first tee ended up in a drink holder in a golf cart parked at the fairway.
Even then, he was not as accurate among the holes as under the basketball basket, yet he left dozens of professionals behind with a balance of four shots over par.
"It wasn't an ideal way to start, to have to call the referee right after the first shot, but then it sat down a bit," Curry said, adding that a huge frenzy fell on him.
It was very nervous to hit the first shot at the tournament, but there was everything I was hoping for.
"If you'd tell me I'll play 74 in the first round, I'll take it immediately," he added.
If Curry manages to fight through the cut, which is likely to be set two shots under par, he will become the first representative of another sport who can do it on Web.com Tour.
“I want to play better next time and now that I’ve got rid of the frenzy, I’m going to do it,” said an enthusiastic golfer who has played in celebrity tournaments and mixed competitions for amateurs and professionals in the past.
In Hayward, he starts for the first time at a tournament that falls under the PGA.
To participate in the Ellie Mae Classic, he obtained a sponsorship exemption and retained amateur status.
The Web.com Tour series is one category under the elite PGA Tour.
Trump's great victory?
Against this, Mitt Romney is the embodiment of common sense
An unmistakable feature of the Trump era is the political divisions that divide American voters and, on a micro scale, sever whole families.
In the period of Christmas and Thanksgiving, it was teeming with advice on how to cope with relatives electing Trump.
We're supposedly just waiting for their nerves to allow.
However, less attention is paid to Trump's ability of the family to cement.
I'm talking about it because my cousin visited me in New York this week - a poet and fan of the left who lives in Chicago and who in our family for a long time paid for a Republican fool.
She reminded me that tightness in the face of a common enemy could have a powerful effect.
Now, with Trump in the White House, all of a sudden, the whole family - including all the Republican siblings she's been bickering with about politics for years - is on one wave.
It's a good thing at dinner at the family table; but I'm surprised that, in a broader context, it poses a significant risk.
Trump strengthens the left, but the negative effect of his presidency has the power to push us all to the right.
Speaking of "normalization" and Trump, we refer to the appalling possibility that his clowns may one day lose attractiveness.
However, there is an even more horrific long-term scenario in which Trump “normalizes” right-wing Republicans who, contrary to its standards, will suddenly embody what can be called rational.
In these today with nostalgia in my heart, I remember Mitt Romney and his version of crazy dealings - a dog on the roof of a car, 14% income tax (at least we were aware of his tax return) - of which both examples look like very slight misdemeanors compared to Trump.
After Trump's departure, it's enough for a follow-up Republican nominee to be at least normal, which will be an immense improvement.
We took my cousin's visit, and we took the kids to Central Park for the biggest merry-go-rounder run by New York's park department - you won't find a better use for your three dollars if you don't need caffeine.
The horses are enormous, the central cylinder is covered with fiberglass - clowns and animals are filming and in horror looking at their own shoulders - a completely gloomy look and clinching music oddly reminiscent of public transport.
Many clichés will soon get tired: the view from high-rise buildings, the panorama will peek, instead of breathing the atmosphere on you, and the reality of the city is lacking in your imagination.
Thirty-five years later, I still didn't get over the fact that Swiss Cottage isn't actually a Swiss cheese - not counting Ye Olde Swiss Cottage's thematic pub - but London's subway station.
The merry-go-round in Central Park, however, provokes a remarkably magical experience, to the extent that two two-year-old children, as well as two mature women, have a great time on it.
If I could evaluate the merry-go-round, he deserves maximum points, but fortunately, no one asks.
This week I experienced a similar experience in the store when I was asked to evaluate my “experience” on a scale from one to five when paying by credit card.
It's the same in a cafe on the corner of my street, with the difference that I can throw in a 15% tip for something I buy behind the counter.
Not only is it slowinging by an otherwise fast sale, but the waitress is thus forced to intrude into the favor of shoppers when it's not necessary.
The woman at the store apologised to me with a smile when I reluctantly appreciated my five-star credit card transfer.
Oscar Pistorius was taken from the prison to the hospital because of chest pain
This is the second time Pistorius had to be taken to hospital.
Last year, he was treated because of cuts on his wrist, which, according to the statement of the prison, caused himself after falling in his cell.
Pistorius was convicted of murder following an appeal against the original conviction for manslaughtering.
He killed his girlfriend Steenkamp in the early hours of Valentine's Day 2013 with several gunshot wounds through the door of a toilet closet at his home in Pretoria.
Pistorius claimed to have mistook her for a thief.
The plaintiffs announced their intention of reappeal, this time against Pistorius' six-year sentence, which they said was too mild.
The National Prosecuting Authority said it would appeal to South Africa's Supreme Court and the trial will take place later this year.
If successful, Pistorius faces an extended sentence of up to 15 years.
There is no death penalty in South Africa.
For the first time in history, she gave birth to captive cubs of a bred panda, which was mating in the wild.
It's a huge success for the panda rescue program.
The center is trying to expand the genocide of pandas reared in captivity.
Zhang Zhung told the New China news agency that there was a great risk of kinship interbreeding.
At the end of last year there were only 471 pandas in captivity.
There are only two thousand survivors in the wild.
Trying to mate captivated pandas with the wild ones is the first step in helping to protect this species.
Fifteen-year-old Chao-chao was launched into the nature reserve at the beginning of the mating period on March 11.
Thanks to the tracking device on the neck, scientists were able to see her every move.
She met her chossen one 12 days later.
Sexual intercourse with the panda male lasted a minute and a half and landed successfully.
This, too, can be considered a small miracle, because panda males are not considered the most successful lovers of the animal kingdom.
Males fairly misjudge the time when the female is ready, moreover, they are often confused and have no idea what to do.
The baby was born healthy and, even since Chao-chao had an extraordinary appetite during her pregnancy, weighed 216 grams after giving birth.
The average weight of a newborn panda is 70 grams lower.
Two Bulgarians have been arrested for illegal camping in Greece
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced the detention of two Bulgarian tourists on the territory of Greece for illegal camping, the statement quoted bTV as saying.
A group of tourists, including two Bulgarians, were detained on August 1 in the early hours by Sithonian police.
They have all been released on the same day, but the investigation continues.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs told bTV that Bulgarian tourists were later released.
Greek authorities have tightened the rules of free camping and impose strict fines on anyone caught in a place where camping is not allowed.
Inspections will take place throughout the season.
The ministry said the court could impose a fine.
