Germany, Belgium and the Netherlands are withdrawing millions of toxic eggs from the market
The Netherlands itself has already temporarily closed 180 poultry farms because of the incident.
According to Reuters, NVWA's Dutch food safety authority said it was investigating 20 percent of all Dutch poultry farms, meaning 80 percent of eggs on the market are harmless.
The Authority stressed that only a limited number of egg types could be identified by specific numbers.
According to the German authorities, the amount of toxic substance in eggs is small and does not pose a danger to adults, but it is a potential threat for children.
Petr Majer, a spokesman for the Czech JRC, said that eggs and chicken from the Netherlands and Belgium, where the authorities also proceeded to the closure of farms, have not been imported to the Czech Republic recently.
Veterinarians, however, will take random samples of eggs and chicken originating from Belgium, the Netherlands, France, Italy, Germany and Poland, on the markets of whose problematic insecticide brands have emerged.
According to the Veterinary Administration on the Czech market, eggs from Belgium were the lastest in April and eggs from the Netherlands for the last time in May.
Chicken from Belgium last appeared in the Czech Republic in June.
However, there is a certain amount of chicken from the Netherlands in the Czech Republic.
Fipronil is a substance that tends to be part of flea products, everything and ticks.
However, it must not be served to animals whose meat is consumed by humans.
The World Health Organization considers the preparation to be slightly toxic.
Its high doses lead to a feeling of nausea and dizziness.
