The only question is when and where I will sign.
How real is the possibility that you will start a new year in Zlín?
Anything can happen.
We'll see what happens in a month, where what happens.
Now all I can do is wait to see what the agent arranges.
Do you prefer a contest?
Last season wasn't such for me to be able to choose.
So I'm waiting for the phone.
The government's fling at Let continues.
In January of this year, I wrote, among other things, about the camp in Lety u Orlíku.
The essence of the problem remains the same, so I would like to recall the pivotal passages of the article.
There has been talk of a camp in Lety for many years.
For readers who don’t know exactly what it’s about, it’s probably best if they take a look at, for example, the official website of the cultural monument Pity Place Lety.
At the beginning of the historical overview, they learn that the camp stood two kilometers east of the village of Lety, that from 8 August 1940 it served as a labor camp, from 1 January 1942 it was a collection camp and from 2 August 1942 it became a gypsy camp.
The next text describes the course of life in the camp, including inhumane conditions, until March 1943, when the first airport prisoners were included in Auschwitz transport.
On May 4, 1943, 370 people were transported to the extermination camp.
There were 420 in total.
A very sad history of the immense hardships of human beings, caused by human beings and depraved ideology.
There is no doubt that it is necessary to constantly commemorate these events.
There is neither a doubt that the commemoration should take place at the memorial in the place of experienced suffering of these people.
The problem is elsewhere.
Under socialism, the camp was somewhat forgotten, and in the 70's of the 20th century a pig farm was erected in the vicinity and part of the former camp.
The history of the camp was accidentally discovered in 1992 by American Paul Polansky, a memorial was unveiled in Lety in 1995.
In the mid-1990s, the government promised to buy and demolish a pig farm, but it has not yet.
In 1998 the monument was declared a cultural monument and since 2009 its administration falls under the Lidice Memorial.
In July 2013, the U.N
So muchly facts about the post-war fate of the camp.
It wasn't until last autumn that the government approved the purchase of the pig farm and decided that an expert opinion be drawn up to determine the price.
According to the Minister of Agriculture, the government will then buy the pig farm.
According to Culture Minister Herman, "there are some numbers, but I'd probably thwart those intentions if I went public with them."
We'll see what an expert opinion brings.
According to some unofficial estimates, it could be hundreds of millions, according to others, the price can reach up to a billion.
Either way, it won't be cheap.
And yet just a few hundred meters from the former camp stands a monument, in whose modifications the state invested almost ten million crowns in 2010.
Even if the United Nations Human Rights Committee financed the transaction beautifully, which is probably not in danger, I think it would have been throwing away money anyway.
Doesn't the government have other priorities?
The memorial, a dignified commemorative place, stands in the immediate vicinity of history practically in the immediate vicinity of the tragedy, and anyone who wants, can bow here to the memory of the suffering and murdered.
There's no reason to change this!
Let's leave it as it is, and let's keep the commemorative place with the utmost care.
The entrusted property, i.e. also the money of taxpayers, should be handled by a responsible person, in this case by the Government of the Czech Republic, with the care of a proper manager.
At least with such as in your own company!
Of course, the finance minister and the deputy prime minister is not the whole government.
But even so, from the point of view of the above-mentioned facts, I perceive the slogan of the leader of the ANO movement only as an empty pre-election proclamation.
In May this year, I went directly to see the place.
Very decent driveway, landscaped parking lot, replicas of wooden camp buildings.
In one exposition, in the other integrated toilet with running water.
Small natural amphitheater with the possibility of sitting and with a view of its own commemorative place, which is created from stones located in a gentle slope.
Lots of flowers and wreaths.
All attributes corresponding to the meaning of this place, as it suits and belongs.
Archaeological research is currently taking place on the site of the original camp.
It would be prudent to consider whether it would be possible, for example, to preserve part of the uncovered foundations and at least make it visually available in an appropriate manner.
In this direction, Culture Minister Daniel Herman could strain his efforts.
In the event that he manages to buy a pig farm for state money in the government, it will be just another unfortunate step in his already problematic tenure as Minister of Culture.
Red meat, dairy products and chocolate can alleviate the symptoms of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)
Red meat and dairy products can be a nightmare for your digestive system.
However, a new study suggests just the opposite: a diet rich in animal protein and dairy products could help relieve the symptoms of inflammatory bowel diseases.
An incurable disease that affects up to 1.3 million U.S. residents and 300,000 patients in the U.K. causes abdominal pain and bloody diarrhoea, weight loss and extreme fatigue.
Patients are usually reminded not to use meat and dairy products to alleviate symptoms. However, researchers at the University of Washington have found that the essential amino acid tryptophan contained in the protein helps to create immune cells that promote bowel resilience.
The study's supervisor, Dr. Marco Colonna, explained that immune cells protect the intestines and ensure that harmful microbes from food are broken down.
The number of inflammatory cells is balanced by the number of cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without damaging sensitive tissue.
As soon as the scales tilt to the side of the inflammation, an inflammatory bowel disease can occur.
As part of the research, the researchers found the presence of immune cells supporting the body's tolerance in the stomach of mice with a particular bacterium.
This bacterium requires the presence of tryptophan - one of the building blocks of proteins - to create cells.
Professor Colonna said: "We have found a link between one type of bacteria - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a common part of the gut microbiome and the development of cells that promote tolerance of the body.
The more tryptophan the mice had in their diet, the more they had these immune cells."
At the same time, he suggested that if it turns out that the same is true for humans, then the combination of L. reuteri bacteria and tryptophan-rich diets would help develop a more tolerable and less inflammatory intestinal environment.
Postgraduate researcher Dr. Luisa Cervantes-Barraganwas, as part of a study of a certain kind of immune cells supporting body tolerance, discovered that one group of test mice had these cells, while the other group of the same mice reared separately lacked cells.
She inferred that one group of mice has a different gut microbiome - a communion of bacteria, viruses and fungi that normally reside in the gastrointestinal tract.
Mice's intestines were tested by the DNA sorting method, and researchers found six species of bacteria in mice with immune cells lacked by a second group of mice.
Mice that were bred in a sterile environment and lacked an intestinal microbiome - did not develop this kind of immune cells - were given by L. reuteri bacteria - and formed immune cells.
In order to understand how bacteria affects the immune system, the researchers developed L. reuteri in a fluid and then transferred a small amount of fluid - without bacteria - to undeveloped immune cells that had been stored in isolation.
Immune cells evolved into tolerant cells.
After removing the active ingredient from the liquid, it turned out to be a by-product of tryptophanic metabolism known as indolyl-3-acetic acid.
When the researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in mice's diet, the amount of these cells increased by 50 percent. At the same time, the number of cells fell by halved.
Dr Cervantes-Barragan explained that humans have the same tolerant cells as mice, and most of us keep L. reuteri bacteria in their gastrointestinal tract.
It is not known whether the by-products of tryptophane from L. reuteri cause the development of cells in humans in the same way as in mice, but defects in tryptophan-related genes have been detected in people with inflammatory bowel disease.
Dr Cervantes-Barragan added: “Each of us should probably try to develop these cells because they control the inflammatoryness of the inner surface of the intestines.
A potentially high level of tryptophan in the presence of L. reuteri can increase the cell population.”
Jose Mourinho considers the signing of a contract with Nemanja Matić and his transfer to Manchester United as 'genius'
Manchester United have acquired a "genius" into their team in the form of Nemanja Matić - at least Jose Mourinho thinks so after the midfielder made his debut in Wednesday's match against Sampdoria.
Matić, who signed a 40-million-pound contract, claimed a 2:1 win in Dublin in the first match - the last pre-season match before the UEFA Super Cup, where United will meet Real Madrid on Tuesday - and have impressed in their role as midfielders alongside Paul Pogba and Andreas Pereira.
However, because he did not participate in the pre-seasonal Asian tour, Matić did not have form and played only 45 minutes, according to Mourinho it is necessary to work before the beginning of the next Premier League season.
"He needs time, but he's experienced, he's intelligent and he's got an ingenious mind - he's thinking football," Mourinho said.
"We'll help him get in shape as soon as possible."
Like Matić, his new team-mates Victor Lindelöf and Romelu Lukaku have also played their first match at Aviva Stadium, and Mourinho is slowly starting to shape his eleven for the next season.
An independent source announced this week that Mourinho still wants to sign with three other players but insists the club has done a "fantastic job" and brought three of the four most important players into the squad.
"I thank the club for helping me get three out of four players," he added.
“Four out of four would please me more, but I admit they did their best for me.”
The life challenge of Láda Hruška, the king of chicken cuticles.
He wants a lesson from Pohlreich!
The multipromising picture began to loom thanks to a new show on TV Prima Zdeňka Academy.
Pear immediately smelled the chance.
"Dear Zdeněk, I'd like to sign up for your cooking academy, because I think I can learn a lot from you," he said in a social media video.
And because the two of them have been peaking for several years, Láďa didn't forget to irritate him even this time.
"In return, I can teach you Znojmo from dietary chicken, American cucumbers, or invite you to the water and show you why fried cheese is your favorite," Pear said.
He's really serious about participating in Pohlreich's new show.
It's time to move up the level and learn from the master.
"The question is, how about the boss," Lightning said.
Pohlreich, however, did not comment, because he was filming new episodes of his next show Yes, Boss!.
Only Prima reacted to Pear's participation in the culinary academy, saying that he takes the matter as his personal activity, which goes outside television.
Hruška dealt a big blow to Pohlreich in December 2004 with his first cook.
Within a few days it sold more than 300 thousand pieces!
The new show, presented yesterday by Prima at a press conference on the autumn broadcast scheme, is a genuine cooking school.
Pohlreich chooses his pupils from among the audience and introduces them to twelve cooking techniques in twelve works.
In the last episode of his show Now the Boss in November 2014, Zdeněk had a crossed-out pear on the back of his T-shirt.
If he had my face, I'd have laughed a lot.
"I'll just smile at the pear - pear is a healthy fruit and has a lot of vitamins," Láďa responded.
He thinks of Láda's culinary art.
"Cooking according to Láda Hruška is not an option even at home, because I will not take home gastropunk," the boss said.
I respect Zdeněk Pohlreich very much, I even, according to him, make chicken for honey.
On my show, however, his recipes will definitely not appear," Hruška did not forgive himself.
He's an asshole!
He's no chef to me, he's a TV clown.
"Gastronomic illiterate or asshole," Pohlreich said of Pear at a lecture.
"I'm not twenty to be able to wave my hand over them, but I don't deal with them anymore, too," Hruška said.
A British inventor uses a microchip in his hand to unlock a car and a house
The British inventor became the country's first man with a microchip injected into his body. With a simple wave of his hand, he can unlock the door from the house and office and even start the car.
Thirty-three-year-old Steven Northam has a small chip implanted between his thumb and index finger and is currently offering the same service through his BioTeq company to companies and individuals who want to make their lives easier with technology.
Dr. Geoff Watson, anaesthesiologist at Royal Hampshire County Hospital in Winchester, supervises the implantation procedure and adherence to treatments.
The technology works on the same principle as microchips implanted by dogs and cats, and the whole procedure lasts 30 seconds.
Northama’s company also offers a door and car lock replacement service, compatible with a microchip. The price of one lock is £300.
The married father of one child had the technology integrated into his home and his BMW Z4 car, which he starts with a simple waving of his hand.
The White House prop cost taxpayers thousands of dollars
The White House's decision to arrive with a navy helicopter at South Lawn to celebrate the U.S. manufacturing industry held last month came at £24,000, according to Navy records, issued by the TIM.
The white Sikorsky VH-3D helicopter, also called Marine One in the presence of the president on board, was the highlight of the July 17 program at the White House, which was a parade of U.S. design programs.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence and several senior White House officials surveyed the product show from all 50 states.
The Connecticut helicopter was on display next to a yacht from Maine, a Wisconsin firefighting car, and a fork-shaped forklift from Mississippi.
A White House military officer issued a fragmentary order, or FRAGO, and applied for the presence of a helicopter less than 36 hours prior to the helicopter's landing on South Lawn as part of an "extraordinary" event, Marine Helicopter Squadron One (HMX-1) - the unit responsible for the helicopter's management - said.
“I wanted to make sure that you were aware of such a significant event,” the HMX-1 commander wrote in an e-mail to his supervisor, Deputy Commander of the Navy for Aviation, the day before the event began.
The report indicated that the helicopter will arrive at South Lawn on July 17 at approximately 7:00 p.m. on the occasion of the parade, the beginning of which is scheduled for 15:00 and will remain in place until the entire site is completely cleared, which was planned between 18:00 and 21:00.
The e-mail further said that if there is a delay on departure from the White House, a replacement crew will be dispatched for the return flight due to working-hours regulations.
The original crew remained in place that evening along with the helicopter, and along with them a unit of security personnel.
It was not apparent from the records - obtained by the weekly TIME - if the second crew was eventually dispatched.
Hatch Act prevents civil servants from using government property for political purposes.
Records indicate that HMX-1 is investigating possible violations of this Regulation.
"We were wondering if it was a violation of the Hatch Act and we were assured that everything was fine," the commander wrote.
"It sounds like you've been blocked, "the deputy commander wrote back.
This "Made in America" parade has been designated by the White House as an official event, and the Hatch Act wouldn't apply.
But even official events have political subtexts.
In participating in the event, the president championed a health reform that went through the Senate and talked about trying to keep government regulations at bay.
The use of aerial vehicles such as Marine One or Air Force One for political purposes is hardly a new phenomenon.
The presidents use them when they travel.
However, their presence purely due to presentation reasons is unusual.
Trump ordered control in February after the presidential plane drove in the background at his first rally as part of a campaign in Melbourne, Florida. His Twitter account is overwhelmed by complaints about the use of the president's plane by his predecessor.
“The White House is delighted with the ‘Made in America’ parade, which has introduced products from all 50 states produced by Americans from American materials,” said Lindsay Walters, deputy press secretary.
HMX-1 helicopter crews often traine landings in the Washington and South Lawn areas, rehearsing possible arrivals with the President of the United States to the White House with maximum accuracy.
While Trump participated in a G-20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 made 58 training landings at the White House.
Flight at the Made in America parade was part of the crew's current training, a state official said.
On the same day, the helicopter flew for another eleven hours as part of "non-presidential support missions."
The White House said no other props had been reimbursed from taxpayers' money.
Sikorsky's spokesman said the company was in no way interested in negotiations to issue a helicopter at South Lawn.
HMX-1 does not keep budget records for presidential flights, and the exact costs in connection with the parade are difficult to determine, according to the navy's statement.
According to the Ministry of Defence, the hourly price for the fiscal year 2017 is DoD's expense for VH-3D $24 380 per flight hour.
The unit's records indicate a time of 30 minutes from Marine Corps Air Facility Quantico in Virginia and back.
The White House argues that the actual operating costs of the helicopter are well below the stated amount.
"Introducing taxpayers' costs based on estimates for reimbursement of expenses is very misleading," Walters said.
“These estimations include personnel, maintenance, and many other hidden costs that are included in annual costs.”
According to a 2015 RAND study, DoD's reimbursement of expenses does not include personnel costs, but includes fuel price, maintenance and contractual costs.
Then White House press secretary Sean Spicer defended the use of a helicopter for parade purposes.
"The idea of the parade that's taking place this week is to introduce products produced in the U.S.," Spicer said.
“I am aware that Sikorsky and the State of Connecticut are very proud of the fact that they contribute to the defense of our state, and that they provide work to hundreds, if not thousands, of people.
I think, like most Americans, that we are proud of the helicopter and the other military installations in which countless U.S. citizens are involved.
It is worth remembering this fact.”
It's unclear if Trump, during his trip to South Lawn, was also to look at the helicopter. It's certainly only possible to sat behind the wheel of one of the fire trucks.
Back at the White House, Trump reached out to Sikorsky's representative and joked with the media about his own fleet of products.
"I know Sikorsky very well," the president said, “I own three.”
A young woman got stuck in a swamp near Mácha's lake, the police waded towards her.
The girl set off for the beach around two o'clock in the morning.
Instead of bathing, however, she remained trapped in wetlands.
When she decided to ask a mobile phone for the help of a police officer, she had the last 12 percent battery.
"For this reason, the officers soon lost contact with her," police spokeswoman Ivana Baláková said.
Several police patrols went to the site, but it wasn't easy to find a young woman.
"Some of the police officers had to wade through reed wetlands hundreds of meters before they felt solid ground under their feet again," Baláková said.
She was similarly missing.
Officers oriented themselves according to the direction of her call for help.
"In order not to lose at least this connection, they have agreed with her that she will count out loud," the spokeswoman said.
Officers found the woman at three-quarters to five in swamps in the Borný locality.
She was on the verge of physical and mental strength, showing signs of hypothermia.
That's whay the summoned ambulance transported her to a Czech hospital.
Grandpa's connection with STAN?
The coveted bride becomes a selling girl.
That's a bit ironic.
With his web project lookdameslusny.cz, the billionaire basically tells voters that STAN suffers from a deficit of decent people.
And he also confirmed that a politically disparate bunch of lesser-known mayors and independents actually has no national agenda for House elections.
Her electoral candidate simply becomes a decent person after an interview with Grandfather, no matter what political opinion she has.
That's how Vít Bárta used to start like this, and it didn't turn out very well.
With his hunt for decent people and personalities, who will eventually replace some mayors on the candidates, which Gazdík acknowledged, in addition, Dědek cancels the popular argument of the head of STAN that the biggest value of the movement is politically proven and experienced mayors, traditionally the most popular politicians even according to surveys.
They can now be replaced by hastily collected unfamiliary personalities.
Gazdík also explained that the movement does not want to lose its identity on the candidates of the people.
However, if only a few people suspected STAN of identity at that time, after the arrival of Dědek, the mere game of identity also ends.
Not only Gazdík has long opposed the ANO of Andrej Babiš, but at the first joint press, Dědek, who publicly praises Babiš, said: "My goal is that the ANO movement, i.e. the Mayors movement, will gain twenty percent in the upcoming elections."
Let's hope he didn't mistake the movement.
Grandfather is a successful entrepreneur, and the billionaire is certainly no coincidence.
