STAN has been reminiscent of a reality show for some time, rather than a real political party, and Dědek's helping hand seems to make the coveted bride even more into a selling girl.
For some time STAN went with TOP 09, then with the people and now he's looking for decent people for a change.
Let's hope someone calls in.
Police dogs trained in Most by helicopter
Dogs specializing in the search for explosives and search dogs rehearsed helicopters at Lake Most today.
The police dog must not be timid and afraid of flying, Petr Tomášek, the head of police cynologists of the Ústí region, said.
The training of a police dog lasts about two years.
At Most they practiced both adult trained dogs and those who are just going through the training.
In addition to standard training, during which they were supposed to detect sharp samples of explosives around the lake and look for the lost figurant, all 12 animals had to board a helicopter and fly over the lake.
The main purpose of the exercise is to test the dogs in the helicopter.
So that as soon as the helicopter lands, the dog can go to work so that he does not have a problem with the helicopter journey.
"A helicopter lands, the dog goes and searches immediately," Tommy said.
A dog that is restless or unable to start working right away by helicopter is eliminated from training.
"Mostly they are then kept by dog handlers, for example, in the garden," Tomášek added.
The police have 76 dogs with various specializations in the Ústecký region.
German shepherds predominate, but there are also more Belgian shepherds.
The handler takes care of it from the puppy.
"He raises it himself, he prepares it for a service that the dog will do for us," Tommy added.
The animal undergoes repeated veterinary examinations, which can reveal possible medical disabilities.
If the dog is healthy and non-poignant, it composes the first tests in 20 months
"He's been getting into a sharp intervention around two and a half to three years," Tommy added.
The length of service with the police depends on the health of the dog.
They usually serve within eight to ten years.
Then they usually live with their dog handlers.
Trailer for Season 3 Narcos: Pablo Escobar is out of the game and Cali Cartel becomes the centerpiece of Netflix's hit series
Despite the conclusion of Narcos' second season, when Pablo Escobar's killer is uncovered, Netflix's series is far from over.
Confirmation on the part of the streaming service has already reached the third and fourth seasons - the former has already received the first trailer and an outline of other negative characters.
In the third season, the DEA focuses on the world's richest drug cartel: Cali Cartel.
Under the leadership of four influential godfathers, they operate "like a Fortune 500 company," with the only difference being that they escalate violent acts and bribe more government officials.
Gilberto Rodriguez Orejuela (Damian Alcazar) is the leader of the cartel, Miguel Rodriguez Orejuela (Francisco Denis) plays the brain of operations, Pacho Herrera (Alberto Ammann) is in charge of connection with Mexico, and Chepe Santacruz Londono (Pepe Rapazote) controls New York.
In the new trailer, Pedro Pascal returns as Javier Peña, a character recruiting U.S. and Columbian law enforcers in the fight against drugs.
Other actors include Matias Varela, Michael Stahl-David, Matt Whelan, Miguel Angel Silvestre, Kerry Bishé and Arturo Castro.
Narcos's third season will make its premiere on September 1.
According to the United Nations, Afghan democracy relies on women in politics
Elections can only be credible if all members of society, including women, take part in them, the U.N. representative in Afghanistan said on Wednesday.
Nicholas Haysom, a senior U.N. auxiliary mission official, said hundreds of women are taking part in local council elections, three women are applying for the post of vice president, and more than 30 percent of newly registered voters are women.
"The role of women cannot be overlooked," he said in his statement on Wednesday.
Elections can only be fully representative and credible if women take part, as well as all related processes related to elections.
Local and presidential elections in Afghanistan are held on April 5.
The Taliban have posted several threats against the electoral process, which is moving international combat activity to the other track.
In this year's elections, for the first time ever, one democratically elected government hands over its power peacefully to the following government.
Haysom stated that the strong role of women will strengthen Afghanistan's democracy.
Afghanistan's independent electoral commission said that out of a total of £3.6-million registered voters make up 1.2-million women.
A unique brewery brews beer again.
For a good beer to a resurrected brewery.
Enthusiasm and honesty of the new owners returned to life a few years ago the brewery in Lobči u Mšena.
The brewery, commemorated for the first time already in 1586, has a Renaissance core and in the nineteenth century it was rebuilt into a vapor engine operation.
It was followed by years of flourishing, when the brewery was famous mainly for its dark beers.
During the Second World War, unfortunately, production was halted and in 1984 the brewery was nationalized.
Since then, buildings have served a state estate.
The work of destruction was completed by the owners of the brewery after 1989.
The abandoned area was devastated and dismantled into building material.
In 2007, however, a new hope shone in the form of new owners, architects Jana and Pavel Prouz.
They began to repair the buildings with the utmost respect for the spirit of the building and every detail.
They are carefully engaged in the study of archival materials, trying to preserve the original materials and building elements.
The brewery has the same form as in the nineteenth century, when its golden era began.
Their work was appreciated by the brewery’s declaration as a cultural monument by the Ministry of Culture, and the construction also became part of the European Industrial Heritage Trails.
In 2015, they ceremonially renewed the tradition of brewing beer.
If you want to taste a great Lobeč beer in the picturesque landscape of Kokořínsko, visit the brewery.
When brewing beer, they use Kokořín water sourced in a protected landscape area, malt from Lobeč fields and Žatecký hops.
In the bar you will taste traditional light lager, but also dark beers and specials.
And don’t miss a tour of the brewery, it takes place on Saturday and Sunday at noon and at half past two.
The largest artillery fortress of Czechoslovakia?
One of the largest fortresses of its type on a global scale?
Such a unique building was supposed to be the Stachelberg fortress between Trutnov and Žacléř, at the top of the off-road ridge above the village of Babí.
Originally, it was supposed to consist of up to twelve bunkers connected by an extensive underground.
There was supposed to be headquarters, barracks, ammunition depots, material, power plant, engine room and hospital.
However, the Munich Agreement ended construction.
In less than a year he managed to build a single massive reinforced concrete bunker and underground spaces were broken.
Thanks to enthusiasts from the Stachelberg Association, who began to reconstruct the unique building in the early 1990s, you can also look here.
The underground is 75 percent available!
In addition to the T-S 73 infantry cabin with exposition and underground, there is also an armed light fortification building, as well as two built-up infantry log cabins, trenches and an educational trail Fortified Trutnov region.
Refreshments, cosy gazebo and breathtaking views of the countryside guaranteed!
This unique and beautiful Czech building has made it onto the list in addition to monuments such as the Great Wall of China, Traian’s Port of Rome or the Assyrian Palace in Nineve and Nimrud.
Originally from the beginning of the thirteenth century, Chotěšov Monastery, which today consists mainly of a massive four-wing convent building above a beautiful garden.
The list he got to, but unfortunately belongs to the unique buildings that are at risk of extinction.
Enthusiasts don’t want to give up and organize a lot of events in support of the monastery.
One of them is the next edition of The Evening for the Chotěšov Monastery this Saturday the 5th of August, where Vladimír Mišík, Ivan Hlas will perform, you will see fencing performances and look at these places of a beautiful large building, which are usually inaccessible.
But most of all, you will enjoy the atmosphere of this great place.
Do you want to look Neanderthals in the face?
The exhibition Welcome to the Neanderthals in the pavilion of the Anthropos of the Moravian Museum in Brno attracts an unusual excursion to the deepest prehistory to the Stone Age, to the world of our predecessors.
You will find out how Neanderthals were able to cope perfectly with various extreme climatic conditions, especially with the alternation of icy and interglacial times.
Despite their simple material culture, they were extremely successful predators of their time.
You’ll also learn what they looked like, what their anatomy, technological skills and hunting skills were, but also their aesthetic sensibilities.
That you wouldn't say it to Neanderthals?
Let yourself be persuaded in Brno.
The fourteenth KELT & WORLD MUSIC festival takes place on Saturday the fifth at Veveří Castle.
In addition to great music, you will see the construction of a Celtic village, you will try the Celtic way of life including crafts, games for children and adults, divination, magic or ritual body painting.
There won't be La Quadrilla with Irish and Scotch dances, drumming jamming, dude play and an amazing fiery surprise performed by Carpe Noct.
How does Moravian-Silesia taste?
The seventh year of the well-known gastrofestival How the Moravian-Silesian smucks takes place on Saturday, August 5, 2017 in the area of the mountain spa Karlova Studánka.
Chef Miroslav Kalina, you will taste culinary specialties and regional food, you will enjoy musical and dance performances, a rich program for children such as creative workshops, theatre, bouncy castle, Smurf competition and, of course, a number of competitions.
Get to know the plant with one click.
The new free application Pl@ntNet will help students with the creation of herbariums and ordinary plant lovers.
If they wonder what kind of plant they just came across on a walk, this application will advise them.
All they will have to do is photograph the plant, and the application itself recognizes what species it is.
The new application, which is available for both iOS and Android, was unveiled on Tuesday by representatives of the CEEV Ethical and Environmental Education Centre in Bratislava’s Mountain Park as part of the City’s Secret Life project.
The project also involved pupils from 50 Czech and Slovak schools, which with teachers photographed and added plants from their surroundings to the application database.
The newly established French app is free to download on the Secret Life of the City website.
The main goal of French programmers was for as many people as possible to contribute to the application with their images, thereby increasing the number of plants that the application can recognize.
"Children who hold a tablet or a mobile phone in their hands can go out into the forest," said Ivana Poláčková, coordinator of the project.
Based on a sufficiently high-quality photo of the flower, leaf or fruit of the plant, the application can recognize its name in a matter of seconds, which it then assigns under the photograph in both Czech and Slovak.
The French have allowed us to translate the application into Slovak and Czech into the Secret Life of the City project.
It is therefore welcome for users to contribute a picture of a plant that does not yet appear in the database after creating an account into the application.
"French will evaluate whether it's beneficial, and then add it to the database," Poláčková said.
GMB's concerns about investing in McVitie's factory in Glasgow
The unions are demanding assurances from McVitie's biscuit maker's executives about a long-time production commitment at a factory in Glasgow.
The GMB union said the company was silent for the time being about a possible investment in a Tollcross factory.
There are more than 500 employees working at the factory, who also produce Hobnob and Rich Tea biscuits, among other products.
McVitie's owner, Pladis, said it had invested more than 5 million pounds across the area within several previous years.
She added that employees and representatives of the unions will be "among the first informed" in the event of changes in the operation of the factory.
In a letter to the company's construction manager, GMB Scottish unions expressed their concerns that McVitie's "will not deliver on its promises of financing and upgrading production lines."
GMB's chief organiser Drew Duffy added: "The fact that we have not received a clear response from the construction manager regarding the company's needs and the future investment plan in Tollcross is very disturbing for our members.
Generations of many families have worked here, and employees mostly come from the local community and the Glasgow area, where work in the local factory represents a critical pillar of decent employment in an area of high unemployment and social separation.
In terms of work, salaries and benefits - and despite the outlook for Brexit, rising living costs and declining slow production - the eastern part of Glasgow needs a desperately prosperous producer like McVitie's."
A spokeswoman for Pladis said: "Tollcross has only received a letter from GMB yesterday and the company will issue a statement as soon as we appreciate the continuing commitment to employees and union representatives.
A regular meeting with GMB employees and GMB representatives in Tollcross was held two months ago, and many issues were openly discussed at this meeting.
We spoke to the employees that, as in all companies operating in extremely competitive and precarious economic conditions, we evaluate our operations on a regular basis, which applies to all our operations so that we can continue to successfully compete with others.
“We have invested 5 million pounds in Tollcross in recent years, especially in production lines and streamlining operations.
We greatly appreciate the heritage of Tollcross and the locality here, and in case of any changes in the operation of the local factory, the employees and representatives of the unions will be informed immediately."
Did PSG warn Barcelona about Neymar's move three years ago?
La Liga may regret its interest in Marco Verratti
Paris Saint-Germain will not allow, in its quest to get Neymar to his side, after composing £189 million, the amount needed to redeem Neymar from his contract, but did this French team have warned Barcelona about their record-breaking raid three years ago?
This sensational transfer has stirred up the waters of summer transfers. The Ligue 1 club is ready to surpass more than twice the previous record amount for Paulo Pogba's transfer to Manchester United, who paid £89 million for him last season.
After confirming and completing the transfer, Neymar will become the most expensive player in the world and at the same time the highest-paid professional athlete in the world - after tax, he will receive over £50,000 a week from the PSG team.
Barcelona are outraged by the way Neymar has come out of his contract, despite a confidential source confirming that the club has always tried to accommodate Neymar in everything since his arrival from Santos in 2013.
But has the football world been alerted to this fact three years ago by PSG's president?
When Manchester City expressed their interest in Blais Matuidi in 2014, Nasser Al-Khelaifi warned all the club that would be interested in defending one of PSG's best players.
"I'm not afraid because once someone tries to get one of our players, then we're definitely going to reach out to one of their players," Al-Khelaifi said.
“We respect all clubs.
But if they're trying to get one of our players, they'll face consequences."
Barcelona appear to have crossed an imaginary line when they expressed interest in PSG midfielder Marco Verratti in the summer.
PSG have made it clear in the sense that under no circumstances will it allow the Italian representative to leave. It is therefore possible that Barcelona is now swallowing a bitter pill with Neymar's departure for his presumptuous negotiations.
Who will explain the sanctions?
Does political punishment have widely understood rules?
Donald Trump nodded to anti-Russian sanctions.
The day before, the U.S
And even a day earlier, Trump wrote that China is doing nothing with the North Korean threat and that it will no longer be possible.
This, too, has the idea of sanctions like a straw from shoes.
You can't throw those things in the same bag.
Maduro's Venezuela has indeed embarked on a path to dictatorship, even totalitarianism.
China is not just behaving in the way that the US president would like.
Russia is a chapter for itself.
But if someone wanted to summarize all of this in such a way that even an ordinary voter, American, European or otherwise, would understand what it consists in and on what system of rules the policy of sanctions relies, it would be a task almost impossible.
In sanctions against Russia, President Trump is merely tightening up what President Obama has already prepared, only giving it an implementation stamp.
So, why such a fuss?
Last year, Europe feared that if Trump wins the U.S. election, hugs Putin and withdraws sanctions.
Terrible idea.
But in practice, it's the other way around.
Sanctions aren't being released with Trump, but they're tightening.
But Europe is even more afraid.
German Secretary Zypries says Trump's sanctions violate international law.
It's like kosher is just sanctions approved by the U.N. Security Council?
There are no kosher or anti-Russian EU sanctions.
European Commission chief Juncker has complained that Trump's sanctions threaten the EU's energy security.
It's likely that sanctions should only be used weak enough not to endanger anyone?
Ordinary voters grope.
For three years he heard that the fate of anti-Russian sanctions depends only on the fulfillment of the Minsk ceasefire agreements in Ukraine.
Now he learns it's about the EU's energy security.
Voter gropes even more broadly.
Sanctions are coming to Russia for getting too much where it shouldn't.
China is slim for having little assembly.
Venezuela's president is being punished for building a dictatorship within his country's borders.
All these punishments, or just considerations, have their rationality.
But does anyone see it as a system, order or rules?
Andrej Babiš: I'm not thinking about working with Okamura.
Andrej Babiš got married, criscrossing the republic with his visionary book and finalizing the electoral programme.
In addition to the Pardubice region, he already has all the candidates ready.
According to him, he did not significantly interfere with them.
He even agreed to lead the Moravian-Silesian governor and the shadow minister of education ANO Ivo Vondrák.
"But if he succeeds, he will have to choose whether he wants to be an MP or a governor," Babiš confirmed.
You have annoyed a lot of representatives and national politicians that directly elected mayors and mayors should run municipalities and towns themselves, only with managers.
Are you serious?
It wasn't like that.
I am a supporter of direct democracy, we elect a president directly, and I am convinced that we should elect mayors and mayors anyway.
I'm not talking in my book about the cancellation of councillors, they might as well be there.
The question is how many there will be.
At the presentation of your book What I dream of when I accidentally sleep, I heared with my own ears that you were talking about abolishing councils.
I don't remember that.
I was talking about a direct election and a reduction in the number of representatives.
New York City, with a population of nine million, has five districts and 51 representatives.
Prague, with 1.2 million citizens, has 1171 representatives.
And the mayor is reproached that she builds little and has not prepared big projects, but she does not have the competence for it.
The same is true of a government run by only two people in our history, Klaus and Zeman.
That's not to say they did it right.
But the current Prime Minister doesn't control anything, he's just moderating the debate.
It can't work that way.
That's why you want to enforce a majority system?
He'd be ideal, but it's hard to enforce.
There's only a change to a one-round Senate election in the program.
We have also proposed a sliding mandate of ministers and an amendment to the Rules of Procedure of the House in order to prevent unnecessary exhibitions of MPs, who are supposed to work mainly in commissions and in committees, not to be demonstrated at meetings of the House.
It's certainly not true that I want to destroy the parliamentary system.
That’s exactly what your opponents are worried about.
That, as a dictator, you will run the government, control the House with 101 deputies, abolish the counties and the Senate, and, through grateful mayors and mayors, control the entire country.
That's certainly not the case.
There's nothing wrong with debating the number of MPs.
French President Macron announced the reduction of the legislature by a third, while there are 57,000 French per legislator, while in our country it comes to 17,000 Czechs.
However, no one accuses Macron of endangering democracy.
Even if in the Czech Republic someone wins elections by a majority, we have mechanisms that would prevent them from making any dramatic changes.
However, seductions for those who have a parliamentary majority are great, you just have to look at Poland or Hungary.
There is no such thing in the case of the Yes movement.
We just need a team that will work for the people in the government, not for its parties or resorts.
I don't understand, for example, why we couldn't agree that only the salaries of teachers, employees in culture and social services would increase.
Why did they overrule me and add 21 percent to GIBS members who are already taking 60,000?
As prime minister, would you step on it?
The prime minister is supposed to intervene when ministers are behaving uneconomically, confusing the government with a travel agency and, with the state's money, would like to do business like Marian Jurečka, who wanted to buy forests in Romania and land in Africa.
Ministers are not supposed to argue in government, contradictions are supposed to settle outside it.
There must be a ready-made material in the cabinet, there is no room for coalition rifts, force votes and deals, where it is supposed to be decided.
How do you want to have a cohesive team when ministers behave mainly partisanly?
There is a constitutional law above the parties.
When it came to breaking bread, Sobotka said that the Coalition treaty does not apply, and kicked Babiš out of the government.
Any prime minister can do that.
But if she's a personality and she has management skills, she doesn't need similar vain gestures.
It measures everyone equally and requires perfect performance from everyone.
Do you already know who you're going to propose to the finance minister?
I don't think about it at all, we probably won't be in that government, nobody wants to cooperate with us, although I don't know what I've done.
There's not so much skepticism.
The mayors got the entrepreneur Dědek, who would like to cooperate with you with you, the people's party may win the wing of your inclined Jiří Čunek.
I don't think so.
The ČSSD is led by Lubomír Zaorálek, who does not exclude a coalition with the ANO, in the ODS, Václav Klaus Jr., who will also not oppose it.
I'd leave it to the voters.
The deputy prime minister and vice-chairman of ANO, Richard Brabec, said that you have many points in common with SPD Tomio Okamura, for example, in the field of the economy.
So the Okamurovs come into consideration as a coalition partner?
Mr. Brabec says he didn't say it like that.
Mr. Okamura has his political history, he run as a puppet of Vít Bárta for VV, then he got money from Dawn for his company, there was a coup.
He's, of course, a distinctive personality.
But somewhat brownish.
Taking his agenda, he has a lot of people's involvement in the management of society, direct democracy, that's something to agree with.
That's not what I'm asking you.
I'm interested to know if it's an acceptable person who has xenophobic views, speaking at demonstrations with gallows and wanting to leave the EU?
We're not considering working with Mr. Okamura's party, I can't imagine.
It's obvious that he went into politics to profit from it.
And what about the silent support of the communists to your minority government?
I can't even imagine that, but the voters decide.
It's going to be about negotiating the winner with the second in the standings, but God knows who's going to be the winner and who's going to be number two
Of course, it's important that they're able to agree on a coalition government's agenda.
Let's get back to your quest to run the state like a company.
But the government just has to govern, Prime Minister Sobotka lets everything rot, he never decided.
Coalition affairs such as ČEB or EGAP didn't work.
After all, Sobotka is his puppet, and I think that's the reason he fired me.
As soon as he did, CEZ controlled the Czech government.
It is unbelievable, after all, that the Prime Minister brought a resolution written by the management of CEZ, which obliges the Minister of Finance to vote at the CEZ General Meeting.
If you become prime minister, will you change the personnel conditions in the Supervisory Board and replace CEZ top management?
Jesus, if I'm prime minister, we'll see.
I'm not one yet.
It is rumored that CEZ has already sent signals in your direction.
He would like to exchange good cooperation with ČSSD for even better with YES.
Maybe after the election, everything will move on again.
I don't know anything about it.
I don't have any lobbyist Pokorný or Růžička, I'm a separate unit and I don't deal with this at all.
Then respond directly.
If you are at the head of the government, will you seek to replace the management of CEZ, Czech Television or the General Health Insurance Company, i.e. the institutions you criticize?
I would like CEZ to be a transparent company.
So far, he's been doing whatever he wanted for years, thanks to commissions for traditional political parties.
All you have to do is look at Mr Schwarzenberg's remarks.
Although the state has a majority in ČEZ, he has never driven it, it has only been there in numbers, which is wrong.
It's a trivial thing for me.
ČEZ is supposed to take Dukovany, appreciate them, put them in a subsidiary company, arrange financing, do a supply competition or find a co-investor and build a new nuclear unit.
The concept that the state is supposed to finance and build new nuclear blocs is absurd.
And Czech Television?
Some of her shows are running a permanent purpose campaign against me, but I don't and I won't have any competence to fire anyone.
But controlling yes.
I don't want to control anyone.
I'm just saying that citizens pay about 7.2 billions a year for public radio and television, but they don't really know how these institutions manage.
How come they don't know?
After all, the Chamber approves the report on their management, which has a direct influence on the positions of directors-general of ČT and ČRo.
Look how the reports are discussed, for the super power pact Sobotka Kalousek, which has always worked.
This applies both to the current parliamentary commission for leaks from investigative files and by lex Babiš.
And Sobotka was so stupid that Kalousek got pushed to throw me out of the government, which passed a percentage of TOP 09, but ČSSD took them away.
They are afraid of disrupting their corruption system, so they are talking about the threat to democracy by Babiš.
No one's immune.
What do you say to the articles describing how the First Vice-Chairman of ANO, Jaroslav Faltýnek, builds highstory facilities by installing his son in the Supervisory Board of Teplárny Brno and arranging contracts with municipal and state-owned companies such as the Prague Transport Company or Czech Railways?
That's where we've got an ongoing dispute
I don't like it, and I've blamed him several times.
Faltýnek argues that the aforementioned lawyer works at substantially lower rates and is reliable.
I wouldn't proceed that way.
As finance minister, I didn't have anyone.
We, unlike socans, have never had a corruption affair, no Pelta, Kratochvílová, Březina.
If there was any indication of clientelism with us, we immediately fired such a person.
In this regard, I am uncompromising.
